      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>AVOID INDIGESTION Mon tr ? BY USING</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>TO GO vv.,. GREEN THE SPOT SHOP</head>
        <p>TO GO vv.,. GREEN THE SPOT SHOP</p>
        <p>FOE Z li-JEI;LYTEEINGr</p>
        <p>wore alcoholic drinl s. end if Le wanted to get equivalent for hard earned cash he must not go to a public-house for it. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS AND ITS EVIL TENDENCIES. Mr. COYNE said the wheel of progress had been scotched by the intemperate habits of the people, and they were out to remove the obstacles which stood in the way of progress. The sooner workingmen realised that intemperance caused a great deal of the poverty, destitution and darkness in their homes, the sooner would the drink evil be removed. They should make a determined stand and say, "We will not have the drink which wrecks and ruins our homes." Drink was responsible for most of the murders in the country, and many other crimes could be traced to drunkenness. Wives were beaten, homes ruined and children were neglected, because strong drink was taken in excess by the father.</p>
        <p>After the CHAIRMAN had referred to the Corporation providing more bowling greens, Mr. B. T. PARKES also addressed the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>PANTRY .JOTTINGS.</head>
        <p>PANTRY .JOTTINGS.</p>
        <p>Butter .should be kept closely covered, and if a large quantity has to be kept in hand it should be pressed as tightly as possible into a jar with a close-fitting lid. Tea must be kept in an airtight ti ri in cool place. Coffee should not be bought in large quan- tities, as it loses flavour. To be in perfec- tion it should be roasted and ground daily. It must be kept in airtight receptacles. Sugar of all kinds should be kept in closely covered tins or jars. Flour must be kept in an airtight placP. It soon absorbs moisture., and this turns it musty. .</p>
        <p>Household" flour is more nourishing than the finer varieties. Grains of all kinds should be bougllt in small quantities, otherwise they may get tiny insects in them. Oatmeal soon becomes- stale, and sour, so should be purchased in small quantities, and kept in a tin. Soap should be bought in large quantities, cut in pieces, arranged on a shelf, so that the air can get to it. It will then dry and harden and waste much less in the water. Spices are best bought in small quantities, as they soon lose 'flavour; they umieet to* stored in airtight tins.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>MORE BOWLING GREENS.</head>
        <p>MORE BOWLING GREENS.</p>
        <p>Healthy Recreation as a Set-Back</p>
        <p>to the Drink Evil.</p>
        <p>"I am glad the Town!Council is waking up to the fact that workingmen want places where they can obtain proper recreation. We must get our local legislators to open more bowling greens." Such were the remarks of Mr. Thomas Dale, who presided over an open-air meeting. held under the auspices of the Warrington Citizens' Temperance League at Cornhill, on Wednesday evening. His contention was that if more facilities for outdoor recreation were granted by the Corporation there would be no need for men to resort to public- houses to indulge in their favourite pastime. The principal speaker was Mr. W. C. Co3ne, of Manchester, who gave a very stirring and interesting address on the Temperance cause, telling of his own experiences and the pledge he had kept for 23 years. STARTLING LOCAL FIGURES.</p>
        <p>At the outset, the CHAIRMAN said that intemperance was very rampant in our town. Those who rtad the Chief Constable's report in February last would find some startling figures. There had been 471 males and 101 females arrested for drunkenness during the year, and Warrington took the s.econ€l place for drunkenness of tan large towns in this part of the country. That was not very encouraging, and they were inclined to ask" what is the reason for this ?" The Rector of Warrington, speaking some time ago, said he thought one of the causes was the redundance of licences. He (the speaker) did not know what the magistrates were thinking of 30 or 40 years ago to grant licences broadcast, for they found that a redund- ance of licences only encouraged drinking.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>DRINK AND POVERTY.</head>
        <p>DRINK AND POVERTY.</p>
        <p>He was not going to say that intemperance was the only cause of poverty, but he would say that it aggravated the poverty in our midst. If the money, spent on drink were used in the homes, a different state of affairs would be existing. (Hear, bear.) He knew of cases in Warrington where drink was bringing the homes to certain ruin. Young men who were addicted to intemperance need not seek to get good positions . to-day, -for employers of labour wanted the men -who were total abstainers. The child life of our town was suffering on account of the intemperance of the parents, children going about our streets poorly clad and ill-fed. It was true the fathers worked hard for small wages, but they were sorry to think it was spent in the wrong shop. The dearest things a man purchased</p>
        <p>" Holiday Haunts in the West of England' tells yrn where to go and wh2re to stay post free 2d. from Supt. 1 I et the Line, Paddington Station. W., or at G.W.R. Offices and Station. New illustrate ri bror" . "DEVON, the Lovely Land of the' Maytlowr Local agency: 42, Sankey-st. Warrington., ..P,-, ra-4.....---i '''.l - ,•Z.--,.- , .- i . 'N, • 011"....• - ....;- _</p>
        <p>Devonshire! Does not the very name conjure up visions of sweet-smelling, undulating country; of green hill and dale; of rivers and fishing and boating; If Ipples and cyder; of the simple grandeur of historic rural England? ;re is in reality as beautiful as this, and no Englishman has seen his country at her best wha has not visited "GLORIOUS DEVON." No better travel, no greater comfort than that °tiered by TIIE HOLIDAY LINE. Travel by the Great Western and start your holiday when you board the train. EXPRESS TRAIN SERVICES from ALL PA"</p>
