      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>Ai MC FLOUR FOR PASTRY. The Examiner, Saturday, June 28, 1913.</p>
        <p>who earns enough for comfort, or, at least what pro- duces comfort in one case, poorer than he really is. Seeing I happen to know what it is to work hard for a "very few shillings a week, and hare to eke out a miserable existence" on it, namely,lss. a week, and live in one room, with a wife and three children, and pay 4s. rent for same, I think the charge of libelling this class, is a little" off Ode." I happen to know quite as much of this side of life as perhaps your corres- pondent, and therefore do not hesitate to say what I know. I contend the man who is earning £2 or more a week need not, if the money is used right, either live in a slum, or become a pauper the first week he happens to be out of a job, and often would not, were i t not for the public-house. PETER TAYLOR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>frtters te Thitor</head>
        <p>frtters te Thitor</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>TIIE EDITOR of the " Examiner " invites correspond- ence on all Subjects of public interest. " LETTERS should be brief and pointed. Their olject must be to impart or seek information, not abuse: , LETTERS2nust in all cases be accompanied by the name</p>
        <p>... . and address of the writer. COHMUNICATI6NS received early -in the week—except urgent matters—can always be given the best attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD AND THE SUFFRAGE QUESTION.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD AND THE SUFFRAGE QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l read Mr. Tilling's letter in last week's Examiner " with interest and appreciation. Like my late President, Mrs. Stewart-Brown, I have also resigned from the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Women's Liberal Associations. Mr. Tilling may there- fore think that my continuance on our local executive is equivocal. But there is this explanation of it— though my brother may be sitting on the hedge in regard to this question, he is not down on the wrong side of it like Mr. Harold Smith. He might even descend our side.</p>
        <p>Personally, I believe in John Bright's motto: " Be just, and fear not," a much-forgotten one in these days of expediency and want of political faith, and I entirely agree with Mr. Tilling that the present govern- ment's otherwise fine record is grievously damaged byits behaviour to the Women's Suffrage cause; the which cause the National Liberal Association, repre- senting all the Liberal men in Great Britain.practically, decided unanimously in 1905, 1906, 1908, to be "A fundamental basis of Liberalism," and "an urgent reform that should no longer be delayed." Why have they eaten these words, shelved a Bill that seven times has passed its second reading,broken pledges of support by the dozen? Because that true and honest old Liberal, Sir Henry Campbell Banner- man, died, and Mr. Asquith reigned in his stead! Opponents of Women's Suffrage may be sure of one thing—that Mr. Asquith's name will go down in future history, not as the leader who broke the power of the Lords, helped through Home Rule and Welsh Disestablishment, saw the insurance of the people,and the great movement t 9 free the land begin, ecc.;but as the man who tried to obstruct a mighter cause than I even any of these—the upward progress of the women and with them of the men of England._</p>
        <p>T.-H. CROSFIELD.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>"THE TRADE" AND SWEATING.</head>
        <p>"THE TRADE" AND SWEATING.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The letter published in your issue of June 14th, over the name of "Edward .Tucker," . says:—" The argument that the drink trade pays less wages or employs less labour, as it is sometimes put, in pro- portion to its capital than any other trade, has long since lost its force." This may be his opinion, but it is not the opinion of those who know any- thing of the question. The right Hon. John Burns M.P., President of the Local Government Board, in the Free Trade Hall, Manchester, on October 13th, 1904, in the course of his "Lees and Raper. Memorial Lecture," said : "The extent to which liquor does not employ the same proportion of men per million of capital invested, as do other trades, is illustrated by two concerns. Whereas a 'railway employs 38,000 men who receive £3,250,000 in wages per annum, a well-known distillery employs 2,000 men, whose wages amount to £lOO,OOO. If the distillery employed the proportion of men equal to that employed on railways, 7,142 men instead of 2,000 would be engaged, and £357,0130 instead of £lOO,OOO per annum as wages would be circulated."</p>
        <p>"The wholesale trade is not conspicuous for its generosity to its workpeople, whilst the retail trade is, generally speaking, in the matter of long hours to its male and female workers, and, in wages per hour, possibly the worst of all trades, whilst the unlealthi- ness of the employment and liability to disease, makes both brewing-and public-house work an industry that should be scheduled as a dangerous trade." My. Burns used that argument after quoting the Govern- ment returns issued by Sir Michael Hicks-Beach, when President of the Board of Trade in 1891,, which were published in a Blue Book : Paid in wages Page in Occupation. out of each £lOO. Blue Book, value produced. 17 'Mining 55-0 24 Shipbuilding 37.0 40 Docks and Harbours 347 32 Railways 30-0 10 Agriculture 29-0 33 Canals 29-0 28 Cotton Manufacture 29.2 41 Water Works 25-7 22 Iron and Steel Manufacture 23-3 30 Textile Industries 22-6 18 Gas Manufacture 20-0 43 BREWING 7-5</p>
        <p>This does not appear as though the argument had lost its force, but then, that is from my side. Suppose I take one from the other side. We are told in Wright's" Text-book on Brewing," that the cost of a 36.ga110n cask of ale is about £1 Os. 11d. (including duty), and of this 2s. is all he puts down fJr "wages and salaries." If the same amount of value in coal was produced over lls, would be paid in wages. I cannot see how Mr. Tucker can honestly call the trade by any other Lame than "sweater," when we laarn from their own figures that, employed in manufacture and distribution of liquor there are about 420,000 but of these less than 100,000 are actually engaged in the production of what costs the buyers £162,797,229 (1911). Mr. Pratt ("Licensed Trade "), in hi. defence of the traffic, put the figures as 70,000 engaged in brewing and earning £6,250,000 in wages, and 485,000 distributors earning £29,009,000 a year. " Of these employes : "Seventy-one are young men under 25 years old, 43,000 beinn, barmen, or lintel servants ; _ .</p>
        <p>" 95,000 a're unmarried women, of whom 31,000 are barmaids or women publicans, and 64,000 domestic.: servants in hotels and other licensed premises." Even taking these figures, they do not in any way alter the fact, stated by me in my address. that " the trade is the "Champion Sweater," and workingmen seeking the interest of themselves or their fellcw-workingmen, would never spend their bard earned cash on liquor. As-one more argument, suppose you, sir, allow me to use a comparison made some time ago by one who has made this phase of the " question " a special study, (G. B. Wilson, 8.A., Howard Medallist, loyal Statis- tical Society), in his " 'The Trade' or the Nation" : "A comparison of official figures as to production and cost of coal in the United Kingdom with Mr. Pratt's figures given above, shows for 1902 : 805,100 miners getting out coal, valued, at the pit's mouth, at £93,519,000, the wages paid being £61,053,000; 70,000 brewers turning out beer valued, at Brewery, at £39,000,000, after deducting duty, the wages paid being £6,250,000; or in other words, the raising of £1,000,000, of coal employs 8,611 men, earning over £650,000; the making of £1,000,000 of beer employs only 1,795 men, earning £161,000." In my address my contention was, and is, that "the trade" does not employ pr oportionatelv the number it should, when compared with other trades, and there- fore was, and is, a sweater. It is also a sweater in the number of hours worked by employes as well as in the amount paid them as wages. W e have the employes' words for the hours and wages. I am more than pleased that my address has, through your columns, pricked someoae, or my visit would have been in vain. We temperance advocates always feel we have done some good when we find someone " smarts" under the "word spoke"_ _</p>
        <p>It is quite evident your correspondent, Edward Tucker, was not at the meeting, or he would not have rushed into print, but would have afforded me an opportunity of discussing the question vs ith him at the time, and then more information would have been given the audience who did hear what I did say. Your correspondent who raises the question of "Drink and Poverty" was not at the meeting any more than Mr. Tucker, and therefore did not hear what I said. I opened by saying I was not silly enough to come there and say that all poverty was caused by drink, but there was• poverty caused Iv drink. I quoted returns from Liverpool, taking three districts in which the number of licensed houses and the number of paupers were compared. Your correspondent says ,the comparison is not fair, as one of the districts is peopled by the comfortable middle class, and the other by the people who earn only a few shillings a week. sir, it is a surprise to me to find your correspondent calling the shipwright, the fitter, the painter, the joiner, the compositor, &amp;c.. a member of the comfortable middle class when living in one district, but of another class when he lives in the other district, yet men following these trades live in each of the districts named. I believe the men employed in these trades receive the same wage in Liverpool, whether they live in Hornby-street, or in Toxteth, and the wage that enables the one to live in comfort in the one district, ought to be sufficient to provide the same measure of comfort k the other ; but, no sir, the multitude of licensed places of tempta- tion robs the home of comfort, and makes the man</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>2, Palmerston-avenue, Wl:alley Range, Manchester. THE TARIFF QUESTION IN THE UNITED STATES. •</head>
        <p>2, Palmerston-avenue, Wl:alley Range, Manchester. THE TARIFF QUESTION IN THE UNITED STATES. •</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. E. England, in his letter on the proposed tariff in the United States, seeks to correct an impres- sion which he deems unwarranted, but in so doing he goes to the other extreme, and that more definitely than any enthusiastic Free Trader. This impression Mr. England conveys, not so much by what he himself says, but by giving a specially selected quotation; which, standing alone, might be thought to favour the strange economic theory of Protection to which Mr. England appears to have given his adhesion. Fortunately, the American President is both a clear thinker and a clear speaker, and be has so often spoken on the Tariff question as to leave no room for doubt as to his position. The quotation Mr. England gives is in perfect harmony with the rest of the President's utterances. The present intention of the President and his party is certainly not to adopt Free Trade in it entirety. A nation, like an individual that takes a wrong road, cannot immediately start on the right one. It must first retrace the way already taken ; or, to change the figure, national industries, like plants that have been hothoused into prosperity by Protection, cannot at once be exposed to the keen invigorating open air of Free Trade. They must be gradually hardened. But it is best to let Woodrow Wilson speak for himself, and from the quotations given Mr. England and your readers may form. their own opinions :</p>
        <p>"We shall deal with our economic system as it is and as it may be modified, not as it might be if we had had a clean sheet of paper to write upon, and step by step we will make it -what it should be." "We niust break down that great dam that runs around all our coasts, the restrictive tariff that hems us in,that chokes us, that smothers us."</p>
        <p>It will be seen from the following that the President admits the possibility of the advantage of protecting "infant industries," and in this he is in accord with Litz, of Germany, and, I believe to a certain extent, with John Stuart Mill. But as this argument is not now applicable to this country, and only very partially to America, for practical purposes it can be put aside, but it receives prominence in Woodrow Wilson's pro- nouncements. He says : "The existing system was built up by states- manlike and patriotic men, upon a theory, upon which even the most sceptical economist must concede it possible to found a valid and effective policy. It is very likely that by a slower,sounder, less artificial means the country might have worked its way up to the same extraordinary development and success, but that is a question no longer worth debate by practical men. As a matter of fact, the method of artificial stimulation was adopted. It will not do to reverse such a policy suddenly, or in revolutionary fashion. It must in some conservative way be altered from decade to decade, if possible from year to year, until we shall have put all customs legislation upon a safe, reasonable, and per- manent footing," "Only those undertakings should be given the protection of high duties on imports which are manifestly suited to the country and as yet unde- veloped or only impe_rfectly. developed. From all</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>rest PreteCtionshould be vitlidrawn."</head>
        <p>the rest PreteCtionshould be vitlidrawn."</p>
        <p>What is of practical significance to us is the attack the President makes on an argument that is the chief foundation stone of the Tariff Reform movement, the contention that it is a sound policy to foster by Protec- tion home industries, even if it be at an increased cost to the consumer. The President says : " It has been assumed that the prosperity of the</p>
        <p>individual interest considered wOuld be the pros- perity of the country; but the poor sophistry of that argument has long been commonplace. By hard use that assumption has been worn through to the thread. It must be replaced by new and sounder stuff. 'Feed and sustain those corpora tions and they will employ you; feed your employers out of the taxes and they, in turn, will give you work and feed you.' But no candid student of this great question can now confidently believe that a policy which has the profits of the manufacturers as its main object is likely to promote the impartial, natural, wholesome, sym metrical, general development of the country.' "The object of the Government being not to support its citizens in business but to promote the full energy aLd development of the country, and existing protection should not be suddenly with- drawn, but steadily and upon a fixed programme. For the rest, the jobject of customs taxation should be revenue for the Government."</p>
        <p>This last sentence is ihe most important, because it emphasises and approves the British`Fiscal Policy, that customs should be for revenue and not for protection._ _ _ _</p>
        <p>- I do not know if Mr. England would expect me to say anything about the quotation he giv,es from Abraham Lincoln, but your readers will probably agree with the old President, that he "does not know much about the tariff," when they consider how difficult it would be to cram any more confused think- ing into five lines of print than is contained in Mr. England's quotation.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER. Penketh, June 24th, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>IMPROVE YOUR COMPLEXION.</head>
        <p>IMPROVE YOUR COMPLEXION.</p>
        <p>If you wish a skin clear of pimples, blackheads andd, other annoying eruptions, hands soft and white, hair live and glossy, and scalp free from dandruff and itching, begin to-day the regular use of Cuticura Soap for the toilet, bath and shampoo, assisted by an occasional light application of Cuticura Ointment'. No other method is so agreeable, so often effective and so economical in treating poor complexions, red, rough hands, and dry, thin and falling hair. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by. chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post-free from nearest depot. Address: Newbery, 27,Charterhonse Sq., London; R. Towns it Co., Sydney, N. S. W.; Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town; Muller, Maclean ec Co., Calcutta and Bombay; or "Cuticura," Boston, U.S.A. Men who shave and shampoo with Cuticura Soap will find it best for skin and scalp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>QUAINT REMEDIES,</head>
        <p>QUAINT REMEDIES,</p>
        <p>Tigers flesh and new-born puppies were among old remedies, said Dr. F. M. Sandwith, Gresham professor of physic, lecturing in Lon- don. One book, he said, recommended a live spider to be rolled in butter and formed into a pill and then swallowed as an 'antidote "To jaun, dioe. Vipers were held in high esteem, and a broth made from them was said to strengthen the eyesight. In the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries powdered mummies were prescribed, and so great was the demand that fraud led to more mummies being supplied than ever came out of Egypt.</p>
        <p>V,/ It must be remembered that the rational view of disease was quite modern. In some of the older .herbal works a striking fact was the con- stant recommendation of various remedies for bruises. These give an idea of the manners of the day. John Gerrard, who was gardener of Lord Burleigh's 'garden in the Strand, and had a garden of his own in Holborn (then called a "suburb" of London), published a book in 1597 as his own, although it was, in fact, a transla- tion of a Belgian herbal. Gerrard recommended "Solomon's seal" for the taking await in one night of the blackness of bruises caused by falls, "or women's wilfulness in tumbling upon their hasty husband's fists, or such like ! "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>RUGOSITIES OF THE ORANGE-SKIN.</head>
        <p>RUGOSITIES OF THE ORANGE-SKIN.</p>
        <p>It may be added that if all the elevations on tli6 earth were removed and filled into all the hollows we should have a. world smooth as a 'biWard-ball and completely surrounded by an vean 1,450 'fathoms deep. This is called the 'mean phere level." The Atlantic has an aver- ego depth differing hut little from the "mean sphere level." Compared with the other great oceans it has an unusually large area of com- paratively shallow water. Of its tot%l area 27-5 per cent. is covered by water less than 1-,COO fathoms deep; .18 per cent, lies between 1,000 and 2,GtO fathoms; and 47 per cent. between 2,000 and 3.000 fathoms; the remaining 7.5 per cept.' is still deeper:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>DEATH OF MRS. W. BAFtILOW.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MRS. W. BAFtILOW.</p>
        <p>An Octogenarian Who Was Congratulated by King Edward.</p>
        <p>• One of the oldest and most respected inhabitants o Warrington passed away on Sunday in the person o Mrs. Nary Barlow, of 39, Bewsey-street. Although she had attained the ripe old age of S. years, Mrs Barlow had enjoyed remarkably good health up to thf beg;.nning of the present month. She was the wife Mr. William Barlow, head of the firm of Messrs. W. Barlow and Sons, corn merchants, Winwick-street, and a veteran Druid. Mrs. Barlow was the daughter of Mr. Peter Hedgcock, farmer, of Norton. It was on February 11th, 1850, at the Runcorn Parish Church, that she was married to Mr. Wm. Barlow by the Rev. Canon Barclay. In 1900, Mr. and Mrs. Barlow celebrated their golden wedding, and the occasion was marked by the holding of a family gathering in the Masonic Hall, Bold-street, at which presenta- tions were made and congratulatory speeches delivered. Three years ago the aged and devoted</p>
