      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LATE MRS. W. BARLOW.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MRS. W. BARLOW.</p>
        <p>their diamond wedding anniversary, and Mr. Barlow was the proud recipient of the following congratu- latory telegram from King Edward VII : Buckingham Palace. Mr. Barlow, 39, Bewsey- street, Warrington. I am commanded by the King to congratulate Mrs. Barlow and yourself on the attainment of your diamond wedding anniversary.— KNOLLYS.</p>
        <p>An "At Home" was held in Toft's Assembly Room, in recognition of the notable occasion, the guests including the Mayor and Mayoress of Warrington (Alderman and Mrs. Forshaw). Among the congratula- tory messages, addressed personally to Mrs. Barlow, was a letter from the late Mr. Charles White, who -wrote: "I do not think you have any friend of such long continuance as myself, and I am sure there is no one who desires your and Mr. Barlow's happiness in this world and the next more than I do." Mrs. Barlow was for many years a devoted worker in the cause of Wesleyan Methodism in Warrington. She was long identified with Bold-street Church, and before that building was erected she attended the services in the old Bank-street Chapel. She and her husband joined in the proce3sion from Bank-street to Bold-street Church when the latter place of worship was opened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place in the graveyard at Hill Cliffe, on Thursday afternoon. Owing to indisposition, Mr. Barlow was unable to attend. The last rites were performed by the Rev. W. A. Beckett, assisted by the Rev. Hugh Williamson. Prior to the interment, a service was held in the Bold-street 'Wesleyan Church, where a large number of friends of the deceased congregated to pay a last tribute of respect. The Rev. W. A. Beckett made touching reference to the high personality and character of Mrs. Barlow, both in her home and church life. In her home she had a powerful influence for good, and they thanked God for the place He had given her in the Church and for the ministries which had been associated with her long life. The congregation joined in singing the hymns, "God of my life through all my days' and " Lord it belongs not to my care." Mr. F. Hickman was at the organ, and played "Be thou faithful unto death," "0 rest in the Lord" and the Dead March in" Saul." The chief mourners were: Miss Barlow (daughter), Messrs. W. Barlow, I. E. Barlow and J. Barlow (sons) and Mr. H. Wilson (son-in-law). Among others present in the church or at the graveside were: Alderman James Evans, Mess". Wm. Silcock, Thomas Baxter, P. A. Harris, Wm: Isaac, J. B. Doncaster, P. Lawton, P. Pierpoint, G. Weaver, J. W. Hayward, C. g Parker, and Mrs. Parkinso6.</p>
        <p>Floral tributes were sent by the following :—Mr. William Barlow (husband), The Family, Grand- daughters (Gladys, Dorothy and Nancy Barlow), Mr. H. Wilson, Mrs. Wilde (sister), Mr. B. Warburton, Mrs. W. Wright and Mrs. E. Jolley, Alderman Forshaw and family, Mr. and Mrs. D. Wood, Mr. and Mrs. T. Lane (charley), Mr. and Mrs. E. Bradshaw, Mr. and Mrs. W. Wilde, Mr. and Mrs. Coleman, Mr. and Mrs. S. Rowe (Liverpool), Mrs. Parry, Mrs. Carruthers and Mrs. Lythgoe Miss M. Hedgcock, Mrs. J. Wilde and Mr. and Mrs.' B. Wilde, Mr. and Mrs. Levi Richardson, Mrs. and Dr. Edward Fox, Mr. and Mrs. L. A. Voisey, Mr. and Mrs. 1 Millard, Mr. and Mrs. H. Holloway and Miss Holloway, Mr. John Cooper (Lower Walton), Mr. and Mrs. King (Grappenhall), Bold-street Wesleyan Church Sewing Meeting, Mr. and Mrs: James Lingard, Mrs. Hoff and family (Manchester), Mrs. C. Hughes and family, Mrs. Higgins and Annie (maid).</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS. Straight Tight Sheath-like Dress</p>
      </div>
