      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Imminent Danger. LACE INCRUSTATIONS.</head>
        <p>Imminent Danger. LACE INCRUSTATIONS.</p>
        <p>Our King and Queen have departed from traditione,lr customs in the matter of the education of tbe.ite children, some of whom have been sent, when (ltilio young, to a boys' boarding schcol. Prince HenrY, pursuance of the plan laid down for his education, !liti shortly enter at Eton. The Queen who holds origla; opinions with respect to the education of Pe approves of emulation and competition in consequently - consequently Princess Mary who, like her mother, se, an only daughter, is not being educated in loneriliete and isolation, as was her great-grandmother, the 'ad, Queen Victoria, but her lessons and games are eberiehe when the Court is at Buckingham Palace, by ;ha daughters of certain Peers, girls of her own eV, daily attend at the Palace, and are taught by' tut he and,governesses selected by Her Majesty, for ere education of the Princess. It is said that the se'cl,39 simplicity of the attire of the young Princess :big influenced' the fashions of young girls' dresses last summer, these being much more girlish than</p>
        <p>season.</p>
        <p>FULL, ALL-ROUND SKIRTS. OA Straws show which way the wind blows, fact that one of the cleverest of London designers4.i:le made dresses for her youthful and fasbioao bt customers with full skirts,the antithesis of the straihgo tight, 'sheath-like garment, to which every wornat3,",o been so long wedded, is a sure indication el", 311 imminent and drastic change. Every adjunct, se!'dtb. the trimmings of these dresses accentuate their and these are not the only signs that point to a res"'"o4 of picture fashions. Round capes and mantelett ot with long ends falling on the back of the dressill the front, as when these were worn years' agolhe have a vogue this summer. They are drawn in 5t waist, which is high, and fall over the sleeves a 5 as the elbow. With these quaint shoulder garl3l-113. the Mid-Victorian Leghorn hat goes exceedingl9; Tnese hats are trimmed with bizarre chouz ef.."-sre and streamers of coloured ribbons, On which - 'fashioned the quainest of tiny posies. THE SUMMER BLACK VELVET HAT. Tot Quite a twentieth century idea is the black "re.og, bat for summer wear, which looks quite in 1531,1; s• and remarkably well, with the flimsiest of are-itees Hats of black velvet, tulle and lace, are first favaurilje just now, and were quite conspicuous at Ascot. oo combination may sound incongruous, but it is n°l;ttle in reality, and the changes that may, with a ft of ingenuity, be rung on it, are nothing sho:tor marvellous, and would seem to have a particular tion for milliners this summer. The hats with ," vot velvet crowns and uhite lace brims—sbady, bu'i;glat by any means extreme in width—are delightfullY ce and very beautiful in line. Very often these hata 'oat the lightest of trimmings, merely a rather. bigb !rod of lace, although leathers are frequently empinYe.u,',l3ed to good advantage. The all-black velvet hat, IT!' et also with black plumage, which towers aloft, 11 at all heavy, nor does it look so, but" goes with the dresses designed especially for it. I)'l3ii ness, indeed, is a feature of this year's millinerY, ;3ibe the butterfly bat will be very much worn' trimmings of a number, of these are beanti modelled butterflies with bodies and antennte of velvet, and wings wide outspread of lace': othersll6 a number of wings of tulle for their sole aclornmePt' MOIRE AND WHITE PIQUE. The popularity of moire deservedly increases (Offal week, and next to mousseline-de-soie it.is the learifr favourite. Milliners use it quite as freely as dr; ot makers and tailors, the latter finishing their ewer _oo coats with this handsome fabric. It makes crotinor and trims hats of every description. A Particaire type of hat has its crown covered with wide otA ribbon, which is simply tied in long loops at the bb of and constitutes the sole trimming, save for a buil„-aer flowers or fruit in the front. Black moire, in ct,ore tion with white, makes a very distinctive toilettePr at especially if the belt affords a bold dash of cel°tlscitt, the waist. A black tulle hat, relieved kes white feathers, or masses of soft white tulle, of chit.° delightfully appropriate bat for such a black 50" sciyet toilette—which may be worn again and again allrigge never pall on its wearer or her friends. %OW ases: is another material for which designers find noli'tailol" among which may be mentioned the waist-coat are sending out with their smartest tailored cols° Brocade and Ottoman silks in dark colours /reable* used in this connection, but although more sery'cos the and not soiling so readily, they are not so chic d tbe white. These waistcoats ire cut very low, .3nto the frills that adorn them give quite a Byronic tux.</p>
        <p>wearer. INCRUSTATIONS OF LACE. - t Des The makers of modes would seem to use endeavours to elabbrate every article of attire. sive, innovation, which is both extravagant and esPe° roe is the incrustation of lace on various materials'Jattel; fashionable lace glove .is not wholly of the; fabric. It is of kid, on which are in crustat'°a- bto lace, that, starting at the back of the hold' re lace, elbow, giving it must be admitted, a rich, %lope? ance. The lace incrusted shoe, is quaint 2E1;001: having long narrow square toes, of the 18 tu:S; Is" very handsome and rising somewhat at the s"—ago'svo• .new model, to be worn with a beautiful evenipgiSread Is of yellow bengaline covered with fitle nr,a- Vienna of an ecru shade. Many fashionable l';:r ' -Venna are at present intent upon making t",",ii ° evening shoes, and very dainty are the re— oboe° their unwonted labours. Not only gloves 15,11c1 ted tc; are incrusted with lace, but dresses are°- this process. Some charming gardea-partY lace' delicately coloured lawn are covered with tbi°7 L. so, But the result is not quite so satisfactorY, °eyed.. impression of warmth and weight are c°l3lairls, However, the dresses are not intended for rung Dale but for women who are frankly anproachiug age.</p>
