        <p>ANDALUSIANS. 414, 110'w come to one of our oldest breeds of poultry. ithe" are also one of the most useful. A The time was d f torl, nearly every farm-yard stock comprise o. th(RaLBlan blood. The reason for the falling o in because of the craze for new breed and nd seem to have been overdone in this itt.4e-1,4111 and while one does not belittle the efforts ei-'OP poultry in any way, one has to admit that Nrec3rning of new breeds has been slmew hat l'6111(3ne• In the long run the fittest only ster-41.7ive and then we shall find that some of the tifll4.vatietits will again come into their own. Like 'ad 1114 ea, the Andalusian is an ideal backyarder. 4"4ll3litside the almost extinct Black Spanish, .If3alth,4Bians lay the largest eggs of any breed of 40•:• The only drawback seems to be that they bre( d very well to colour. A large per- ill.kii7lBl e 0! the progeny come mismarked ; but niti do ntirely on the ultility side of the question. , cole° not at all matter in the least. No one minds 113:nt ee touch if they lay plenty of eggs. From rr thi,tTri Point of view, there is something to be said 64-h...4eficiency of number in good coloured speci- etiN ause you can always demand the highest ;Tr perfectly coloured specimens. Not ..mly • also makes the work of breeding that much titterest o lag. The more difficult a bird is to breed 17(),:npt3tP° and colour, the more interesting the hobby nel'iveja• The past few seasons have witnessed a of the Blue Andalusian, and anyone taking tit-P now should find them a most profitable f -eln_ti• Here, then, are two breeds of poultry; k"tnitiV,p variety and one new, and which should h,:u.7 amateur with ample scope for his ingenuity fand with plenty of oppor- y o poultry succes</p>
