      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>LATE MR, R. BURROWS.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR, R. BURROWS.</p>
        <p>An Appreciation.</p>
        <p>BY G. A. LEACH.</p>
        <p>The late Mr. Ralph Burrows was just a bumble village peasant. The whitewashed walls and uncovered floors of his country cottage were in harmony with his own simple tastes. He was one of Nature's own children. The velvet lawn and the artificial terrace knew him not. Country lanes, woods and dells were his happy and frequent retreat. He loved little children, he loved music; but there was no music so sweet to him as the rippling brook and the rustling of the autumn leaves. He paid tribute to the skill of the modern horti - culturist ; he watched with interest the development of the chrysanthemum and the begonia. He looked with pleasure at the handiwork of the cottage gardener. The order and symmetry of modern methods were equally praised by him, but, after all,he found a greater joy in gathering buttercups and daisies.</p>
        <p>The flaunting flowers our gardens yield, High shelttring woods an' wa's mann shield; But thou, beneath the random biehl 0' clod or atane, Adorns the histie stibble-fleld, Unseen, alane. —Burns : "To a mount in daisy." These lines, from Ralph's favourite poet, can best express his love of Nature's flowe rs.</p>
        <p>Eccentric-, retiring, he lived his life. The world is all the better for these humble, noble men, and by not a few people, Ralph Burrows will be greatly missed. His real knowledge of botany made him closely acquainted with even the rarest f-recimcn of wild flow( rs. In a letter, Mr. Dun lops (Warrington M us)um) says that our late friend was their largest collector of wild flowers, and this year alone he had brought in no fewer than 200 specimens. He took a great interest also in parish affairs, and closely studied anything in connection with Grappenhall. At the time of his death he was, along with mys elf and Mr. Thomas Shakeshaft (an old friend), preparing the article for the pages of the " Warrington Examiner " which appeared last week on the ninth page. He was very devoted to the " Exa miner," and often talked of its useful- ness and its future. He was a great lover of poetry, and his library included the works of Burns, Poe, Scott, Cowper and Browning,!&amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>His remains were laid to rest in the Grappenhall Churchyard on Monday last week. The chief mourners were : —James and Sarah Burrows (niece and nephew), Mr., Mrs. and Miss Hampson, Mr. Thomas Burrows, set'., and Mr. Thos. Burrows, jun. (distant relatives) ; Captain Waging, Messrs. S. H. Jenkins, George Hankey, James Goulding and Geo. A. Leach. The bearers were :—MeFers. H. Carter, J. Houghton, James Bate, Paul Peters, W. Peters and Job.a Clare. Floral tributes were sent from "James, Sarah and Emily " ; "Harriett and George and family" ; Jim and Mary Hampson ; Mr. and Mrs. Carter and family ; and a few bunches of wild flowers were placed on the coffin as it was carried to the grate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>LADY FAINTS IN HORSEMARKETSTREET.</head>
        <p>A LADY FAINTS IN HORSEMARKET- STREET.</p>
        <p>A lady was looking in at Suits Ltd.'s window in Horsemarket-street this morning, when she was over- come with faintness. Upon regaining consciousness she exclaimed, " 1 really couldn't help it, the surprise was too much for me ! I never saw such bargains in my life before; it was the suitings in Suits Ltd.'s window at 20s. to measure—which did it. The MAnager of Suits Ltd. did all he could to restore the lady—but in order to prevent such accidents in the future occurring from the same cause the public are warned that if they will look in at their windows they must be prepared for surprises. The value they give in their Suits to measure from 20s. will prove equally as great a shock to them as it did to the lady who fainted this morning. The Tailors have not yet recovered from the shock that Suits Ltd.'s 20s. Worsteds, Serge and Tweed Suitings gave them when they opened their local branch in Horse- market-street, for a saving of 103. a suit to the public is an item the public think worth having, especially when a written guarantee is given, that if the suit is not satisfactory another one will be made free of charge, or the money refunded in full to the customer. This Suits Ltd. do. More ,ver, there are over 1,000 different cloths to select from at their branch, which it is impossible to find at any other shop but theirs in Warrington. Their terms are :—A small deposit with order only, and the balance when till order is com- pleted and in every way satisfactory. Will you call or send for patterns ? The address of Messrs. Suits Ltd. is 17, Horsernarket-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>SALVATION ARMY EMIGR ATI ON</head>
        <p>SALVATION ARMY EMIGR ATI ON</p>
        <p>A GREAT WORK IN WHICH SUCCESSES ARE MANY AND FAILURES ARE FEW.</p>
        <p>The Emigration Department of the Salvation Army has just issued a book entitled "Census Surplus and Empire," in which is set forth the history of that organisation since its commencement ten years ago. It was the opinion of the late General Booth that any scheme of emigration worthy of the name must be of threefold usefulness. In the first place, it must seek to benefit the individual by raising his life to a new level of hopefulness and endeavour ; then it should be of value to the over-populated Motherland by relieving her internal strain ; and it ought to be of service to the lands across the sea by securing for them people of good character, whom they need in the interests of their development. The Emigration Department in its step by step progress has endeavoured to carry out this ideal. Founded in 1903, up to the present time under the direction of Commissioner D. C. Lamb, it has emigrated between 70,000 and 80,000 people to the Dominions, mostly to Canada. Out of this number less than one per cent. have proved failures, which speaks highly for the carefulness of the department's method of selection and for the denkand there is in the Dominions for all who are willing to put their shoulder to the wheel. The army guarantees work on the land for all who desire it, its labour bureau for the other side being worked by means of an extensive immigra- tiun organisation, whose officers, from their special tiaining and intimate knowledge of local conditions, are able to give scund advice and render substantial assistance to the laic( ming settler. Contained within the pages of "Census Surplus and Empire" is the cumulative experience of ten years' emigiation and colonising work, which is valuable for the light it gives as to the kind of people who are most likely to benefit by emigration, and the many details it gives as to the life lived by the settler in the Dominions in the preliminary struggles of his pro- gress along the highway of prosperity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>DEATH OF THE VICAR OF GLAZEBURY.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF THE VICAR OF GLAZEBURY.</p>
        <p>A familiar figure at local church conferences has been removed by the death of the Rev. George Feather, vicar of All Saints', Glazebury, in his seventy-sixth year. Mr. Feather went to the parish in IS6S as curate, and live years ago he was the recipient of a handsome present from the parishioners, in recognition of his forty years' work. During Mr. Feather's illness, the rarish has been in charge of the Rev. F. C. ShirtclifT, who is highly respected in Warrington for his good work among the poor in St. Ann's pariah,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, June 28, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 28, 1913.</p>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>THE LABOURERS' STRIKE.</p>
        <p>Over 2,000 Men Cease Work in West</p>
        <p>Lancashire,</p>
        <p>" RECOGNITION " INSISTED UPON.</p>
        <p>All doubt as to the intentions of the farm labourers in the Ormskirk district of West Lancashire was removed on Monday, when over two thousand men, whose notices had expired, not only refused to resume work but organised and carried out a scheme of picketing that apparently n ade all agricultural work, and more plait ulat ly the marketing of the produce, a matter of difficulty. In the townships between Ormskirk and Liverpool feeling ran high, particularly in Maghull and Lydiate, situated en the main road to the iiy, along which the largest part of the farm produce is carted. On tlonday several disagreeable incidents were reported. A ycung Irish labourer stupped a pir(_ duce merchant's waggon at Ormskirk station aid was arrested for beit g or der the influence cf drink. At Maghull a Liverpool hay merchant's men, whilst caring two loads of hay, had the ropes binding the hay to the waggon cut by strikers, whilst at Lydiate a number of pickets went into a farmer's field, detached the horses from a plough, despite the farmer's protests, and insisted on the animals returning to the stable. This was done by the farmer, who immediately cycled to Ormskirk. An extra staff if police have been covering the affected area by foot and bicycle, but despite their vigilont efforts the strikers were effectual in Mlle instances in using physical force, th,ugh no serious damage resulted.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a meeting of the Strike Cum- mittee was held at Ormsk irk, and a manifesto was issued that all x egotiations from now must be carried on between the farmers and the Strike Commi:t( e, and that under no circumstances must individuals attempt to come t.) an agreement with employers. A night-shift of pickets was appointed to prevent produce being carted through to market at night. It was reported that forty f.irmers in the district had grantel the union demand, and that fifty had applied for permits for the safe carriage of produce to market. It is understood that permits have been given to those farmers who have granted the union demands. Lord Derby arrived at Knowsley on Mn day for the purpose of meeting the men employed on his farms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>FARMERS AND LABOUR SHORTAGE.</head>
        <p>FARMERS AND LABOUR SHORTAGE.</p>
        <p>- Cheshire farmers have no strikes to contend with but there is a great shortage of labour, and this is proving a serious difficulty now that harvest-time is here. It is feared that many farmers will be unable to get in all their crops and will have heavy loss through this lack of assistance. Rain fell in tcrrents in many parts of Cheshire on Tuesday, and all harvesting was stopped. NO COMPROMISE.</p>
        <p>Each party to the dispute intends, apparently, that there shall be no compromise and no decision except by the defeat of the other. The estimate made by the Union is that no more than 2 or 3 per cent. of its mombers are still at work, and that apart from 6(83 there are only about 70 men in the whole area who are at work in the fields. The contest is going on bitterly. The employing farmers who try to get their produce through to the Liverpool markets are b. jag met by bands of strikers on the way. There was another disturbance on Wednesday, when a convoy of carts coming from Maghull was stopped by about 100 strikers and held np for some time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>PUBLIC AID FOR AGRICULTURE. ---- Mr. Runciman a Strenuous Advocate For Education.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AID FOR AGRICULTURE. ---- Mr. Runciman a Strenuous Advocate For Education.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rucciman, Pre3idert of the Board of Agricul- ture, opened on Saturday the farm school established by the Carnarvonshire County Council at Maaryn Castle, an ancient family seat, with an estate, covering some 2,210 acres, which was acquired for small hold- ings purposes, two years ago, by the Carnarvonshire County Council for £45,000. In declating the school open, Mr. Runcintan said the reason why Ireland was the most favoured partner in the United Kingdom in the matter of agricultural grants was that part of the money derived from the Irish Church funds went to agricultural education, and that Ireland also rated itself to the extent of seven eighths of a penny for the same object. In Scotland, again, they devoted the whole of the "whiskey money" to agricultural education. In Wales it went towards general technical education. But as long as he was at the Board of Agriculture he was prepared to be a strenuous advccate of giving all that money to aid agricultural education. They were already making up leeway to some degree. Through the Development Fund he had £325,000 placed at his disposal for farm institutes. Finding that upon the basis of population and acreage only 12 per cent. of this amount could be allocated to Wales, he made a comparison between Wales and England in the matter of holdings under 100. acres in extent, and be then found Wales was entitled to 18 per cent., and he had also given her a little more than that. An agreement had been come to with the Development Com- missioners, by winch the grants for horses and pigs were to be very largely supplemented. He hoped every patch of waste land in Carnarvonshire which would be regarded as a sin in sun° Continental countries would in time disappear. If the utmost productions of the land were obtained the wealth of that county would be increased by £200,000 a year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>MILK RECORDS,</head>
        <p>MILK RECORDS,</p>
        <p>BAD COWS LIVE ON PROFITS OF THE GOOD ONES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Alfred Mansell, in the course of a paper read at a meeting of the Shropshire Chamber of Agriculture, said that in the breeding of milking stock milk records stand in the relation to utility that the registered pedigree does to prepotency, and until this is generally recognised there is almost certain to be in every herd a proportion of cows living on the profits earned by the better milkers. The farmer who pays attention to useful details is assured of a reward for his trouble. The owner of a dairy herd in the Thames Valley, who started farming on his own account only about six years ago, states that he has in that time greatly increased the output of milk without adding to the nuts. ber of cows or to the food bill. He adopted the system of keeping milk records at the beginning, and has continued it throughout, utilising the figures as a guide in Felecting the animals to keep and to sell. The production of milk per cow has improved annually, and he expects this year to have an average of over 800 gallons for each animal. Most o: the young cows were bred on the farm from animals that were deep milkers and by bulls of suitabla breeding, with the result that his cows in their first lactation period give from 600 to 700 gallons of milk, or more than the average for the great majority of herds whose milking capabilities have never been ascertained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>HAY HARVEST NOT• ABNORMAL.</head>
        <p>HAY HARVEST NOT• ABNORMAL.</p>
        <p>The number and size of stacks of new hay tell that good progress has been made in the harvesting of the clovers, seeds, and even meadows, says the "Times." There are many crops of more than average bulk, especially of clovers and other rotation hay, but on an average there will be no abnormal production, many of he early meadows indeed being short of expecta- tion. The weather has been so favourable for hay- making, however, that the material can be stacked in any bulk without fear of heating, the size of the stack being regulated solely by consiAiration of conveni- ence. Compared with last year the first crop of rotation hay stands out to great advantage. It would be premature to say much concerning the meadows beyond that what has already been carried is of fine quality, colour, and flavour. The quantity is only moderate, and there is no appearance of a different result in the late meadows, although there i still time for the crops to improve both in thickness and in lengih.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>ROYAL SHOW AT BRISTOL. His Majesty's Entries of Cattle, Pigs, and Sheep.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL SHOW AT BRISTOL. His Majesty's Entries of Cattle, Pigs, and Sheep.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Mcßow, the secretary of the Royal Agricultural Society of England, has issued his customary pamphlet giving all the leading particulars relating to the forthcoming show. From this we learn that local support, one of the leading essentials to success, has been on a satisfactory cicala at Bristol, some 600 contributors having got together a local fund</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>of over £6,000. No less than £ll,OOO is offered in prize-money, an amount only once exceeded —at Kilburn in 1879.</p>
        <p>In the horse section, prizes amounting to £3,591 are offered for: Shires, Clydesdales, Suff,lks, hunters, polo and riding ponies, Cleveland bays or coach horses, hackneys, hackney ponies, Shetland ponies at(l Welsh ponies. The classes for cattle, for which the prizes amount to £2,854, include: Shorthorns, Lincolnshire Red Shorthorns, Herefords, Devons, South Devons, Long- horns, Susex, Welsh, Red Poll, Aberdeen-Angus, Galloways, Highland, Ayrshires, British Holstein, Jerseys, Guernseys, Kerrys, Dexters, and Dairy Cows. For sheep, prizes amounting to £1,836 103. have been provided. Other details of the prize list are; Pigs £762 ; poultry, £432; butter, £56; cheese, £B2; cider, £56; wool, £O6; hives, honey aud bee appliances, £5l ; but ter competitions, £Bl. The entries are: Horses, 581; c ittle, 1,13 i ; sheep, 736 ; pigs, 394; poultry, 1,436; produce, 685 His Majesty the King will have 34 entries from the Royal fauns. From Windsor will be sent 15 exhibits—all of cattle—consisting of six Shorthorns, five Het efords, and four Devons. The t'andt ingham entries are 19 in number, and comprise eight Dexter cattle, nine pens of Southdown sheep, and two Berkshire pigs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>WHITE PINKS.— I have been looking carefully in a a Rood many gardens lately for the old whi.e pink which was at one time so popular and so extensively grown. Only in one place have I found it, and then it was referred to in rather an apologetic way. Its p‘ace has been usurped by Mrs. Sinkins and Her Majesty, and while it is idle to deuy the merit of these tw., superb i inks, it seems a pity that the older variety, which had much to recommend it, should be apparently doomed to extinction. I hope that in many a quiet and unpretending garden this old favourite may still hold pride of place, although, sorry am I to say so, I myself have suffered it to be tusted by the larger and more showy varieties above mentiotaed.— K. Bright in " Gardening Illustrated."</p>
        <p>THE PI.A6UE OF SLUGS. - I do not remember a season when s'ugs caused so much havoc amongst tender things, says J.C.F.C. in "Illustrated Gardening." In my light soil it has been almost impossible to beep things from being destro3ed, do what one may. Where the soil is heavy it has been very difficult to raise many crops from not being able to break the soil down. Under the lumps cl soil the small slugs hide themselves, and DO amount of dusting with soot, lime, &amp;c., has any effect cu them. In one garden I have charge of, the soil being very heavy, I sowed some peas, and when they had been sown a few days the rake was used to crush the lumps. Before doing this a good dressing of soot and rough ashes was thrown over the soil. This was mixed with the soil in making it fine. Since I did this all has been well. Like Mr. Cornhill (May 17th), I have found bran an excellent trap for slugs. SOOT IN THE GARDEN.—I have always regarded soot as one of the best manures, and have never been able to get enough. Few things are more helpful to plants, etc., growing in pots, used alone or in conjunction with other manures for making manure-water. This spring I used it as a sutface dressing alone on the cabbage-bed, with the very best results. Where clubbing is troublesome it is most helpful. My method is to apply a good coating, mixing it well with the soil some time before the s'ed is sown. When the plants are a few inches high a coating is put on, and about once a month all through the growing season. Last year I had much trouble with clubbing on my wallflowers, but a dressirg of soot mixed with poultry-manure applied on the surface and likely forked did a deal of good.—West Surrey, in "Illus- trated Gardening."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>DISEASES OF ANIMALS.</head>
        <p>DISEASES OF ANIMALS.</p>
        <p>In the week ended June 14th there were 65 fresh outbreaks of swine fever, chiefly in Yorkshire and Wilts. The pigs slaughtered as diseased or as having been exposed to infection numbered 772. In 13 new cases of anthrax 15 cattle were attacked in four counties in Scotland and five English counties. There were two new outbreaks of glanders, while one case of sheep scab was notified from the West Riding of Yorkshire.</p>
      </div>
