      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>INDEPENDENT METHODISTS.</head>
        <p>INDEPENDENT METHODISTS.</p>
        <p>Education, R eligious Equality and</p>
        <p>Peace.</p>
        <p>The annual conference of the Independent Methodist Churches has been held at Wigan this week. The retiiing President (Mr. W. Price, Wigan), in his address, said they rejoiced in the peace movement, but lamented the materialism in the churches of the land. Their own churches needed warning against the latter. They should not set a cash value on service, Lut uphold the ideal of a free church and a free ministry—a minis- try which could at least present the background of a disinterested service. There must be a living message for a strenuous age like this ; the pulpit was the place not for the spolegetie, but for the positive declaration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>NONCONFORMIST TEACHERS' GRIEVANCE.</head>
        <p>NONCONFORMIST TEACHERS' GRIEVANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Watson (Nelson) submitted a resolution welcoming the Government proposal to introduce a measure dealing with the educational system, and expressing the hope that it would be upon the lines of placing education on a really natloaal awl unsectarian basis. Mr. Watson referred to the injustice under which Nonconformist teachers suffered as a result of denominational tests. In mnry to• Ans, he said, there wore fewer council schools than denominational schools, and when a vacancy arose in a council school the headmaster of a denominational school generally applied. If he were appoin'ed, there was, of course, a vacancy in the denominational school, but a Noncon- formist could not get appointed there. Mr. Hunter (Leigh) seccnded the resolution, which was carried unanimously. Mr. E. Barter (Colue) moved a resolution supporting the Welsh Church Bill. He sad he hal no antipathy to the Anglican Church, hut there would never be the unity which should exist among the Christian Churches so long as the Stele recognised ono to be in a different ' position from the others.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Broadhead (Pendleton) fecencled the proposi- ion, and it was ndoptel. ENGLAND AND GERMANY.</p>
        <p>A resolution welcoming the signs of growing friend- ship between Englan' and Germany and protesting against any scheme involving compulsory service was moved by Mr. E. Italphs (We,thoughton). Mr. Ralphs said that thero was a barrier in the way of educational progress because of the vested interests of the Anglican school; there was a barrier to religious equality because of the vested intensts of the clergy of the Anglioan Church; there was a barrier in respect of licensing reform because of the vested interests of the brewers and shareholders in breweries ; and so far as regarded the world's peace, there was a vested interest in regard to the production of armaments. It was a great factor in the preservation of peace that there were in this country 10,000 persons, chiefly ministers of all denominations, associated for the promotion of good- will between England and Germany. Mr. W. 11. Riding (Colne) seconded, and the resolu- tion was passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>LICENSING REFORM.</head>
        <p>LICENSING REFORM.</p>
        <p>On the prop osition of Mr. John Crumblehulme (Southport), seconded by Mr. A. Dean (Bingley), a motion was adopted urging the Prime Minister to introduce the promised Licensing Bill during the present session. The resolution added : "This Assembly further begs to assure the Government that so-called disinterested management has not the support of those who desire to see effective temperance legisla- tion placed upon the Statute Book."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The election of officers resulted as follows : President, Mr. J. Johns )n (Sunderland) ; secretary, Mr. J. Vickers (llorwicb) ; assistant secretary, Mr. E. Embleton (Sunderland) ; treasurer, Mr. A. Watson (Nelson) ; editor, Mr. A. Motmfleld (Warrington) ; foreign missionary secretary, Mr. J. W. Hancock (Stretford). There were invitations from Waterside Church (Come) and Pendleton for the next annual meeting, and it was resolved to accept that of the Colne Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>ONCE FAMOUS SINGER.</head>
        <p>A ONCE FAMOUS SINGER.</p>
        <p>Madame Susanna Cole, A.R.A.M., a once famous vccalist and colleague of Jent y Lind, has just passed away in a nursing home at Bornsey, London. Madame Cole made her debut at Exeter Hall in 1849, and at once sprang into public favour, holding a lead- ing position in ti e musical world for many years. In 1870, Madame Cole organised an Englith opera corn- pyny and toured, under her husband's management, through the United Kingdom and It eland with great success, until laid up with illness. On the death of her husband, in 1896, the Prime Minister (Mr. Balfour) made Madame Cole a substantial grant from the Royal Bounty Fund, and she devoted her time since [then to teaching the art she herself mastered with such signal success.</p>
        <p>RUSKIN MANX HOMESPUN&amp; The St. George's Guild, of which Ruskin was master, founded at Laxey, Isle of Man, a mill for the produc- tion of good honest cloths. Anyone needing Dress Lengths, ratings, etc., as recently supplied to H.M. the King, of unimpeachable quality and beautiful design, ,hould send for patterns of THE RUSKIN MANX HOMESPUNS, the ST. GEORGE'S MILL, LAXF.Y, ISLE OF MAN, Or G. HOLROYD .sc, SON, 110, Station St, Huddersfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>SOME WORKING</head>
        <p>SOME WORKING</p>
        <p>GIRLS LOSE TOO MUCH TIME</p>
        <p>Two Girls Tell How To Avoid It.</p>
        <p>There is nothing that teaches more than experience. We therefore quote from the letters of two girls who suffered and were restored to health. The same remedy is within the reach of all.</p>
        <p>"Before taking the first bottle of Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound I suffered agony, but after your wonderful medicine had been taken awhile I felt a little better, and after taking seven bottles of it I feel that I can truly say I have no more pain or inconvenience.</p>
        <p>"As I am out in the business world as a typist, I come into contact with many girls, and when the opportune moment arrives I tell them about Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound and I know that quite a few are taking it." (Name on request.)</p>
        <p>Another Girl's Experience.</p>
        <p>"I am a typist and book-keeper, and Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound has saved my life. I am enjoying the best of health now, but I was suffering from women's ills, and would have backache, headache and fainting spells." (Name on request.)</p>
        <p>.y for a free private book (to women only) to the Lady Manager, the Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a Hampstead Road, London, NAV. For 2s. 9d. she will send you a bottle of the Lydia E. l'inkham Vegetable Compound, or for 4s. 6d. a much larger bottle. Any chemist will supply it at these prices.</p>
