      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>NEW MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>NEW MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A list of eight new magistrates for the corhair of Chester includes the names of Andrew Thomas traith, 21, Croxteth-road, Liverpool, general manager of the Castner-Kellner Alkali Company, Weston Point, Runcorn, who was recently elected a member of the Cheshire County Council as the repreenintive of Itunc,:rn North ; Francis Wiswall, 10, Staaley-villas, Runcorn, civil engineer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>GALLANT RESCUES IN THE bIERSEY.</head>
        <p>GALLANT RESCUES IN THE bIERSEY.</p>
        <p>A gallant rescue was effected by a Widnes man In the river Mersey, on Sunday afternoon. While a •tumber of children were playing near the Transporter Bridge a little girl named Eliza Roberts fell in. William. Taylor, who was standing near, ran to the bank and :Jaw the child being carried away by the strong ebb tide. Taking off his coat he plunged into the water and gr...sped the child, but swimming against the running tide he appeared to lose strength. nonce Atherton, aft xt captain, dived in to Taylor's assistance anl hauled him to land safely with the child. The girl was unconscious, but revived after about half-au-hour's work '),y Atherton with up-to-date first aid metiaods.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>'CHILD BILTR:ST TO DEATH..</head>
        <p>'CHILD BILTR:ST TO DEATH..</p>
        <p>A burning accident occurred atlton '3took, near Rut:corn, on Tuesday night, reputing in the death from terrible injuries of the three-and-a-'nalf-year-old daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Miller. The mot her was away from home and the father v.al at work, the children being left in the custody of a ten-year-old. daughter. A neighbour saw the child in fhimes and at ~nce went to :yts assistance. The child's brother was Trourill water over his sister's head at the time, but the neighbour extinguished the flames by wrapping a r ;oat around the child. After receiving medical atten lance the child was taken to the Ituncorn Cottage I- .ospital, but the injuries were so serious that death to ok place early on Wednesday morning. The child was 'terribly burnt, its features being beyond recognition. JUMPED INTO TIIE 'ANAL.</p>
        <p>Merger-a Islintyre of Market-r,treet, Widnes, was charged at Runcorn on Tuesday, with attempting to commit suicide. Lancelot hail wood, a young sailor, residing in Percival-lane, ltunc,arn, said on Monday he heard screams coming from the direction of tLe Waterloo Bridge, and saw pr:.sorisr jump into the canal. lie plunged in, and with an,Aber boArnan, who had a boathook, they succeeded in rescuing her.—Prisoner's husband told the n anistrates that his wife was sub- jected to pains in the head, !rind wen in the habit of taking powers. She took two powders in one day, and went for a third, and the chemist warned, her that he would not be responsible for what might happen. The pains came en suddenly, and then she seemed to lose control of herself.—'The Chairman told prisoner her husband would take I. er away, and that. she must be sensible and leave powd. ss alone for the future.—Hall- wood waa complimented on his bravery. HEARTLESS RUNCORN PARENT'S SENT TO GAOL.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn on Saturday, Arthur and Sarah White were charged with abandoning their four children. Mr. Hough, who prosecuted on behalf of t'3o N.S.P.C.C., said ths prisoners recently resided in Cross-street, Runcorn, but on the 11th inst. the mother went away and left the children in the street. The father the same night went to his mother's hem and asked her if th 3 could take the children in. She ~old him she could not and he went away and left the children. Eventu- ally the children got into the herds of the Poor Law- Authority and they were remove', to Dalton Work- house. Subsequently, ii warrant was taken out for the, prisoners. The male prisoner gave himself up to the police. Inspector Evans said the case had been under the society's supervision since 1908. It was purely a. case of drink. Euperintendent Ennion said the mara bad served six weeks for child neglect, and the female prisoner this year had been sentenced to three month for neglect. A sentence of six raw ths' hard labour was passed on the woman, and two nac,a.tha' hard labour on the man.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB REPORT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FOOTBALL CLUB REPORT.</p>
        <p>The directors of the Runcorn Football Club, Limited, Issued their second annual report on Monday. They state that the performances of both teams throughout the season were more pleasing than for some time past, and the very great improvement at the latter end of the season gave great promise. The Works Competition was a great success. The Schoolboy Competition was not so good as anticipated owing to the had weather. The new ground gives promhe of being in excellent, condition when they open the season on September 6th with a visit from tit. Helens. None of the present directors retire this year. Tha statement of accounts shows an income of £1,098 2s. Id., which included .C 703 9s. 11d, gate receipts, part gate receipts £lO2 13s. Bd., special subscriptions £7l 19s. 9d, York Club guarantee £l5, works competi-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Loss of Appetite.</head>
        <p>Loss of Appetite.</p>
        <p>Loss of appetite is usually traceable to some form of stomach or liver trouble, and may be taken as a sign that the digestive system is in need of a stimulating tonic. When you can't cat, or when food is distasteful, just try the effect of taking Mother Seigel's Syrup after your meals every day. This splendid herbal remedy tones up and strengthens the digestive organs, sharpens the appetite, and regulates the whole system. Then you not only eat your food with a relish, but, more impor- tant still, the food you eat enriches your blood, nourishes your body, increases your strength and vitality, and makes you look wells feel well, aud keep well. _ .</p>
        <p>ticns .C 63 Gs. 5d., schoolboy competition £9 125., insurance company £3O, season tickets £B3 Os. 6d., and sports receirns £lB 9s. 10d. On the eebit side appeared players' and trainers' wages £353 18s. lid., travelling and hotel expenses £196 He. 7d., works competitions expenditure £33 3s. id., and schoolboy competitions £l5 6s. 11d. On the year's working a profit of a 198. Bd. was realised. THE " DUMB " SPEAK. IMPOSTER UNMASKED IN COURT.</p>
        <p>A sensation was created in the Runcorn Police Court. on Tuesday, when Serge-tnt Claque was asking questions it the deaf and dumb language of James Blades, of Bi.-kenhead, to ascertain wLether he was guilty or not of begging in Vine-street, Runcorn, the accused surprising the court by opening his mouth and speaking out loudly, admitting the offence. kkillully had he carried out the deception that the Clerk wrote on a piece of paper, and was about to hand it to the prisoner when the incident happened. The note read as follows : You are charged with wandering abroad and begging alms in Vine•street, Runcorn, at 430 p.m. eon the 23rd June, 1913 Do you plead guilty or not? Constable, Collier, who apprehended prisoner, raid be was going from house to house and handing a letter ' to the people. He raw him receive several coppers from pertons. ' The Clerk : What have you got to say, Blades ? , The Chairman: It is stated here on the charge sheet ; that thit man is deaf end dumb.</p>
        <p>The Clerk : Yes, air. The police were led to believe that, but the prisoner has rccovt rcd his speech this morning since he entered the dock. Mr, Johrron then rend the following remarkable letter, which the constable said he was banding to people to !Tad :</p>
        <p>" Kindly try your best to help me. lam a poor orphan, born deaf and dumb. I am a good shoe- maker, and sheps. labourer, only I had to leave my work on eccount of my eyes, for I have lost my left eye. lam only twenty-six years of ego, and I can prove myself honest and true, and if anyone here thinks lam not true, let them please tell me, and I will prove myself while I am in his presence._</p>
        <p>Prisoner said he wanted to speak his mind and get it done with. lie was guilty. sergeant Clague said prisoner told him that morning, in the deaf and dumb language, that he bad been to a ' deaf and dumb institute for thirteen years, but witness suspected something, for his alphabet was very slow and poor. He admitted having been convicted for larceny on three previe us occasions. The police were led to believe by his actions that he was deaf and dumb right up to the time he entered the dock that morning. It was proved prisoner was not blind, and his speech was in no way affected. The Cha'rman : You must go to gaol for a month's hard labour. 11Iy colleague and myself are only sorry we cannot give you more. Prisoner (sarcastically) : I am not particular. I Can't get work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ENTRANCE SCHOLARSHIPS.</p>
        <p>Ile St. Helens Education Committee scholatships, value £0 per annum fnr three years, tenable at Liver- pool University, hns been awarded to William Arthur Jackson and Malcolm Jones, both of the Cowley Boys' School, St. Helens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>DEPUTY SURVEYOR'S NEW APPOINTMENT.</head>
        <p>DEPUTY SURVEYOR'S NEW APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Mead, deputy surveyor of St. Helens, has been appointed borough engineer of Ipswich, at a salary starting at £350 and rising to £5OO a3car.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS POISONING SCARE.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens on Tuesday, Mr S. Brighouse, county coroner, concluded the inquiry into the circumstances attending the death of Sarah t..4andford, the three-year- old child of Henry Sandford, of Dudley-street, who was supposed to have died as a result of ptomaine poisoning. The evidence showed that the child partook of some corned beef, and a post-mortem showed that death was due to severe gastro-enteritis set up by some irritant poison in the meat Twenty people had been affected. The jury returned a verdict of " Death from mis- adventure," and heH that no blame was attachable to any one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>FATAL COLLIERY ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>FATAL COLLIERY ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse held an icquest at St. Helena on Saturday, on the body of John Rattigan, aged thirty- nine years, of 6, Moss Nook, Sutton, who died in the St. Helens Hospital early on the previous Thursday morn- ing from a fractured spine, sustained in the Collins Green Colliery in August last. John James Walters, of Eirlestown, a haulage hand, said during the night of August 15th last he and the deceased were in the brow sending boxes of dirt up the pit, when he heard Rattigan shout "Oh! " Witness stopped the machine, and went down, lie found the deceased fastened under a box. Rattigan told witness that a full box got off the rails and when he tried to reach over the box to the signal wire to find the electric tell, his foot slipped on the rails, and the box caught him and pulled him some yards up the brow. Dr. Jackson said the man's spine was fractured, and there was little hope from the first. A verdict of "Accidental death " was returnel</p>
        <p>TOBACCO CULTIVATION IN NEW ZEALA.ND.</p>
        <p>Several attempts have been made to establish the tobacco plant at the New Zealand Government school of instruction at Ruakura, near Hamilton, and it appears from the last number of the " Journal of Agriculture "as if success had been achieved, some splendid specimens being almost ripe for harvesting. A tobacco expert, who inspected the plot, said that he had never seen better plants in the United States. Ile estimated the crop at 9 cwt. of dried leaf per acre,and valued it at le. Od. per lb.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, June 28, 1913,</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 28, 1913,</p>
        <p>ORFORD HALL.</p>
        <p>Its Unique Place in the Educational</p>
        <p>World.</p>
        <p>CERTIFICATED TEACHERS' COLLEGE.</p>
        <p>Those visitors who attended the annual prize-giving and re-union at the Orford Pall Training College on Saturday afternoon, could hardly resist feeling envious of the students whose good fortune it is to spend a year amid such charming surroundings. As all Warringtonians know, the corege was formerly the residence of a distinguished townsman—William Bearnont, first Mayor of Warrington, local historian and antiquary. It stands in well-wooded grour de which, on Saturday, were looking their best in their wealth of rich greenery. OrLrd Hall the only college in the country set apart entirely for untrained certificated teachers who, after a few years' practical work in the schools, desire to underg r a college course of ore year. It is fulfilling a long-felt want in this part of the country, and those who have availed themselves of its advantnges speak in glowing terms of the resuPs. At the prize-giving on Saturday, the Principal (Canon Morley Stevenson) presided, and was supported by Professor Campagnae (Professor of Education at the University of Liverpool), who dis- tributed the awards, the Ven. Archdeacon Spconer, the Revs. W. Bendy Cock, G. Vale Owen and Mr. W. Mason.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>NEW REGULATION.</head>
        <p>A NEW REGULATION.</p>
        <p>The PRINCIPAL said he regretted that their numbers during the past year had 11,A been so large as in the preceding twelve months. That was greatly due to a new regulation which the Board of Education bad passed, obliging all students to serve for not less then two years after taking their certifinate before they entered a college. The regulation was a very wise one, but temporarily it had a diminishing influence upon their numbers. They had every reason to believe that the numbers would be larger text year; indeed, they had had more acceptances for next year than there were students at present in the college. One advantage in having a small number was that they had been able to deal with the students individually, and if that had not been E 0 well for the college financially,it had been.better for the students intellectually. Especially had that been the case, from what Mr. Mason had told him, in their work in the practising school. In all other respects the work of the college had gone on very happily and successfully. The report of the Government inspector stated that the work in the schools was well organised and supervised, and that the students were unanimoin in their opinion that the training was very helpful. (Applause.) Having tendered hi thanks to the staff, the Principal expressed regret that they were about to lose the services of Miss Selby, the lady superin- tendert, who was going to a larger and greater sphere of work in Manchester. They were gratetul to her for the kindly interest she had shown in the studen:s, and for the efficient way in which sha had managed the household. (Applause.) A UNIQUE INSTITUTION.</p>
        <p>The college, continued the Principal, was a unique institution. It was the only one of its kind in the kingdom. Students tuch as entered it entered other colleges, but that was the only college which laid itself out entirely for students who had passed the certificate examination and who, after a certain amount of work in the richools,felt they would like a year's training, lie felt sure that, from every point of view, that was a most wise course for them to take, lie thought those students vho had undergone it, would agree that the year's training had bent flted them physically. It Lad been a year of rest from teaching, of change of occupation, variety of work,and he thought they would return to their schools refreshed and strengthened. They would have a wider outlook, for they had been brought into touch with new and varied educational methods,and the intercourse with each other, and with others, v. ould have been for those who, in many cases, had led solitary lives, a very great help and incentive. The purpose and work of the college was a very useful one, and he felt certain that, when it became better known, they woul4 have no difficulty in filling it every year. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>HOPES THAT SUGGEST DESPAIR.</head>
        <p>HOPES THAT SUGGEST DESPAIR.</p>
        <p>Professor CAMPAGNAC, in the course of a helpful address, referred to the danger which beset the busy practitioner in every profession of falling behind the best thought and work of his time. He might be busily successful, or busy without being successful, but the routine occupation of the day absorbed him. He did what he could, well or ill, and he had hardly time to refitct upon what other people were doing and thinking, still less time for that profounder reflection h'ch involved the unifying of thought with action, of ideals with conduct and purpose. The charm and promie of such a college as that, was that it provided for people who had already had some experience in Leeching, an opportunity of standing aside for a year from the busy activities of their profession, so that they might review both it and themselves, so that they might see what it called for from them, and—a more difficult and alarming vision—what they bad to offer -to it. Speaking of the value of practical teaching, under supervision end criticism, the Professor said that the eftect would probably be, in some ins ances, to cause them to revise their practice, to make it something wholly or pa-tly different from what it was before. Those who had spent a year in such pursuits, and in such pleasant sot rounclings, were very much to be con- gratulated. Ile hoped they would not regard it as altogether impertinent of him to wish them well for the future. lie imagined that the year they had passed there had put before them certain hopes which some- times they felt were almost a mockery, hopes so high that they could hardly venture to believe they would be fulfilled. They had seen, iu one department of their work, what an elaborate acd intricate analysis of the human mind had been made, day after day, and with increasing amplitude and exactness, by psychologists. They had read something of ethics and morals. They had made FOGIq, studies in the history of education and had reviewed rapidly, but, he hoped, usefully, the great progress of educators and of education in times Fast. It was they who had to take up that great burden of knowledge, who had to accept that great inheritance of work done and to be done, and they might very well falter before such a task. Their high hopes might suggest despair, because of their very grandeur and nobility. It was, however, proper that, from time to time, there should be such feelings as those entering into one's misel. The man who felt he could lightly and easily do his work might be a strong man, but ho would certainly be a vain man, and it was very probable that he would have over-estimated his OK n powers as much as he had under-estimated his opportunities. It they thought highly of the work they had to do, they would probably think, not meanly, but asith humility, of themselves. Without wishing to press that point upon them, unduly, he suggested that such a feeling might be used and turned to something more productive and more active. THE TWO TYPES OF TEACHING.</p>
        <p>The Professor drew a suggsstive picture, from a recent experience s, bile sailing into a Spanish harbour, of the manner in which dolphins and gulls, apparently ssithout effort, were able to keep race with the move- ment of the vessel. It had been suggested that those creatures had the power, unknoesn to and unexplored by us, of drawing into themselves, from the air and the water, elemental forces of which our knowledge gave us no evidence ; that they moved, not because of power of their own, but because of the capacity they had for drawing into their own service powers altogether exceeding and transcending any they possessed. With- out discussing the scientific poEsibilities of that theory, be would endeavour to apply it to life in this world. People, neither better nor worse tlai ourselves, people with obvious imperfections and weaknesses, and people with limited experience,were different from us because more than ourselves, they were able to draw into their own lives, forces, elemental and divine, 'which made them insuperable, invincible and untiring. There were only two types of teaching—the humdrum tesching of those who merely earned a living and the teaching which had greatnesg in it. That they should hope to attain greatness in their work was quite compatible with the sincerest humility, and be hoped that that greatness, which was inspired by the forces to which he had alluded, would be theirs. (Applause.) A PRINCIPAL WITHOUT PARALLEL.</p>
        <p>Archdeacon SPOONER, in proposing a vote of thanks to Professor Cdmpagnac, said he had been much impressed with the pbssical effects produced by the surroundings of Orford Hall, and by the evidence that the students deeply appreciated the curriculum and the influence of those who had been appointed to carry on the teaching. They were anxious that the number of students should reach the maximum of thirty. They had a principal without parallel throughout the length and breadth of England—(applause)—and the surround- ;rigs were more than they could possibly expect in the immediate neighbcuthood of IVarrington. (Laughter and applause.) The Rev. HENDY COCK seconded. Speaking from his experiences as a tutor at the college, he said that in the whole course of his educational work he had never seen so much expansion and development in twelve months as had characterised the work of the students at Orford Hall. (Applause.) The vote was heartily passed. The company then ' partook of tea in the grounds. Opportunity was also afforded of inspecting some very creditable specimens of handwork done by the students.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>Divinity: F. E. Lee. Practical Teaching : Jessie Phillips. English : E. A. Bowman. History : E. A. Bowmen. Arithmetic : 1, L. N. quinion ; 2, Jessie Phillips.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>FALSE PROPHETS.</head>
        <p>FALSE PROPHETS.</p>
        <p>How Tory Predictions Were Falsified by Events.</p>
        <p>THE LAND CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>Councillor Middleton, of Oldham, is well-known to local Progressives, and it is little wonder that, when he was announced to address the weekly meeting of the Progressive Labour League at Bridge Foot on Monday, a large crowd assembled round the van. A forceful and homely speaker, the Councillor is no mean humorist, and during the course of his speech he relates many p inted stories.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>TORIES' I\EW LAND POLICY.</head>
        <p>THE TORIES' I\EW LAND POLICY.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. W. JAM ES, who presided, sr oke of the splendid work the Liberal Party had done for the masses of the country. The Insurance Act was one of the most recent examples of how the Liberal Party were lookivg after the interests of the people. He wondered what- ever the Tory Party would do with regard to the Insurance Act, if the impossible did happen and they were returned to power. Ile wondered if they would try to damage the Act ; whether they v.ould try to amend it to such an extent that it would be of no service to the working-classes. The Tories opposed any measure the Government sought fit to bring tormard for the well-being of the workers. He saw on the placards for that day that the Tory party were talking about in; reducing a land policy. Why would they persist in baffling the people with such talk ? hey tried to make the people believe that they mould gi‘e them some of the land. (Laughter.) But such a suggestion from awls a source would not be credited. Ile could not understand why the Tory party should oppose all the measures put forward by the Liberal Governmcnt. (A Voice : " They want to get ir.") " That is it," added Mr. James," they want to gain office. They did not care to wbat depths they stooped so long as they gained office. If the Tory party got into power they knew that, whilst they told the people that Tariff Reform " was not going to be introduced until it had been submitted to the country, they had had dealings with the Tory party in the past and their previous record was not such as to inspire any confl fence. He would remind them of the old proverb, "Once bitten, twice shy." In conclusion, be hoped they would not forget that we had in Warrington a man who was worthy to represent a constituency like this—(hear, hear)—and he hoped they would all do their best to turn the present member out and elect a man who was thoroughly entitled to the support of the workers. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>PROPHECIES THAT WENT WRONG.</head>
        <p>PROPHECIES THAT WENT WRONG.</p>
        <p>Councillor MIDDLETON, bsfore dealing with the politics that were interesting our minds at the present moment, said he would like to remind all present of two astonishing facts. One was that they had been living for 7i years under Liberal Government, and the other was that they were living yet. (Laughter.) Continuing, he said, the Liberal party, had done, were doing, and would continue to do all they could for the working-lasses of the country. Proving that the pro- phecies of the Conservative party were no prophecies at all, he said that the last eight years had been notable inasmuch as they had been boom years. Thy had gone on improving in such a way that from a trade point of view, there was no reason why we should either change our Government or our policy. They knew quite well that Free Trade was the one thing for this country, and no they could d smiss Mr. Chamberlain and his prophecies with regard to our trade—(hear, hear)—and they could rejoice that during the last seven and a half scars they had had that glorious record. When the Liberal Government began to indicate the kind of legislation they were going to put forward, Lord Rosebery eaid, "fhis means the end of all things." That meant the end of the world,and when a man commenced to prophecy the end of the world, it was time he died. (Laughter.) The end of all things had not come ; they had got a good deal of legislation and other things that the Opposition said were going to bring about the end of all things, but the people had been left with more of everything, and he was quite willing to go on in that fashion. Then Mr. Lloyd George came along—(applause)—and it was at a very awkward time. He was able to stand up to Mr. Joseph Chamberlain, which no one else could do. The Liberal party took Mr. Lloyd George up and be had proved his statesmanship. THE CHANCELLOR'S COURAGE.</p>
        <p>After relating the incidents which led up to Mr. Lloyd George being appointed Chancellor of the Exchequer, the six aker said he took hold ot the finances of the country when the one thing needful was money. Mr. Lloyd George carried the work through because he had resourcefulness, ability and courage. He also brought to bear a set of brains equal to any other set in the world. Not only had he all those qualifications, but he had also the desire to use them in the service of the common people, and when they got a man with such good points they had a splendid combination which ought to carry any man through. He raised the money from those who had it. He set about realising the things we had been taught ta hope for—a better equality of taxation such as the graduated income tax and the super-tax, and provided means to carry out his great purposes. "THE LAW AND THE PROPHErS."</p>
        <p>So long as Mr. Boner Law led the Tory party it was very hopeful for the Liberals. He was the man who had heard that " the law and the prophets" went together, and he, being named Law,thought he ought to be a prophet. (Laughter.) He said the insuranc3 Act would never come into operation. Was not that prophecy great? (Laughter.) A wonderful prophecy, seeing that he was a Law and a prophet. (Laughter.) On 'Lon try 15th, the Act came into operation, and the leader of the Tory party would no doubt be seen stamp- ing his washer woman's card. (Laughter.) He would then know that Ha prophecy hal gone wrong. (Laughter.) Next, Mr. Bonar Law said there would be no benefits. On the same day that payments commenced one very important benefit came in. Those who had one stamp on their card were entitled to Sanatoria benefits and within a fortnight of the Act coming in there were hundreds of people receiving that benefit which they weft) told would never come. After that he was convinced that it was high time the Tories gave up the line of prophecy. (Hear, hear.) Then, again, their prophecies went wrong in regard to the doctors, and he believed they would soon be as eager as anyone to make the Act a still further suc3eBs. As long as the Liberal party were carrying out the wishes of the people it was quite certain that they would go on with their great work. TORIES' DASTARDLY ATTACKS.</p>
        <p>Referring to the dastardly attacks made on the character of the Chancellor, the Councillor said the Tory party hit Mr. Lloyd George because of the way in which he had tried to carry out the wishes of the people. Instead of destroying the man one man had had to pay £l,OOO. In Criccieth an institute was being presented to the village, and £l,OOO of the cost of it was the money received from the newspaper for that libel. He (the Councillor) thought that Mr. Lloyd George would feel exultant that he was able to give Tory money to his own village. Mr... Lloyd George was the man the Tories bad been aiming at all the time. Challenged to produce his bank book and private papers, he did so and told them everything. The Tories had also said that he had gone on buying shares and selling them, making a profit of £lOO,OOO. He proved to them that it was a great deal less than that sum. (Hear, hear.) After snob unjust procedure, they said in the House of Commons that he acted in a straightforward manner, and that there w3s no imputation on his personal honour or character. Lord Robert Cecil, however, said, "Oh ! but I could make a worse report if I had a mind." Now he said that the evidence he had did not justify him in making a worse report.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>MEN OF HONOUR.</head>
        <p>MEN OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>There was not the least doubt about it, that from now to the general election, which he prophesied for 1915, they would ba followe 1 by those tactics and have to fight them all along. Certainly their men were liable to make mistakes ; but who did not make them ?—the people who never did anything. The man who did the most was the most liable to make a mistake They were, however, gl,tcl of the decision of the House of Commons, because it showed that their man was a man of honour and worthy of their support and confidence. The Tories could say what they liked, but they, as Liberals and Progressives, believed that their men were prepared to continue the work they had been doing for years past on behalf of the people of the country. They would soon discover that when Mr. Lloyd George began to move, there would be a better movement amongst the people of the country in his favour. Let him start his land campaign, and it would not be received as coolly as Lord Lansdowne's was on Saturday, at Matlock Bath. They, as Liberals, had nothing to feel sorry for, and no reason to withdraw their con- fidence from these men. There was not a single man in Mr. Aequith's Cabinet who bad a directorship of a com- pany iuterested in Government contracts. In Mr. Hallows' Government there were ten ministers who hold amongst them fifteen directorships, Then such people could come along and talk as they bad done with that record behind them. It was the greatest piece of check he had known in politics. When the next election came he lnd not the least doubt that Warrington would remedy the mistake it made at the last election and put itself on the side of Liberal government and general progress. (Applause.) Votes of thanks concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>-, • - . . • ~.. ~ . • ... , . - -I • .. . .. t _ eats a Mies exterminated by DANYSZ VIRUS. •. Non-poisonotte and barmier le Human Being; Denmatto and other Animal*. Single Tube 21-I $ Taber 21-1 poet free front : DANYSZ VIRUS LIMITED, (Rot 216 ). Os Leadenhall start, London, and of al/ Chemist&amp;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <p>FIGHTING CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>£57,000 a Year for Medical Research.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>COMMITTEE APPOINTED.</head>
        <p>COMMITTEE APPOINTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd George, as Minister responsible to Parliament for National Health Insurance, has appointed the following persons as a C,,rrunittee, with executive functions, to be known as the Medical Rt search Committee, fer the purpose of dealing with the money made available for research under the Insurance Act:</p>
        <p>Lord Moulton of Bank, LL.D , F.R.S. (chlirmln).</p>
        <p>Christopher Addison, M.D., F.R.C.S., M.P.</p>
        <p>Waldorf Astor. M.P.</p>
        <p>Sir T. Clifford Allbutt, K.C.8., M.D., F.R.C.P., F.R.S. Regius Professor of Physic, University of Cambridge. Charles John Bond, F.R.C.S., Senior Honorary Surgeon, Leicester Infirmary. William Bullock, M.D., F.R.S., Bacteriologist to the London Hospital and Prof. of Bacteriology in the University of London. Matthew Hay, M.D., L.L.D., Prof. of Forensic Medicine and Public Health, Aberdeen University. Frederick Gowland Hopkins, MB., D.Sc., F.R.S., Reader in Chemical Ph)siology in the University of Cam bridge. Brevet-Colonel Sir William Bog Leilhman, MB., F.R.S., Prof. of Pathology, Royal Army Medical College. _</p>
        <p>These first appointments are for three years in each case ; in and after 1916 three members shall retire at intei vale of two years, their places being filled (whether by reappointment or otherwise) by the Minister responsible. The Committ3e will formulate the general plan of research and inquiry for each year, make arrange- ments for carrying it out, and will also deal with the collection and publication of information. For this purpose it will determine the expenditure of the money available each year, the total of thd sums available being about £57,000 per annum. Before the Minister responsible for insurance gives his final assent to the Medical Research Com- mittee's scheme for any year, he will receive criticisms and suggestions in regard to it from the Advisory Council for Medical Research.</p>
        <p>The Advisory Council of 41 members (including the Chairman, Lord Moulton of Bank), has been appointed by Mr. Lloyd George, after receiving suggestions for suitable names from each of the Universities, Royal Colleges of Physicians and of Surgeons, Royal Society, and other imprtant bodies. After 1916 one-third will retire at intervals of two years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>NOT AN ISOLATED CASE.</head>
        <p>NOT AN ISOLATED CASE.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn woman's story given here is not an isolated case ',-,y any means; week after week, year after year, our neighbours are telling similar good news.</p>
        <p>"I was left "in a very weak state after a confine- ment," says Mrs. E. Hankers, of 5, Bridgewater-street, close to All Saints' Church, Runcorn. 'There seemed to be no streegth in my back, and I suffered with a continuous, burning pain over my kidneys. When I stooped it was just as if a knife were being driven into, my back. "My rest was disturbed and my nights were one round of misery to me. I was tired and weary, and used to come over BO dizzy that I was almost afraid of falling. "The flow from the kidneys was difficult and unnatural; one of my ankles swelled up with water, and if I touched it the impression was some moments going away. "I was recommended to try Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, and to my great delight I soon found they were doing me good. The pains gradually went away, a free and natural action was restored to the kidneys, and the swelling in my ankle disappeared. By the time I had taken three boxes of the pills my back and kidneys were thoroughly. strengthened. I take a few of the pills now and again if I feel out of sorts—they are indeed a splendid medicine. (Signed) (Mrs) E. Hankers." Price 2s. 9d. a box, (3 boxes 13s. 9d. ; °NH dealers, or from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxfsrd. street, London, W. Don't ask for backache and kidney pills—ask distinctly for 1)OCIll'8 Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mrs. Hankers had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>BY-ELECTION FRAUD.</head>
        <p>BY-ELECTION FRAUD.</p>
        <p>"Bringing up More Workers"</p>
        <p>at Altrincham,</p>
        <p>At Lancaster on Monday,a young man named Richard Hurst (21), of Zebra-street, Derby-street, Salford, was sent to prison for one month with hard labour for obtaining food by false pretences. Chief Constable Harms stated that since leaving school the prisoner had been of a restless disposition and had never kept any situation more than a few months. Last month he represented himself to Mr. Coates, proprietor of the Essex-stkeet Garage, Salfordy as being employed by the Altrincham Unionist Election Committee to visit various towns in Lancashire, Cheshire, and Yorkshire "to bring up more workers." On this pretence he was allowed the use of motor-cars, and also borrowed money from Mr. Coates, who esti- mated his losses at £l7O. The prisoner also obtained £2 worth cf eatables from Mrs. Wolstenholme, a Salford confectioner, on a similar pretence. In neither case, however, would the defrauded party prosecute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>SOCIAL WORK IN LIVERPOOL'S POVERTY</head>
        <p>SOCIAL WORK IN LIVERPOOL'S POVERTY</p>
        <p>LAND.</p>
        <p>There must be very few keen social workers who have not heard of the Liverpool League of Welldoers which does such a noble work among the poor of that great city. Its founder and head is Mr. U. Lee J. Jones, who twenty years ago left his comfortable home at Cressington and commenced his work ,in a modest building in slum-surrounded Limekiln-lane. There he lived, and there he has lived for twenty years (up to May 23rd, 1913), and still lives, whilst throughout all these years of toil, with the exception of one night (and excluding the two months he was ordered away for health), he has cccupied a bed• room Spartan in character at the League's headquarters in Limekiln-lane. The heart of Liverpool's poverty-land has claimed him. The poor, their straggles, difficulties temptations, virtues and vices, no man knows better than he. Never blinded to their shortcomings, he has devoted his life to minister to their necessities, and in turn directed attention to the great problem which poverty in all its phases presents. During the whole period he has not received one farthing salary. On the contrary, he at present, in addition to having given his life for the poor, stands to lose £6OO of his small capital, pledged to the Bank for the work! It is inconceivable that wealthy Liverpool will allow a loss of this nature to such a benefactor and there should be an immediate and generous response to the appeal which has gone forth. To-day Ur. Tice Jones's work is not only known to the citizens of Liverpool, but throughout the country, and copied in many English towns, in Germany, and the United States. The League of Welldoers is really 16 charities in one,and is noted for its delicacy of method in dealing with the worthy poor (high as well as low), for whom it is alone solicitous. Those who work with Mr. Lee Jones do so for a pittance in money, with quite simple food and lodgings, his contention being that those who work for the poor should live for them not on them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>AUSTRALIAN PROGRESS.</head>
        <p>AUSTRALIAN PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>During two years the area under cultivation for wheat in Australia has increased by two million acres. In ten years the yield of butter has increased by 92 per cent. Half the annual yield of 193,000,00011)s. is exported.</p>
        <p>MEI</p>
      </div>
