        <head>BRIDE AND BRIDEGROOM,</head>
        <p>THE BRIDE AND BRIDEGROOM,</p>
        <p>A large congregation assembled in the Primitive Methcdist Church, Legh-street, on Saturday, on the occasion of an exceptionally pretty wedding. The bridegroom was Mr. , John Lionel Millett, son of the late Mr. and Mrs. J. M. Millett, of Warrington, and the bride Miss Edith Walters Elwell, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. T. Elwell, 76, Orford-avenue, \Warrington. The officiating ministers were the Revs. J. B. Buglass and W. Cooper. The hymns "Since Jesus freely did appear to grace a marriage feast" and "0 love divine and golden" were sung. Mr. F. Banner was at the organ and played Mendelssolm's Wedding March as the bridal party left the church. The bride, who was given away by her father,was charmingly attired in duchess° mousseline and carried a bouquet of white lilies and roses. Tier ornament was a gold bangle, the gif tof the bridegroom. The bridesmaids were Miss Annie Sankey (cousin of the bridegroom) and the Misses Eva Lear and Edith Elwell (cousins of the bride). Their dresses were of saxe blue silk and their bouquets con- sisted of sweet peas. They- wore gold brooches, the gifts of the bridegroom. The reception was held in the Legh-street schoolroom, over eighty guests attend- ing. The newly-married couple departed for London, where the honeymoon is being spent, the bride travelling in a navy blue costume.</p>
        <p>Mr. rind Mrs. Walter Dennett, silver-mounted msrmalade jar and spoon Mr. tv,cl :,Irs. L. Jones, pair of pictures Mr. nal Mrs. \. Taylor, teapot and stand Mr. Dewar and Miss Brown, serviettes Miss CartPr, silver jam dish Mrs. and Miss Dearden, Corinthisn ware fruit eish Mrs. and Miss E. Jones, handkerchief and glove case Mr. and Mrs. Cowap, rose howl Mrs. Schofield and family, flower and fruit stand Mr. Sumner, fruit dish Mr'. and Mrs. J. Leo, clock Mr. and Mrs. Lear and family, household linen Mr. and Mrs. Baggott (Ilkeston cheque Miss Batty (London), silver candlesticks Miss C. Sorton, cushion cover</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "The Midnight Mail."</p>
        <p>This sensational domestic drama has been presented before large audiences at the Royal Court Theatre, and what there may be lacking in plot is compensated for by the number of exciting and thrilling incidents. The main object of the play seems to be to illustrate the curse of drink, and in that respect the effort is strikingly successful, Harry Steyne. the newly-married engine driver, is living happily with his wife until Billy Brewer, a drunken reprobate, comes along and drags Steyne from the straight path. He goes from bad to worse, but finally is able to retrieve his lost character and join his wife in the world of happiness. The play is cleverly acted by Dottridge, Longden, and Liell's company. Mr. Albert Sinclair is the typical Harry Steyne, the burly engine driver with a large heart, but a week will, and he puts plenty of vigour into the part. Mr. Bernard Liell acts remarkably well as Billy Brewer, the drunken stoker, making the part of the tempter and bully as repulsive as possible, at the same time aiding in the comedy of the produc- tion. Miss Georgette Thiery is well placed as Mary Steyne, and acts in such a manner as quickly to gain the sympathies of the audience. Miss Jeannie Weston is successful as the capricious "Nancy " and helps to keep the ball of humour rolling. Mr. Edward Vivian is a capital llassan Padisha, and Mr. Charles H. Longden could not be better placed than in the part of Leonard Poyle, the generous railway manager. The other characters are also well sustained. The scenic effects are carried out on a lavish scale, especially in the last scene where we see a full sized engine, supposed to be travelling at GO miles per hour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Next week Mr. George Edwin Clive will present Miss Lilian Aubrey's company in " Llor One False Step."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Florrie Forde has long been a strong favourite on the music hall stage, and this week at the Hippodrome large " houses " have accorded this great chorus singer a hearty welcome. She has a captivating way of rendering the catchiest of songs, and she has ample scope in her two breezy numbers, " Hold your hand rut, naughty boy," and " Dance your troubles away." Her song about the seaside was also very popular. " The White Knight" is the title of an operatic novelty presented by Harry Claff and Miss Alice Tremayne. The setting to the scene is beautiful, and Claff looks exceedingly well in his spangle and white</p>
        <p>suit of mail. He has a good, full-toned voice, which is heard to advantage in A. thousand years agp," and other numbers. Miss Tremayne assists in the har- monious tit-bits in a pleasing manner. Dawn, is a clever trick cyclist, and performs the most daring tricks in a very nonchalant way. Parkes and Price are expert instrumentalists, and on Monday night were warmly encored. They play numerous instru- ments in a really clever and pleasing manner, always having a special regard to the refined. Hillier and Haines score a decided success as comedians, with an abundance of humorous patter and good stories. The couple also step dance remarkably well. The Two Britons are fine specimens of the British athlete, and perform many novel and interesting feats of strength. Jackley and Le Sine, are burlesque comedians and tumblers, and have been highly successful in " The interfering page boy." " Three suitors and a dog " is a laaghable film shown on the cinema.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. The programme for next week will include George D'Albert, light comedian ; Gautier's Animated Toy Shop ; Signor Torti, Italian tenor; The Mars Trio, pedestal comedy acrobats; Victor Wakeman, humorist and mimic ; Hal Bert, the elastic comedian ; Ellerslie Pyne in " Too Late" or "An Actor's Dream"; and It ene Armitage, charming vocalist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>PEcCollom—Boardman.</head>
        <p>PEcCollom—Boardman.</p>
        <p>A very pretty wedding was solemnized at the Parish Church on Wednesday, the contracting parties being Miss Clara Boardman, the youngest daughter of Mr. and Mrs. J. J. I3oardman and Mr. Herbert Hope McCollom, only son of Mr. and Mrs. William McCollom. The bride and bridegroom have for many years been connected with St. Clement's Mission, and there was a good attendance at the wedding. The Rev. W. J. Buckmaster officiated. The bride looked very pretty in a dress of chamois silk with a wreath of orangf, blossom. She carried a bouque• of carnations and liliies, the gift of the bridegroom. The bridesmaids were Miss Beatrice Dunne and Miss Elsie Boardman (cousin of the bride). They carried bouquets of roses and carnations, and wore gold brooches, the gifts of the bridegroom. Mr. P. F. Boardman was the best man. Mr. Dell, organist at St. Clement's Mission, officiated at the organ, and played suitable music, including Meridelssohn's Wedding March. A reception was afterwards held at the house of the bride's parents. Later in the day the happy couple left for the Isle of Man via Heysham, where the I honeymoon will be spent. The bride's travelling I dress was of grey whipcord, with hat to mateh. Many beautiful and valuable presents have been received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>THEATRE ROYAL. Opening of Picture Season.</head>
        <p>THEATRE ROYAL. Opening of Picture Season.</p>
        <p>The Royal Repertory Company have again been successful in "Her Love for Sailor Jack " this week, and large houses have been the rule. The various characters were well sustained, and the play bristles with interesting incidents. Mo,:day will see the opening of the picture season at this cosy place of amusement, and we are given to understand that only the best and finest films will be shown. There will be a complete change of pictures every Monday and Thursday. Pathos and comedy will be well blended in a pleasing manner. A special old yolks' treat will be given every Monday at 2.30, when a limited number, over GO years of age, will be admitted free of charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ANDREWS' PICTURES.</head>
        <p>ANDREWS' PICTURES.</p>
        <p>Those of our readers who have not yet seen the great dramatic film "In the clutches of the Apaches" should take the final opportunity to-day. It is full of ,exciting incidents and shows how a poor hunch-backed boy saves the little daughter of a French officer froal the clutches of the Apaches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>PICTUREDROME.</head>
        <p>PICTUREDROME.</p>
        <p>On Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday," The Eagle's claw," a unique film, in two parts, will be shown. Lovers cf Dickens will be especially interested to hear that for . the remaining days of the week the management have secured a picture representation of " Pickwick papers." It will be shown in two parts, depicting " The adventure of the honourable event," and " The adventure at the Westgate Seminary." The incidents are enacted in the Dicken's country, Mr. John Bunny taking the part of Mr. Pickwick.</p>
      </div>
