      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>JEktiLEE UILDINGS, 175; t SEMARKET STREET, '4',‘AIIIIINGTON.</head>
        <p>JEktiLEE UILDINGS, 175; t SEMARKET STREET, '4',‘AIIIIINGTON.</p>
        <p>Irti THE C•URTS</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MAN'S LEAP.</p>
        <p>Strange Case of Alleged Attempted Suicide.</p>
        <p>Peter Scully, a well-known character, appeared before Mr. R. T. Fairclough (presiding), Alderman Tinnion, Misers. A. J. Jolley and T. Pemberton, at the Borough Police Court on Saturday, on a charge of attempting to commit suicide by throwing himself into the river Mersey the previous Monday. Superintendent Williamson asked for a remand for a few days in order that the police could make inquiries with a view to deciding what was the best thing to do with the prisoner. He was charged with jumpiog from the Walton Arches into the river. At 9.15 on Monday eveniag, James Thomas Green, a telegraph messenger, was approaching the bridge when tae heard SOM9one say, "One, two, three and Igo !" Looking up he saw prisoner jump from the footpath into the river (a dis- tance of about 40 feet). The man dropped into nine inches of water and stuck in the mud. Green went into the mud up to his knees, and, with the assistance of a man named William Irons, got Scully out. Prisoner had been in the Workhouse Inflrmary ever since. The Bench granted a remand until yesterday. • Scully again appeared before the magistrates yesterday. In reply to the Mayor, Superintendent Williamson said there was no reason to suspect that prisoner was not sound mentally. Prisoner was committed for trial at the Liverpool Quarter Sessions. The magistrates complimented Green and awarded him X.l, the Meyor telling him that, in view of prisoner's record, it was to be regretted that the life he saved was not better. However, the most worthless life was con- sidered worthy of being-saved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>HUSBAND'S ALLEGATION.</head>
        <p>HUSBAND'S ALLEGATION.</p>
        <p>Maintenance Order Discharged at Warrington. At the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, before Councillor 11. Roberts (in the chair) and other mAgis- trates Robert Rigby, 9, Duncan-street, St. Helens, applied for the discharge of a maintenance order, made on April 18th, towards his wife, Nora Rigby, 11. Keen- street, Warrington. Mr. Kelly (St. Helens), who appeared for the appli- cant, said the discharge was applied for on the grounds that defendant had been living in adultery with a married man named Senior. The parties were married ii years ago, and there were two children. About 12, months ago Senior went to live with the Rigby's at Earlestown. They left there and came to live in Foster-street, Warrington. Owing to various incidents which Mr. Rigby noticed, his suspicious were aroused as to the relationship existing between his wife and Senior. He noticed on a number of occasions that after he had gone to bed his wife insisted on staying up whilst Senior was downstairs. His wife told him frankly that she was going to leave him and live with Senior, who intended to open a fish shop. She said she had lost her affection for her husband, who, however, was very patient with her, and was inclined</p>
        <p>to forgive her. He said that if she went with Senior again he would finish with her. Senior then left, and stayed with his own wife for a few days. Rigby and his wife went to live in lodgings in llostock- street. Senior came and stayed with them. After- wards they took a house at 9, Keen-street, Senior again living with them as a lodger. On March 26th, Rigby left his wife, and when he was going she told him she would not leave Senior' with whom she seemed infatuated. Senior lodged with her for six or seven weeks, and at the instance of the landlord they were compelled to leave the house. They went to lodge at 11, Keen-street, where there were only two bedrooms, and two rooms downstairs, and there were ten people living in the house. On April 18th, Mrs. Rigby got an order for 123. 6d. per week on the grounds of desertion, Rigby having left her in consequence of her conduct. He was willing to pay for the maintenance of the children, but he objected to paying his wife anything. There were a few arrears on the order.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rigby gave evidence supporting this statement. Mrs. Senior, 26, Earle-street, Earlestown, said her husband had taken furniture out of the house in order to buy Mrs. Rigby presents. She had had 23 years' happy married life before the other woman came along. A number of neighbours having given evidence as to Mrs. Rigby and Senior having been seen out together, defendant said she bad not lived with Senior. He slept on the couch and she slept in the back bedroom with her two children.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Royle, 11. Keen-street, corroborated defendant's statement. All the neighbours who had given evidence had been bribed. They went a drive on Monday, and they were having a teaparty that afternoon. She would have all the talk stopped. The magistrates discharged the order. Defendant will have to pay off the existing arrears and will be held responsible for the maintenance of the children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
