      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>IMPUDENT ROBBERY.</head>
        <p>IMPUDENT ROBBERY.</p>
        <p>Capture Effected Through Maid's Resourcefulness.</p>
        <p>The story of a daring robbery and smart capture was heard at the Warrington Police Court on ‘Vednesday, when William Henry Andrews, respectably dressed, of 14, Faulkner-street, Liverpool, was charged on remand with having stolen' on Saturday afternoon, a silver cup and electro-plated tray value £5, the property of Mr. F. C. Carton Stone, Wilderspool House, Wilderspool, Warrington. Prisoner pleaded guilty. The presiding magistrates were: Alderman Smethurst (in the chair), Mr. C. Jackson Holmes and Mr. W. T. Williams. The Chief Constable said prisoner had been remanded from Monday in order that inquiries could be made about him. The details of the theft were that at 5 o'clock on Saturday afternoon prisoner called at the house and asked to see Mr. Stone. He was informed by one of the maids that Mr. Stone was away from home. Prisoner then left, but returned in about half-an-hour and stated that be had been in communication with the brewery by telephone from Liverpool and that he I had been informed that Mr. Stone was at home, and that he had to be supplied with tea. A maid gave him tea in the dining-room. When he had finished he took the tea things back and gave them to another maid,and went out. bhortly efter he had left, the cup and tray were missing. One of the maids saw him walking along Greenall's-avenue. He appeared to have something under his coat. She obtained the assistance of a young man with whom she was "keeping company," and they got into a tramcar and followed the prisoner. A SMART CAPTURE. They went to tke Central Station, but could not find any trace of him. Information was given to the police, and Inspector Forster found the prisoner sitting near a book-stall at Bank Quay Stat:on. When questioned, prisoner refused to say what he was doing in Warring- ton. The Inspector discovered the stolen articles under prisoner's coat. Prisoner said he was taking the goods to the owner whom he knew vary well. He was charged and practically admitted the theft. He stated that he had known Mr. and Mrs. Stone for some years, and that he was in terrible trouble or he would not have taken the articles. Mr. Stone, who was in the Isle of Alan at the time, was communicated with and returned on Monday morning. He had no desire to press the charge. SERVED IN THE BOER WAR. The Liverpool police stated, that so far as they knew, prisoner had borne a very high character. He had been</p>
        <p>gir</p>
        <p>in a good position in life ; and had served with distinc- tion in the Boer War in the let Life Guards, from which regiment his discharge bad been purchased. He married n wealthy woman, who, how- ever, died shortly after the marriage. They understood that prisoner bad signed a contract to go back to the West Coast of Africa, where he had been for six years. Prisoner bad been hued 40s. or one month at Clerken- well for illegally pawning billiard balls, which were taken from a public-house. That might hare been a drunken freak. Mr. Stone hoped the Bench would deal as leniently as possible with the prisoner. It was only through the smartness of the maid that prisoner was captured.</p>
        <p>Prisoner said that the billiard ball affair was a drunken freak, because be had plenty of money at the time. He went to the pawnshop to get the balls out to return them to the owner, but was arrested. He married a very rich lady. He did not mean to steal the silver cn Saturday. for he intended to pay Mr. Stoi:e back. He was to sail for the Gold Coast again on July 9th, and would have received an advance from his firm on the 6th. He wcuid then have been able to refund the money to Mr. Stone. He did not mean to steal. After six years on the Gold Coast a man could not get work in England. He was a malarial subject. The Chairman addressed the prisoner at lengtb, putting before him the meanness of the theft. The maids gave him charity and he took the things off the sideboard. They commended the girl for her prompt action which led to the arrest. Prisoner was bound over under the First Offenders' Act.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>t a i 1 Q. 1 • An</head>
        <p>t a i 1 Q. 1 • An</p>
        <p>Borciugh Police Court.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor Roberts (in the chair), Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, T. Locker, W. Richardson and James Hepherd. AN ALLRGED ADVENTURER.—Patrick joseph Caffrey, grocer's assistant, 204, Orford-lane, was charged with having obtained food and lodging to the value of 30s. from Sarah Jones by false pretences.—The Chief Con- stable said prisoner had been in Warrington three or four weeks and had obtained money from various sources by saying he bad a private income of £2 frona a gentleman at Bray, Ireland. He was, however, an adventurer. Ho went to the house of prosecutrix and stated that the woman who was with him was his wife. That assertion was not true, and from a statement she made it was essential that the case should be adjourned. Prisoner was remanded for a week. TIIrRSDAY. Before Mr. W. Roberts (in the chair), Messrs. J. UPsou</p>
        <p>and W. T. Williams. AN ALLEGED ADVENTURER.—Patrick Joseph Caffrey and Nora O'Connor, a respectably dressed couple, were charged with stealing between the 17th and 21st June a suit of clothes, a quilt and a pair of opera glasses, value £1 Gs., the property of Sarah Jones, 20G, Orford- lane with whom they had lodged.—The male prisoner was on remand until Tuesday, on a charge of obtaining food and lodgings by false pretences.—The Chief Constable said the couple had obtained lodg- ings by falsely representing that they were married. The male prisoner stated that he was a commission agent and that he had private means. They had not paid Mr:. Jones any of the money agreed npon,and on Monday the male prisoner was apprehended. The girl left the district, und it was found that the articles had been pawned at different shops.—They were remanded until Thursday. Ix SACE.CLOTIL—When John llargreaves, of no fixed address, was arrested for drunkenness he became very disorderly in the police cell. All the movable articles had to be taken from him, and he then tore his trousers to shreds. He appeared before the magistrates arrayed in sackcloth.—He was sent to prison for one month.</p>
      </div>
