      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, June 28, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 28, 1913.</p>
        <p>The Passing Week.</p>
        <p>RATIONAL HOURS OF WORK.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield has a long and thoughtful article in yesterday's "Man- chester Guardian," in which he points out the wisdom of the establishment of shorter hours of labour. He attacks at the outset the erroneous idea that long hours of labour mean equivalent returns for capital, and says the" stupidity" of obliging men and women to work excessively low,- hours has been conclusively proved. Mr. Crosfield quotes actual experience, at home and abroad, in which a cutting down of hours to rational limits—generally taken to mean an eight hour day—has resulted in an actual increase of output, greater efficiency, and gain both in physical and moral directions. "The most economical production is obtained by employ- ing men only so long as they are at their best. When this stage is passed there is no true economy in their continued work." This quotation, from an undoubted authority, is put in the forefront and supported with all the force Mr. Crosfield has at his command. He promises to deal in a future article with those cases in which shorter hours would involve some appreciable increase in the total cost of production.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>ALEXANDRA DAY.</head>
        <p>ALEXANDRA DAY.</p>
        <p>Roses and lady workers for local charities will have complete possession of the streets to-day, and we sincerely trust they will take by storm all who are in a position to assist in the swelling of a fund that will be devoted to so many admirable objects. Veneration for an exemplary Queen, assistance for the distressed, employment for the cripple—and beautiful flowers in hand as a souvenir of a good act! Who, in theicircumstances, will resist the temptations to buy to-day?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>COUNCIL AND HEALTH.</head>
        <p>COUNCIL AND HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Time is becoming increasingly precious with the members of the Town Council. A month ago it was decided to call a special meeting to discuss the annual report of the Medical Officer of Health, an important document which deals with the vital concerns of the town, the means by which we live and thrive, and without which towns could not exist, and Town Councils would be in vain. Instead of having a full evening's discussion it has been decided to meet an hour earlier on the occasion of the monthly meeting on fuesday to consider the report, and, as members will wish to rise an hour earlier, it is probable that the adjournment from a month ago will prove to be a similar expedient to that of the man who, finding his blanket so short that it left his feet uncovered, cut a piece from the top and sewed it on to the bottom. But the Council will be fresh when they approach the report, which will be some- thing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>GREATER WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>GREATER WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>That coy damsel, Stockton Heath, is press- ing her suit in approved and orthodox fashion. Her request for a joint conference has met with an answer from Warrington which, she considers, does not constitute a. reply, and she says, point blank to her con- templated partner, that "until such joint conference is held" she is "not prepared to submit any proposals on the matter in question." Whereupon, the General Pur- poses Committee have decided that there must be a suspension of courting—wooing or holding at arm's length—until after the Royal Visit, when a special meeting will be held to consider the geperal question of the extension of the borthigh: This is a ques- tion, certainly, that should not be considered piecemeal. The time appears ripe for laying the foundations of that greater Warrington, which is bound to come into existence, and a great deal will depend upon the outlook that we take now. There are other places than Stockton Heath that have a natural connection with Warrington, and our claims upon them and theirs upon us should be considered in a complete scheme. We shall, of course, have to abolish the Stockton Heath amorous figure of speech if we seek to" take in" others, or our enemies may begin to upbraid us for municipal poly- gamy! SECONDARY SCHOOL SUCCESSES.</p>
        <p>Examination results in profusion figure in the pages of to-day's" Examiner." They form a reflection—a substantial and practical one—of the proportions of the excellent work now being performed at the Secondary School and its kindred schools, year in and year out. There are thirteen distinctions this year, and the first-class certificateslare in the neighbourhood of 200. Nor is this success theoretical and on paper only. At a recent meeting of the Education Committee, Canon Stevenson drew attention with evident satis- faction to a letter that had been received from the Postmaster in which he told them that those boys from the Commercial Institute classes who sat at the examination for ,boy learners at the Post Office had all done extremely well, and had qualified for those posts. It is gratifiying not only to the Committee, but to the town, to know that the schools run by the Committee are being so profitably conducted'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>CHILDREN'S SUMMER CAMP. At the Disposal of Poor People, Young</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S SUMMER CAMP. At the Disposal of Poor People, Young</p>
        <p>and Adult.</p>
        <p>[To THE EDITOR OF THE "EXAMINER."] am exceedingly obliged to you for yonr kindly recognition of the work of the Children's Camp Committee and of myself. Yon referred in such flattering terms to my share in the work as I never expected. It is true that I am, as you remark, a childless man, but even childless men can recognise the needs of their fellow creatures. I sincerely trust that the recognition you have made of the splendid results that have sprung from the labours of my committee will lead to a greater breadth of feeling being displayed in connection with such undertakings, and that, in years to come, when of necessity my work must be undertaken by someone else, increased usefulness and a still greater element of success will attend the camp movement. The camp is now ready, and the committee are anxious that the poor people of Warrington should accept in greater numbers than hitherto, the privi- leges which it affords. Those whose personal resources are PO limited that they cannot afford a holiday with its health-restoring attributes, are the people for whom the committee work ; of course, after the claims of the children have been satisfied by the reservation of the camp for their use at the end of July and the beginning of August. On behalf of the committee, I thank you for your sympathetic commendation of our efforts for the good of the poor children of our town. W. 11.3. JENKINS, Hon. Secretary. Warrington Cbildren's Summer Camp.</p>
        <p>Owing to pressure on our space, we were tumble to insert the above letter last week.—The Editor.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guilders at Lymm. Nearly two hundred members of the Guilds con- nected with the various Wesleyan Churches of the Warrington Circuit held their annual picnic to Lymm on Saturday afternoon. Games were enjoyed in Mr. Winstanley's farm field, and an excellent tea was provided in the Wesleyan sch oolroom by the member of the Lymm Guild and friends.</p>
        <p>For the holidays, DRESS BASKETS, TRUNKS, SUIT CASES, &amp;C.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53, sad 55, Bridge at. Warriagton.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>INSURANCE.</head>
        <p>INSURANCE.</p>
        <p>The improvements in the Insurance Act, which the Government promised to make as soon as they were seen to be necessary, have been proposed this week and will produce consternation in the ranks of the Opposition who will find half their occupation gone. Among the proposals in the amending Bill it is suggested that the contributions of the casual labourer shall be adjusted to the period during which he is employed; all insured persons are to receive full benefits up to the Old Age Pension limit; uninsured members of friendly societies, who joined before the Act, are to be credited with medical benefit of 2s. 6d. per head, like insured persons; there will be medical and sanatorium benefits for exempted persons; and arrears during unemployment are not to include employer's arrears. It was always the intention of the Government to do away with the "hard cases" and a real effort is to be made to carry out that intention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WAGE FACTS.</head>
        <p>THE WAGE FACTS.</p>
        <p>Another bubble has been pricked by the findings contained in a report on" Manufac- turing Costs," which the U.S.A. Bureau of Foreign and Domestic Commerce has just issued. In short, the finding of this Com- mittee is that the wage cost per unit of manufacture is considerably higher in Britain than in the United States; in other words, that a higher wage is paid for a unit of goods produced in this country than in America. "Not only," says the report, "as would be expected, are more labourers employed in the United Kingdom than in the United States to add by manufacture 1,000 dollars to the value of products, but also machinery of much greater capacity. In British factories nearly 18 per cent, more power and nearly two and one-half the number of labourers are required to obtain results, measured in the value added by manufacture. In the seven- teen selected industries the average wages in the United States were almost twice those in the United Kingdom, yet the total expendi- ture for wages was only 410 dollars in the United States as compared with 561 dollars in the United Kingdom for over y 1,000 dollars added by manufacture. Owing to the higher level of prices in the United States, the foregoing comparison, being based on values, is more favourable to the United States than would be one based on quantities, if such could be made. During the ten years that elapsed between the census of 1900 and 1910 there was a marked increase in the wages, mechanical power, and productivity of industry, compared with the number of wage earners. Notwithstanding the increase in wages in the United States, the tot al expenditure for wages,as well as the capacity of engines, has increased at a lower rate than the value, added by manufacture."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>AUSTRALIA'S EXPERIENCE OF PROTECTION.</head>
        <p>AUSTRALIA'S EXPERIENCE OF PROTECTION.</p>
        <p>The only information with any pretentions to definiteness which the Tariff Reformers have deigned to impart to an anxious com- munity, is;that the tariff they propose to set up, if and when they get the chance, is to average out at ten per cent. This tariff is to provide revenue for the needs of the State, and at the same time to afford protection to home manufacturers.</p>
        <p>It is interesting just now to consider these proposals in the light of Australia's experience of Protection. Australia has a schedule of duties ranging from 5 to 40 per cent., the general average being about 25 per cent. In spite of this protection, which is more than double the amount of protection which it is pro- posed to give the manufacturers of this coun- try, we find a leading Protection paper in the Commonwealth complaining that the tariff does not afford protection, but "per- mits such immense floods of foreign goods as to exceed importations by almost every other country in the world. Great Britain imports to the extent of some £l3 per head; Australia is now importing to the extent of about £l5 per head, and has to pay £14,000,000 a year interest besides, whilst Great Britain is entitled to receive £100,000,000 a year in interest on her foreign investments." Australian imports from 1902 to 1906 amounted to £199,600,000, and from 1907 to 1911 to £279,760,000, an increase of £80,160,000. "Importers," declares the Pro- tectionist authority , referred to, "have studied our tariff to some purpose; they have played the: game of dumping for all it is worth, and have flooded Australia with goods." And this in spite of an average tariff of 25 per cent.; yet we have been asked to believe that an average tariff of ten per cent. will put an end to " dumping " ! The same paper tells us that "while wages were rising and the cost of production was increas- ing, the tariff remained in statu quo. The consequence was obvious. It became cheaper to import than to manufacture"</p>
      </div>
