        <p>About thirty-six years ago -,o Crosfield organised their first fire brigac;re Mr. Gleave was placed in charge of it. were only five men in the brigade in,nla days, but they did good work with manual fire engine, and Mr. Gleave vivid recollection of fires which o attended at a brush works in Liverpooty and at a chemical works, now ovn,e`: Messrs. Pochin. To-day the brigade of ie'ffe six men has at its command two Wall/ engines and full life-saving equipment. Teetotaller and Non-Smoker. ut By dint of careful attention to his 'lbis Mr. Gleave gained the appreciation employers and was for many years of the soap warehouse. In May, 1904, r Crosfield recognised the worth of of their their oldest employes by a series i e"Tri P g sentations in the Parr Hall. The theZld. was presided over by Mr. A. H. Gr°s—nejor Mr. Gleave, who had then been in the eul'the of the firm for fifty-four years, vt'39„ he recipient of X 25. In his speech of thannth said his life had been wrapped UP the firm, for he was born on " premises and entered the servie,o, the firm when he was very er• The statement was literally true, for , Gleave's birthplace was a cottage in potte: r"to row—one of a block of property Whi,clljgrs• demolished to allow of the erection Crosfield's wharf warehouse. Mr. was actually a tenant under the fill° ow, forty-one years. On leaving Potter Y-r „d he lived successively in Chapel-street 0;0 Liverpool-road, in houses which have bMr swallowed up in the firm's expansion. for Gleave's father, before him, worked the Messrs. Crosfield, but that was before subject of this sketch entered upon his w— ing life. ti Mr. Gleave told our representative ;til during his sixty-three years' connection vi:jes the concern, it had extended its botindT:',,gs in such a remarkable way , that binielros formerly used as a cotton factorY, a fon, bottle works, and a flour mill had, in • been included within its area. fool. Mr. Gleave has five sons, reo. grandchildren, and four great grandei