      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>VETERAN SOAP WORKER.</head>
        <p>VETERAN SOAP WORKER.</p>
        <p>The Notable Record of Messrs. Crosfield's Oldest Employe. WAITING FOR THE KING.</p>
        <p>Probably no one is looking forward with greater pleasure and pride to the approaching visit of his Majesty the King to the works of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., than George Dodd Gleave, who boasts the proud distinction of being the oldest employe of the firm. To look at him one would hardly imagine that Mr. Gleave has an unbroken record of sixty-three years' service to his credit. He carries his years—he will be seventy-two next October—so well that there is barely the suggestion of a stoop in his walk, and he is blessed with such a wiry constitution that, after a remarkably long period of faithful work, be is still able to follow his duties daily from 7.30 a.m. to 5 p.in. Of course, the firm has shown him every consideration, and the situation which he at present holds is such as merely t') pro- vide him with light employment. The Coming of the King. When the King inspects the works at Bank Quay on July 7th, Mr. Gleave will be seen in charge of the hoist which his Majesty will pass just before entering the soapery, where the liquid soap bubbles and boils in huge iron pans. That is not really the oldest</p>
