      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>MR. GLEAVE.</head>
        <p>MR. GLEAVE.</p>
        <p>workman's usual task, but, as it is but a short time since he relinquished the post of hoist attendant, it has been thought fitting to recall him to it on the memorable day in order that he may be brought into close proximity to the Royal visitors. It is not unlikely that his Majesty's special attention will be directed to this veteran of toil, and, should that be the case, there will be no prouder man in England that day.</p>
        <p>When Messrs. Crosfield had _Eighty Employes. . _ _</p>
        <p>Chatting to an " Examiner " representa- tive, Mr. Gleave, who lives at 34, Powys- street—within a stone's throw of the works —gave some interesting reminiscences of the early days of the concern at Bank Quay, which showed how vast and remarkable has been the expansion and development of the enterprise. Born on October 18th, 1841, Mr. Gleave entered Messrs. Crosfield's service at the age of nine. Schooling in those days was a matter of indifference. To use Mr. Gleave's own words: "It was almost work before school, for it all depended on the parents sending their children to school to get them out of the way." However, the youthful George did receive a little " school- ing" at an establishment called the Bank Quay school, which stood on part of the site now occupied by Messrs. Robinson's glass- house. Mr. Gleave's first task was that of tying candles into bundles, but he was so small of stature that he had to be lifted on to his bench. He recalls the time when the late Mr. George Crosfield, Mr. Morland Cros- field and Mr. John Crosfield used to assist him in the warehouse, also doing the clerical work, along with Mr. James McGowan and Mr. Antony McGowan, formerly of Stanley- street. The works was then of very modest dimensi ons, and the employes would not number more than 80 at the outside! To-day the firm employs 2,500 people, and the wages bill is over £2,000 a week.</p>
        <p>A Candle Factory. "They did very little soap-making in those days," said Mr. Gleave. "It was merely brown soap, and a little bit of pale soap "—a striking contrast to the modern output of many varieties of household and toilet soaps. Mr. Gleave remembers when only four or five pans sufficed to boil the soap ; now there are over fifty. It is worth noting that one pan which was installed on the founding of the bnterprise in 1815 still has its place in the works. The manufac- ture of tallow candles was an important part of the firm's operations when Mr. Gleave began work. There was a three-storey builaing for that purpose where the London and North-Western Railway line (low level) now runs alongside the works. The production of candles was confined to the winter months, the climatic conditions making it impossible to carry on that part of the business in the summer. When not occupied in tying up candles, young Gleave did his share of work in the soapery.</p>
        <p>When Soap was Taxed. Mr. Gleave is fond of harking back to the 1 time when the present heads of the firm used to visit the works while on holiday from school. He also told our representa- tive of the precautions that had to be adopted to comply with the requirements of the Inland Revenue authorities when a duty was payable on soap. The excise office stood almost on the very spot where Mr. Gleave I will be stationed when the King comes through the works. When soap had been boiled, the pans were locked over-night so that there could be no interference with the contents until the excise officer hadexamined them. "There was a rare 'to do' when the duty was abolished," said Mr. Gleave, evidently meaning that its disappearance gave great satisfaction to those engaged in the trade. As showing the improvement in the methods adopted, Mr. Gleave said that at one time it was the custom for men to transfer the liquid soap from the pans to the frames by means of pails carried on their heads. Now, it is run from one place to the other through shoots. Every boil of soap used to be " crutched " by hand—that is to say, an appliance resembling a crutch was plunged into the liquid to mix it—whereas now the process is generally carried out by machinery. A large proportion of the raw materials used by the firm is brought to Warrington by water, the works having a large frontage to the Mersey. Mr. Gleave remembers occa- sions when the boats having been left stranded down the river by the receding tide, barrels of materials, &amp;c., have been</p>
      </div>
