      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 28, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ROYAL VISIT,</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL VISIT,</p>
        <p>Local Preparations Enter on Final Stage. INCREASED GRANT TO THE MAYOR,</p>
        <p>Warrington has only another week in which to prepare for the visit of their Majesties, the King and Queen, on Monday, July 7th, and there are signs of activity on every hand. The erection of the framework of the Royal pavilion, in front of the Town Hall, is practically completed, and it is evident that the structure has been admir- ably designed for the purpose and is raised sufficiently to afford the huge gathering which will assemble, a good view of the presentation proceedings. The woodwork will be artistically draped in Royal purple and gold, and, when their Majesties arrive, the Royal Standard will be hoisted above the pavilion. The ladies and gentlemen, who are to be presented, will be in their positions on the platform in readiness for the coming of their Majesties, so that the formalities will occupy only a short time.</p>
        <p>The Town Hall will be treated on exactly the same lines as on the occasion of the visit of the late King Edward—that is to say, the decorations will be limited to the display of boxes of flowers at the windows. The effect of this form of embellishment was generally admired when last adopted and nothing could be more fitting or in keeping with the dignity of the building.</p>
        <p>INCREASED GRANT TO THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>The Town Council will on Tuesday evening be asked to sanction the grant of an additional £3OO to the Mayor to meet the expenses incidental to the Royal visit. This will bring the total amount allocated from the rates to £BOO. We understand that one cause of the inadequacy of the £5OO originally decided upon is that considerable expense will have to be incurred in the draft- ing of large detachments of police from other towns to assist in lining the streets. As the visit of their Majesties is, to some extent, unofficial, the military authorities do not undertake the • responsibility of ensuring their safe passage and the work consequently thrown upon the police will thus be more than the local force, unaided, could cope with. AT THE BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The first-portion of the bridge is practically complete and workmen are now engaged in putting the finishing touches to the road- way,which has been constructed of setts, and in erecting girders which will temporarily serve the purpose of electric standards. Mr. J. J. Webster (engineer) and Mr. Alfred Thorne (contractor) were on the works yesterday. .</p>
        <p>Shortly after their Majesties leave the Town Hall, it is intended to unveil a com- memorative tablet on the north side of the large ;'pedestal of the bridge. This will be executed in bronze repousse and will bear the following inscription :</p>
        <p>I (BOROUGH ARMS.)</p>
        <p>THIS BRIDGE WAS OPENED BY KING GEORGE V.-</p>
        <p>7TH JULY, 1913. Councillor G. W. JOSEPH, M.D., J.P. Mayor. Alderman JAMES SMETTIURST, J.P., Chairman of the Bridges Committee. JOHN J. WEBSTER, M.lnst., C.E., Engineer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>LORD DERBY'S VISIT,</head>
        <p>LORD DERBY'S VISIT,</p>
        <p>Lord Derby will visit Warrington on Friday morning next to give his final approval to the local preparations for the visit. THE TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>The local battalion of Territorials—the 4th V.B. South Lancashire Regiment—will take a prominent part in the day's proceedings. A •company, which consists of employ es of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., will parade under the orders of Major Crosfield. The remainder of the battaliou will fall in at the Drill Hall, as strongly as possible at 12.30 (noon) and march to Arpley station, where they will form a square aud await the arrival of their Majesties. The band will be in attendance, and the regimental colours will be carried.</p>
        <p>A rehearsal of the Royal visit arrange- ments will take place on Wednesday next at 9 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>AT CROSFIELD'S WORKS.</head>
        <p>AT CROSFIELD'S WORKS.</p>
        <p>The Choral Welcome to their Majesties.</p>
        <p>Everything is in readiness for the visit of their Majesties to the works of Messrs Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd. On Monday there was what might be termed a rehearsal of the events of the great day, and a repre- sentative of the " Examiner " had the privi- lege of visiting those departments through which the Royal party will be conducted. We have already described the various pro- cesses which their Majesties will witness, and it is now sufficient to say that they range from the boiling of the soap, in huge iron pans to the packing of the finished article in fancy cardboard boxes. Their Majesties will see the immense establishment in its ordinary, workaday aspect—and that, after all, is the object of their tour through industrial Lancashire. On Monday, all the old employes who will be on duty on July 7th were at their posts, and their Majesties cannot fail to be impressed by the muster of men who have grown old in the service of the firm, and who point with pride to their long connectioli with the works at Bank Quay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>A. SCENE OF ACTIVITY.</head>
        <p>A. SCENE OF ACTIVITY.</p>
        <p>Looking down on to the tablet room, a scene of remarkable activity presented itself. Bars of household soap were emerging in an apparently endless line from the stamping machines, to be piled in little heaps, and then, with a speed that was almost incredible, wrapped up and packed. From the raised footway which runs round the room, their Majesties will have an excellent view of this hive of industry, and, it may be added, the girls will also have a splendid opportunity of seeing their Majesties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>CHORAL WELCOME.</head>
        <p>THE CHORAL WELCOME.</p>
        <p>As the party entered the room, where fancy soaps and perfumes are packed and bottled, the sweet voices of the girls were heard in "The Veteran's song," which, with its refrain "Long live the King," will be an appropriate greeting to the Sovereign. Whether it will actually be sung when their Majesties appear in the room cannot be definitely stated, as the choice will be left entirely to the girls. They looked supremely happy as they sang while their deft fingers placed the soap in dainty cardboard boxes. It is, of course, the habit of the girls—and the firm wisely encourage it—to sing as they work. It is one way of making "the daily round" pleasureable. The favourite songs with the girls are "Cherry ripe," "The lass of Richmond Hill" and "Killarney," but, as most of them are members of the firm's amateur operatic society, they are also fond of singing excerpts from the operas they pro- duce. Fortunately, rag-time finds no place in , the repertoire—nor is it likely to. It is safe 1 to predict that their Majesties will find their visit to the wrapping room the most novel— and probably most pleasing—feature of the incidents connected with their visit to the great soap works by the Mersey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>WILD FLOWERS OF TM</head>
        <p>WILD FLOWERS OF TM</p>
        <p>The Table at the Warrington Museum is • Now at its Best.</p>
        <p>A BEAUTIFUL coLLEeTioN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Photograph by B. S. Jones, Warrington. AN ABUN DANT STORE.</head>
        <p>Photograph by B. S. Jones, Warrington. AN ABUN DANT STORE.</p>
        <p>The wild flower table, established by Mr. Charles Madeley many years ago and continued so successfully by his assistant, Air. G. A. Dunlop, with the object of bringing together in comprehensive manner sp-ciniens of the wild flora of the district in which the Mtnieum stands, is now looking its best. It is a credit to the institution, and may b looked upon with pride by those who are lovers of nature and are affected by considerations of local patriotism.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday we visited the wild flower room at the Museum, witnessed a delightful show of colour and form and noted the excellent methods by which the classification is now carried out with an eye to securing every edur'atictial benefit that such a collec- tion can afford. There were many species on view and we can only deal with some as being examples of the rest.</p>
        <p>Half a dozen of the dozen different kinds of butter- cups to be found in this locality are on the table. To most people a buttercup is a buttercup, but here we not only learn that there are twenty kinds, but the differences are p.,inted out on well written and attractive cards. We are told, for instance, that the difference between the creeping buttercup and the upright buttercup is that the flower is deeper in colour, the stem is thicker, softer and hairy, that the segments of the leaves are larger, that it affects the " running " habits of the strawberry. In the same manner the essential differences between the upright and the bulbous buttercup are pointed out. The plan of campaign as conducted at the Museum is seen, for instance, in the contrast of colours in the clover and vetches, which give a pleasing variety in</p>
        <p>their neigbouring positions. The aim is to get as many species as possible of each flower in its natural order, showing the differences and the family re4emhlances at a glance. There were some remarkably fine specimeng of th e wild pansy, " the original of our cultivated prisy." This flower is most variable in its colour and size. "We have seen it," says the card on the table, "in abundance miles away from cultivation, on a hill top with beautiful violet and yellow flowers nearly as large as a penny piece, and we have found this same species in the Warrington district, growing under difficulties when the flower is much less than the size of a farthing." Nevertheless, the Warrington specimens staged this week were large, bold, and well coloured blooms. Next came the modest field violet.</p>
        <p>Gayest, as ever, came the poppy and the broom and specimens of the marsh cinquefoil made a pretty study in red, brown and green. Hogweed, stitchworts, cam- pious and many other species were represented. The first of the thistles—the marsh thistle—was here; and the grasses, thanks to Mr. G. P. Miln, of the firm of Gartons Ltd., were a very fine show. There were 30 species which occupied a whole shelf, and told in an impressive manner that a wet spring and favouring conditions since have arranged that there shall be a vety rich hay harvest this year. Mr. Dunlop informed us that grass is abnormally high and that this week he had seen cocksfoot that was sft. high. We are glad to know that the schools are making good use of this most excellent wild fl)wer table, which has been adopted as a model in other parts of the country.</p>
