      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ALEXANDRA DAY.</head>
        <p>ALEXANDRA DAY.</p>
        <p>To-Day's Great Effort For Local Charities.</p>
        <p>A ROSE CARNIVAL.</p>
        <p>Warrington will be gay with roses to-day, for, after the manner of other great centres, the town is to keep Alexandra Day in an eminently appropriate and picturesque way. The festival was instituted last year to celebrate the fiftieth anniversary of Queen Alexandra's coining to this country-, and so enthusiastically was the idea received, that Alexandra Day seems likely to find a per- manent place in the calendar. There is no warmer supporter of our hospitals than the Queen-Mother, and the promoters of the festival could not have chosen any form of celebration, more in keeping with her Majesty's wishes, than the sale of flowers in the streets on behalf of those noble institu- tions for the relief of suffering humanity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>DESERVING OBJECTS TO BENEFIT.</head>
        <p>DESERVING OBJECTS TO BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>Warrington has fallen into line with the national movement right loyally, and to-day almost every person one meets will be wear- ing the wild rose. At least, that is the intention of the 400 ladies who have under- taken to sell the button-holes in all parts of the town and district, and we have every confidence that they will accomplish it. The Warrington Infirmary is primarily to benefit by the effort, but on Thursday, the Alexandra Day committee came to a decision—which, we feel sure, will commend itself to the townspeople—that several other deserving institutions should also share in the proceeds. Mr. J. H. Smethurst, who, in addition to being chairman of the committee, is chairman of the Infirmary , Board of Management, made a proposition—which was carried with unanimity and cordiality— that one-fifth of the net proceeds should be given to the District Nursing Association, and £5 ss. each to the Children's Summer Camp and the Cripples' Guild, the balance to be devoted to the Infirmary. LADIES TO SELL 50,000 ROSES.</p>
        <p>The 50,000 artificial wild roses, which it is hoped to dispose of as buttonholes, have been made by the crippled girls trained in Mr. John Groom's excellent institution. The work of selling them has been most carefully organised. The borough has been divided into wards, with lady helpers responsible for each section, and the effort is also to embrace such outlying districts as Stockton Heath, Latchford, Grappenhall, Thelwall, Appleton, Walton, Sankey, Penketh ard Winwick. If sufficient workers can be spared. it is hoped to go even so far afield as Earlestown. The ladies will go about in couples, one having a well-stocked basket of flowers, and the other a' money box. Some will be at their posts as early as six in the morning, and the peddling will be kept up incessantly —by relays of helpers—until 11 o'clock at Inight. The hats of the lady sellers will be ' embellished with roses, and their baskets will be suspended by ribbons bearing the words. "Alexandra Day" in gold letters. The Mayoress (Mrs. Arnold Nield) will visit the municipal offices and the banks for the purpose of selling the roses. Permission has been obtained for ladies to call at Orford Barracks to induce " Tommy Atkins" to bedeck himself with the dainty pink flowers. Appeals will also be made to the audiences in the various places of amusement, so that no one will have a legitimate excuse for not sporting the familiar button-hole.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>DECORATED MOTOR CARS.</head>
        <p>DECORATED MOTOR CARS.</p>
        <p>The Mayor has requested local manufac- turers and tradespeople to mark the occasion by displaying flags and bunting on their premises. The request of Mrs. Henry Greenall, hon. secretary of the committee, that local motorists should decorate their cars with Alexandra roses has been most favourably received, and the result should be an exceedingly pretty spectacle in the main streets.</p>
        <p>LADIES WHO WILL SUPERINTEND. The following is a list of the ladies who have made themselves responsible for the sale of flowers in specified wards and districie : Orford Ward and Orford Village : Mrs. Halliwell and Mrs. H. Jolley. St. Austin's: Mrs. Bowden, Mrs. Coppack, Mrs.</p>
        <p>Joseph, Mrs. Langdale, Mrs. Highley, Mrs. Osborne and Miss Booth.. St. John's: Mrs. Berry,lirs. Powell and Mrs. McKee. Town Hall: Mrs. Parsonage and Mrs. Lindley. Whitecross : MIS. Delaney and Miss Houghton. Bewsey : Mrs. J. H. Smethurst, Mrs. Simpson, Mrs. Bunch and Mrs. Webster. Fairfield : sirs. Willis.</p>
        <p>Latchford : Mrs. Broadbent, Mrs. Locker, Mrss Holmes, Mrs. Short, Mrs. Venn, Miss Gornall and Mis. M. Fairclougb. Howley: Mrs. Evans, Mrs. Hill and Miss Hill. Stiekton Heath Mrs. H. Roberts.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall : Mrs. F. Alytner Frost and Mrs, Pigot. Stretton : Mrs. Fletcher. Penketh : Mrs. Murray. Walton: Mrs. Johnson. Hatton: Mrs, J. J. Whitley.</p>
        <p>Daresbury : Mrs. E. G. Davies. Moore: Mrs. Burder. The! : Miss B. Rylands. Sankey : Miss Jones. Padgate, Win wick, Earlestown : The Executive. Municipal offices and banks: The Mayoress. Places of amusement: The Executive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>TO RAISE £250.</head>
        <p>TO RAISE £250.</p>
        <p>Ladies' Committee and the Allocation of Proceeds.</p>
        <p>" Wear a wild rose in honour of Queen Alexandra and help our local charities" is the motto for to-day. How enthusiastic are the ladies in the enterprise, was evident from the large attendance at the meeting of the committee, held at the Infirmary on Thursday afternoon.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. SMETHURST, who presided, expressed his satisfaction at the splendid interest which- was being shown in the movement. They had all read of the great results achieved in other places, and he was sure they were determined to do their utmost to ensure that Alexandra Day should be as successful in War- rington as in Manchester or Liverpool. (Applause.) He did not know whether they would be able to prevail upon anybody to follow the example of the Liverpool gentleman who gave £25 for one bunch of iliwers—(laughter and applause)—but they would do -their utna ,st to obtain the best possible value for the roses. Having referred to the arrangements which had been made to convey the lady sellers from one point to another in motor cars, Mr. Smethurst went on to speak of the allocation of the proceeds. Originally it was intended tl a-, the whole of the money should go to the Infirmary, and the manner in which the ladies had pruferred assistance was spleadid proof of their earnest desire to help that noble institution. There never was a time when more work was being done in the Infirmary or when more money was required. If those people who had money would only look at the work of the institution through his (the speaker's) eyes, he believed the people of Warrington would be surprised to see how much more could be done on its behalf. At the same time, they did not wish to be unmindful of the claims of cthers. He need not tell them of the great claim which the District Nursing Association had upon the charitable people of Warrington, of the blessing which the Children's Summer Camp was to the poor children, or of the great good which could be done with a little money in connection with the Cripples' Guild. He was con- fident that on Saturday night they would have got rid 'of the whole of their flowers, and have raised at least .+.:250, and he did not think lie was being too sanguine. (Applause.) Mr. Smethurst concluded by proposing that the proceeds should be allocated in the proportions mentioned above. The proposition was unanimously accepted. Mrs. HENRY GREENALL expressed her hearty thanks to all the heads of the wards and their helpers. They had worked splendidly, and even when she had asked them to do the most disagreeable things they had willingly responded. She could only thank every- body for the kind way in which they had backed her up. NURSING ASSOCIATION'S CLAIMS.</p>
        <p>Councillor Henry Roberts, bon. treasurer of the District Nursing Association, writes describing the decision of the Alexandra Day Committee, with reference to the allocation of the proceeds, as gratify- ing and helpful. He urges the special claims of the Nursing Association to support, and points out that £OOO per annum has to be raised by sub- scriptions and donations. This year, the staff of nurses has been increased to meet the demand for their highly-appreciated services. In one month this year, the nurses made 1,800 visits, and Councillor Roberts cogently adds: "Imagine 'Warrington to-day without such an institution as ours ! "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>£25 FOR A FOURPENNY SPRAY.</head>
        <p>£25 FOR A FOURPENNY SPRAY.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool, the Lady Mayoress (Mrs. Harmood Banner) had specially identified herself with the work of Queen Alexandra Day, which was celebrated on Wednesday. Dressed in white, with picture hats decorated with wild roses, the helpers patrolled the streets, and such of the public as did not buy readily for the benefit of the cause very soon found it neces- sary to do so for personal convenience. There was an enormous sale. In the morning, the Lady Mayoress and a party visited the various commercial exchanges Wont inucti</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>COUNTRYSIDE</head>
        <p>THE COUNTRYSIDE</p>
        <p>How Nature takes possession of the derelict.</p>
        <p>A DISAPPEARING CANAL</p>
        <p>Mr. Guy A, Dunlop, sub-curator of the Warrington Museum, who has more than a local reputation as a Naturalist, and writer of fascinating notes on Nature, and her works, contributes the first eta series of special articles he has under- taken to supply to the '• Examiner." This week he shows how Nature is gradually reclaiming the Old Quay Canal, converting the waterway to land now that the 'interference" of man has been with- drawn and transferred to the greater task of maintaining the Ship Canal.</p>
        <p>When the strong restraining band of man is removed from active interference, Nature very quickly asserts, or reasserts, herself. To one with power to reap the harvest of the quiet eye," instances are abundant in the country rambles round our town. A. twenty minutes leisurely walk from Warrington Bridge along the Chester-road tp the Stag Bridge brings into view one of the very finest examples in the country— the Old Quay Canal.</p>
        <p>Stealing Land from Water. Two decades ago, before the Manchester Ship Canal interrupted its traffic at Twenty Steps Bridge, this old water-way had a busy, prosperous life. Now, ite lower reaches from Lower Walton to Runcorn enjoy nothing more exciting than a "sleepy hollow " existence, with only a family or two of dabchichs and a few farm- yard duck,. to disturb its placid surface. The stretch of water between the Chester-road and the Twelve Arches shows many evidences of Nature's reclaiming work, but it is most active near the Arches, where the water is being attacked by the land-forming process, in which the plant life plays a very important part. A few moments' observation and examination of the portion of the canal near the entrance to the culvert will show a considerable encroach- ment of the land into the water. Eight years ago the water edge was two yards further inland with such amphibious plants as the bur marigold, celery-leaved buttercup, procumbent marsh- wort and floating meadow grass dominant at the margin, flanked by great tufts of rushes and sedges on the banks. The ground on which cne formally sank over the boot-tops is now hard and firm and in possession of plants usually associated with meadows, such as white clover, lesser yellow clover, upright buttercup, cock's-foot grass, and smooth meadow grass. The marsh arrow grass (which, by the way, is not a grass, but a relative of the pretty rose-coloured flowering rush) a true marsh plant, formally plenti- ful on this patch, is now scarcely to be found, being gradually exterminated by the transitional conditions. The soft-stemmed margin plants have now nearly dis- appeared, owing to interfering man clearing the edge of the banks to prevent the choking of the culvert. The same process is in active operation on the opposite bank, a little nearer to Weir's farm, where the strong, robust, great willow-herb is the dominant plant in the land formation. The interlacing of the thick strong roots of the water plants, form a basic mass into which the roots of the margin vegetation encroach, and as the abundant foliage and thick succulent stems of the aquatic plants decay each year and sink to the bottom an appreciable addition is made to the water bed. Where this process .is rapid, as in the present case, the rushes and sedges quickly take possession of the prepared network of roots and mud. Once they are firmly established the ground becomes firmer and more solid and is appropriated inch by inch by plants suited exactly to the nature of the soil. Vegetation and Time Filling up the Canal. I Let us resume our stroll a little further, passing I under the old and new railway bridges, when a sharp turn to the left will bring us again to the canal where it issues from the other end of the culvert. From this point to Lapwing-laue bridge at Moore we have a most delightful hour's walk in front of us. For the slower the pace the greater the content of our enjoy- ment. At every step what abundance of informing material there is for the observing eye and eager, inquiring mind ! On every hand there is ample evidence that Nature is silently, slowly, and surely at work, healing the wounds that man has inflicted on Mother Earth with his iron tools. The innoceut look- ing green skin which covers the canal from end to end on fine, windless days has more than a passive interest in Nature's restorative mission. On the days when the wind blows strong from the west, as it very often does here-about, this floating sheet of green is blown in front of the wind until it is forced in great quantities against the first obstacle which it meets. Consequently, at the truncated end of the canal, where it enters the culvert, and at each of the four bridges, great submerged masses are formed. When the wind settles down again much of it bates gFntly up the stream again, but a very large portion becomes broken, soddened, crushed and unfitted to maintain the struggle for existence. The writer has been curious enough to examine these masses cf bruised vegetation, and bas found it at times over twelve inches deep. One can easily appreciate the fact that as week after week, month after month, and year after year as this plant life decays and sinks to the bottom it interferes considerably with the depth of the waterway. It is worth while to examine this green vegetation for a moment, there is much of interest about it for its own sake. A walking-stick inserted into the water can be withdrawn again with several of the individual items of the mass adhering. It will be found that each of these tiny plants, measuring less than a quarter of an inch, has all the parts of a complete flowering plant. It is in fact, the smallest British flowering plant—the lesser duckweed. The little egg-shaped, compressed, floating leaves have a solitary root descending into the water, and in July an extremely small, perfectly formed, yellow flower. It is the presence of these liliputian flowers in uncount- able numbers which gives the canal its yellowish sheen in mid-summer.</p>
        <p>Old Bridges held By Wild Plants. The old bridges, which in the old days used to swing easily round to let the barges pass through, are now fixed and secured to the banks, and have been patched, repaired and altered out of rEcognition. The shelves in the bank, where the bridge rested formally when turned, are now in full possession of qnite a variety ot wild plants. The strong massive beams, now that man's care is withdrawn, are slowly crumb- ling to earth. Exposed to all kinds of weather, the damp began the rotting process. This gave the ever- searching, insidious, microscopic fungi the chance of which they speedily took advantage. By-and-bye, burrowing insects also came to the attack, and then, here and there, grasses and smaller plants began to grow in the crevices. Thus the silent forces--visible and invisible—that work for the destruction of man's interposing handiwork, proceed unceasingly in quick succession. The bridges, likewise, are in the bands of Nature's vandals, but man's authority is always likely to be asserted on their behalf, before too much damage is done. Dominant Plants at Work. The most striking object lesson of all of Nature's repairing work is seen on the south bank of the canal between Lapwing-lane and Bob's bridges. Here, on Sir Richard Brooke's estate, the aquatic and sub- aquatic vegetation have taken possession of about half of the width of the water. The dominant plants at work are quite different from those performing the same task at Walton Arches. The bur-reed, yellow iris, the great reed, all strong, robust and vigorous plants, are in the first line ef attack, the rear in the margin being the great sedge. and several species of rushes growing in great luxuriant, aggressive-looking tussocks. Whilst the water is being attacked by the land from the landward side, it breeds enemies within. The canal in some parts is full of water plants. Water moss, Canadian water weed, pond grow in luxurious abundance, and periodic- ally choke up the water, so much so that the angling authority, which preserves the fish, are obliged to reduce their amount of vegetation by dredging, or rather dragging it out, with indifferent success. But every year many tons of plant tissue in the canal die, decay and become a permanent con - tribution to the eventual filling up of the Old Quay"' Canal.</p>
        <p>G. A. DUNLOP.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dunlop will be glad to identify specimens or give- any information on Nature subjects. Specimens or inquiries should be forwarded to the " Examiner " Office or to the Warrington Museum.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <p>and did a thriving trade. At the Fruit E/change there was such a spirit of gallantry that when the Lady Mayoress offered a 4d. spray for sale it was put up for auction, and bought for 5..25 by a member of the Greek community. The weather, though dull and chilly in the morning, improved greatly as the day passed, and the ladies on duty were spared the unpleasantness which they would have undergone, if the day had been Tuesday. In the afternoon the Lady Mayoress entertained her large staff of assistants to tea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
