        <head>, _ – '..• • – -,. ---- , ...... ~ . .' ' -- ' 71 – ' ...4a!,' -; . -,4'.. . ~..•,., – : ,--..., : : ''',..'' Aid- LT a ‘-.1%.„. T Pi EW3 i, 1. 1-' .... _, ~,...„.• •,..„__. „, • ,___ ~. __ _„_</head>
        <p>, _ - '..• • - -,. ---- , ...... ~ . .' ' -- ' 71 - ' ...4a!,' -; . -,4'.. . ~..•,., - : ,--..., : : ''',..'' Aid- LT a ‘-.1%.„. T Pi EW3 i, 1. 1-' .... _, ~,...„.• •,..„__. „, • ,___ ~. __ _„_</p>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST GARDEN PARTY.—We would call our readers' attention to the announcement on the front page relating to the Lymm Primitive Methodist annual garden party which is to take place next Wednes- day. An attractive programme has been arranged, including songs by 24adame Wilson and violin solos by Mr. Andrews. A variety of games and competitions are to form part of the proceedings, and given a fine day the event should be as successful as its predecessors. CONGREGATIONAL CHRISTIAN SERVICE BAND.—A well-attended meeting was held in the school vestry on Monday evening, when a very inspiring address was given by Mr. Sydney Linley, his subject being "The Christian's March." Mrs. C. Lewis rendered a very appropriate solo entitled, " Simply trusting every day. Mr. Percy Leather presided. YOUNG LlBERALS.—Encouraged by the kind redep- tion that was accorded them at Lymm during the recent by-election the Altrincham League of Young Liberals paid another visit on Saturday, when they held a successful meeting at the Cross. Mr. George Hear pre- sided over the gathering at which Mr. Dupes and Mr. 11. Weller spoke on the land question, a subject on which both gentlemen are well-known authorities. LYMM GAS AND WATER BILL.-1 he Bill promoted by Lymm Urban District Council to acquire the water- works and to extend their gas undertaking, &amp;.c., on Tuesday came before their Chairman of Committees of the HOU33 of I.ords as an unopposed measure, and was ordered to be reported to the House of Lords. It has already wised through the House of Commons.—Mr. William Mullird, clerk to• the Council, proved the preamble, and Mr. 'rhos. Ridgway. solicitor to the Bill, proved the agreements. MOTHERS' UNtos,'.—On Tuesday about twenty-five members of the Lyibm Branch of the Mothers' Union .rnade the journey to Chester for purpose of attending the Triennial Festival held in that city. The party were in attendance at the noon service in the Cathedral, when the Lord Bishop of Southwell was the preacher. Advantage was taken of the fine afternoon for a sail on the Dee to Eaton Hall, where, by the kind permission of the Duke of Westminster, the visitors were allowed the privilege of going through the hall and gardens. The Rector (Rev. G. B. Thurston) accompanied tbe rarty. BRITISH WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION.—The North Cheshire Summer Conference of the National British Women's Temperance Association was held in the ground of" Woodeaves," Hale Barns, on Wednes- day. Mrs. Carver presided, whilst the principal speaker was Mrs. Shilston Watkins. Several of the Lymm members were present, and it is pleasing to note that among the ladies giving reports of their respective branches, Mrs. F. P. Brown, hon. secretary of the Lymm branch, and Mrs. Penaliggon, of Bucklow Hill, both distinguished themselves. ALEXANDRA DAY.—A number of local young ladies thought that this year Lymm should share in the celebrat:ons of Alexandra Day, with the result that last Friday a band of workers composed of Mrs. E. N. Owen, the Misses Collins, Hartley, M. Moston, Nuttall, Saville and Waterwortb, came out with trays full of clever imitation wild roses, the handiwork of crippled girls, which in the name of charity they prevailed upon the passers-by to purchase. Mrs. Owen and Miss Saville also took up their stand on Saturday, when they did a " roaring trade." Thus was the splendid sum of £ll 6s. 7d. raised, which goes towards helping to swell the Manchester total for charitable objects.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>HOUSING PROBLEM.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSING PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Lymm Council Discuss the Dearth of Workmen's Cottages.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>GROSS OVERCROWDING.</head>
        <p>GROSS OVERCROWDING.</p>
        <p>The mcnthly meeting of tbe Lymm Urban District Council was held on Thursday evening, Mr. S. Davies presiding. There were also present: Mr. R. H. Gotobed (vice-chairman), Rev. G. B. Thurston, Messrs. G. L. Welford, J. H. Holt, W. G. Groves, 0. G. Smith, T. H. Warburton, W. Gould, T. F. W. Dixon, F. Lockyer, W. Mullard (clerk;, D. Most (surveyor) and W. L. Donald- son (gas manager).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>NEW BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>A NEW BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting:of the Ronda Committee it was decided on the motion of Mr. Davies, seconded by Mr. War- burton, that it in a recommendation to the Council that the clerk advertise for designs and tenders for the construAion of a ferro-concrete bridge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>LOWER DAM.</head>
        <p>THE LOWER DAM.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Sanitary Committee, it was decided that a letter be written to the Lycum Estate Development Company calling attention to the unsightly and insanitary state of the Lower Dam, and suggesting that it be thoroughly cleaned out and the dead trees and branches removed. . At a meeting of the same committee a letter was read from Messrs. Charles Moon and Co., Ltd., in reply to a complaint laid by Mr. A. H. Fray, of Heatley. It ass resolved that a copy of the letter be sent to Mr. Fray. The minutes were confirmed. THE HOUSING PROBLEM. COUNCIL'S BUILDING EXPERIMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. O. G. Smith read the report of the sub-com- mittee appointed to go into the housing question. He said the/ were of the unanimous opinion : 1, there was an urgent need for cottages in the district ; '2, if practicable, the Council should take up the question of building cottages, which must be sell- supporting ; 3, the land alongside Pepper-street should be used for the purpose of erecting three blocks of three cottages each, facing south, because of the sewer and gas facilities ;4, only one of the three blocks be erected at first so as to test the success of the scheme, the other two blocks to follow if it were found that there was sufficient demand ; 5, each cottage to contain three rooms up and two down. Continuing, Mr. Smith said the supply of cottages was most scant right through the district. One of the councillors had told them that he had had forty applicants for cottages when they became vacant. lhey had young people getting married and being compelled to remain in the house with the old fo,ks, because they could not get a cottage. It was felt by the committee that the Council could not ignore this state of things, and they were of the opinion that, failing private enterprise, the Council should take the matter up and thrash out a scheme. He referred to the favour- able terms which the Local Government Board would grant for the repayment of the loan. Mr. Smith said the members of the sub-committee thought that a rental of 4s. 6d. per week was a reasonable amount to expict. Taking a cottage at £2OO, that would leave a margin of about 23 per cottage. He had not taken into consideration the question of road-making ground rent or anything else. If they were to build at Heatley, where the congestion was more acute, and the problem was more pressing than in Lymm, they could get land at £lO per acre. The charge for land worked out at twopence per week per cottage, but the coat of land was not a very serious element. Mr. Gould asked if they did not think the Council had got enough on their bands without advancing a scheme which meant borrowing more money and adaing ta the rates. Mr. Smith said the committee were unanimous that the cottages should be self-supporting. If there was to be any charge on the rates at all the scheme must fall to the ground. The Rev. G. B. Thurston said the scheme was quite feasible and practicable, and that they would like to go into further details. They felt the need of cottages very strongly, and that Council in the future would have to provide for the housing of working-classes, for which there was a great deal of reform needed. Rents were going up in Lymm, not because cottages were better—they were worse—but because of the demand. The people had no remedy to get out of the extra threepence on their rent. All who were interested in the working-chases and their comfort should take that to heart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>GROSS CASES OF OVERCROWDING.</head>
        <p>GROSS CASES OF OVERCROWDING.</p>
        <p>- Mr. Gotobed said the surveyer had come across quite a number of gross cases of overcrowding which ought to give the Council serious cause for reflection. They had a number of insanitary houses in the district, and they were in a dilemma, for if they condemned them where, were the people to live ?The needs of the people -were being played upon, and the Council ought to take. the side of those who were being made the butt of the landlords. Rents were being charged out of all pro-, portion to the value of the cottages and the people were bound to pay. He hoped the Council would support the committee, and instruct them to go further into the matter. There would most assuredly be a demand for the houses, but he was of the opinion that there should be no charge on the rates. Mr. T. F. W. Dixon said that seeing the demand was so great, surely they had people in the district who wculd take the matter up as a private enterprise. Th Council had plenty on their plate at present. - The Rev. G. B. Thurston said that private people dide not erect the necessary houses. Mr. Dixon: I don't see why they don't. r. Holt believed that if they were further to con- sider the matter, it was most likely that in the mean- time private enterprise would slip in and provide a remedy for the shortage. Mr. Smith said the Council would not be tied to any- thing by empowering the committee to go further into the matter. Failing private enterprise, he thought the Council was bound to step in, as they could not con- demn the cottages and refuse to build more. Mr. Dixon: Have you heard of any other Council who have come successfully out of the venture.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith : Yes. Mr. Warburton said it seemed an alarming thing that rents were going up and private enterprise did not step in. Something would have to be done, but at which</p>
        <p>thing missing in the church file and that something was a minister. Also he, with others, had learnt to like Mr. Penaliggon, and he hoped this friendship, which be believed was due to Mr. Penaliggon's being a a sincere Christian, would continue to thrive. The Rev. Ballard, of Knutsfordi-spoke in commenda- tory terms of his association with Mr. Penaliggon as a neighbouring pastor. Mr. Dean, speaking on behalf of the Bucklow Hill Church, said that their members had been unanimous in consenting to the union of the two churches. He suggested that it should be christened" Bucklow Hill Ltd." and that everyone should feel that they were shareholders. In the course or a pithy speech, the Rev. R. W. B. Whiteway spoke of his intimate relationship with Congregationalism. He believed that they were not only taking the right step by having a pastor, but that they had got the right man in Mr. Penaliggon, and he hoped they would remember to give him a word of en- couragement and sympathy when he had heavy burdens to bear. The Rev. J. A. Penaliggon expressed his appreciation of the kindly manner in which his wife and himself had been received. His address was mainly on four points: (1) "We have to face very great difficulties from without," i.e.,indifference, &amp;c.,which bad caused them to form a union of the two churches; (2) "Mutual sense of responsibility," which was necessary for the success- ful working of the scheme ; (3) 'We must have the real Spirit of God." obtained through public and private prayer and fellowship with believets ; (4) "His right to expect loyalty." He prayed that the scheme would tend to the betterment of both churches, and the extension of the Kingdom of God. Mr. Penaliggon preached to splendid congregations on the following day.</p>
        <p>BIBLE CLASS OUTING.—Owing to the kindness of Mr. Egerton Warburton, of Arley, the rnenibers of St. Thomas's men's class B, together with the cycle club, spent a very enjoyable afternoon and evening on Satur- day, in the beautiful grounds at Arley Hall. The party were accompanied by their leader, Mr. Hallows. An excellent tea was provided in the riding school, after which the party dispersed to engage in games on the bowling green, croquet, and private grounds. A cricket match, married v. single, took place, in which the latter were defeated. On the motion of Mr. Cleaton,seconded by Mr. Jepson, a hearty vote of thanks was pas-ed to Mr. Warburton for his kindness, and to the ladies who had prepared the tea. Shortly after four o'clock a start was made for home via Budworth, and Stockton Heatl was reached soon after nine o'clock. P ARISH AFF AIRS DISCUSSED. THE QUESTION OF FIRE PRECAUTIONS. The monthly ,meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee was held at the Hall Cafe on Tuesday evening, Mr. G. Pilling presiding. There were also present :—Messrs. J. H. Clarke, F. Pearson, J. Whitley, F. Warburton, C. Dukes, A. Bottomley, W. Mclntyre, J. McDermid, j. Hallows, C. W. Hallows, H. J. Westbrook, Stanley Wright (surveyor) and W. B. Warburton (acting clerk). NOISES BY NIGHT. The nightsoil contractor wrote with reference to a complaint from the committee that tbe mare who removed the refuse created more noise than was neces- sary, to the disturbance of the inhabitants. He stated that every care would be exercised in future, the men having been instructed to do the work as quietly as possible. At this time of the year, however, most people slept with their bedroom windows open and noises could therefore be beard more distinctly. Mr. Westbrook: The complaint was not aboutthe noise from the tuabbins, but from the men's voices. The Chairman thought the letter should satisfy the committee: Tee contractor generally carried out all Unit he promised and he thought they might trust him in that matter. THE LIGHTING OF THE VILLAGE. Mr. Whitley, chairman ot the Lighting Committee, said people were complaining about the poor quality of the gas supplied to the village. Mr. Mclntyre: I think that has been due to some alterations made at the Swing Bridge which., have interfered with the mains. Mr. Whitley said there were also complaints that the lamps in some parts of the district were not lit until a late hour. The agreement with the Corporation was that every lamp should be lit within an hour after sun- set. There had, however, been occasions when twenty lamps had not been lit an hour a half or longer lifter sunset. The men did not turn out early enough.: - It was decided to call the attention of the Gas Depart- ment of the Corporation to the complaint,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <p>end of Lymm ? If big developments were going on at Heatley, there would certainly be a rush and it would be inconvenient if the houses were all away in Lymm. The report was accepted, and the committee were empowered to consider further details, on the proposition of Mr. Hblt, seconded by Mr. Lockyer. There were only two dissentients.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>RECOGNITION OF THE REV. J. A. PENALIGGON.</head>
        <p>RECOGNITION OF THE REV. J. A. PENALIGGON.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening, the recognition service of the Rev. J. A. Penaliggon, as pastor of the Lymm Congre- gational Church, was held. Mr. J. H. Holt, as senior deacon, presided over a good audience, amongst which were many friends from the Bucklow Hill and other Congragational Churches in the district, and representa- tives of the local Free Churches. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <p>Mr. J. H. Holt, in giving the reason for the holding of the service, said that owing to improved circum- stances they had been able to come to a working arrangement with a sister church. He was confident they had the right man in Mr. Penaliggon' and expressed his appreciation of the kindness of the Bucklow Hill friends in falling in with the idea of a co-pastorate, and hoped, it would be beneficial to hoth churches. He believtd that the Lymm church was a difficult one to work, and that lack of appreciation was often the cause of failure. He therefore pleaded for the cultivation of a kind and sympathetic feeling. between toe congrega- tion and minister. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Re/. Thomas, of Broadheatli, declared his pleasure at being present as the gathering was one which set the seal on a scheme which he had taken a share in bringing about. Speaking of the scheme of federation amongst various Cangregational Churches in the county he hoped that the present union would result in a bright future for both churches. The Rev. Johnson, of Sale, whilst alluding to his thirty-five years' knowledge of the church, said he was that Mr. Penaliggon started with the full confidence of the people. Mr. J. D. B. Smith, the hon. church secretary, read letters from several friends, including the Rev. E. E. Lovell, expressing their wishes for the success of the gathering, and the future welfare of the church. Mr. Smith said that they called out a pastor because, though the members had worked well, yet they found some-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>NEW PRIMITIVE METHODIST MINISTER.</head>
        <p>NEW PRIMITIVE METHODIST MINISTER.</p>
        <p>Left The Law For The Church.</p>
        <p>It is rather a strange coincidence that three of the local Nonconformist bodies should be almost simul- taneously undergoing ministerial changes. The Congregational and Baptist Churches have just called out new pastors, and next week will see the Rev. R. W. Ferguson, the successor of the Rev. R. W. B. Whitevvay, the Primitive Methodist minister, commencing his duties in the Lymm Circuit. Mr. Ferguson, whose birthplace was Birmingham, is a son of the late Rev. Dr. Joseph Ferguson, a well- known figure in Methodism, being one time secretary of the Primitive Methodist Sunday School Union, and in 1891, receiving the highest honour the Connexion can confer upon its ministers, namely, that of the position of President of Conference. Mr. Ferguson is one of a family of six, three daughters and three sons, and of his two brothers, one, Joseph Ferguson, 8.A., 8.D., is a Congregational minister, and the other, Charles B. Ferguson, M.A., is first master in Johannesburg College, South Africa. After an education at Bourne College, near</p>
