      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>REV: R. W. FERGUSON.</head>
        <p>THE REV: R. W. FERGUSON.</p>
        <p>Birmingham, where he passed the 3rd, 2nd and Ist, College of Preceptors, Oxford Locals and London Matriculation Examinations, with a view of becoming a solicitor, he entered the office of Mr. W. B. Craven, Leeds. However, the influence of his home so acted upon his heart and mind that he gave himself to the work of the ministry. After three years' training at the Connexion al Theological College, Manchester, under Professor Peake, Professor Neilson, Dr. Watson and Dr. Parkin, and where he distinguished himself by obtaining honours each year, he went to the Cambridge let Circuit. Four years later he proceeded to Wisbech, and later on to his present circuit at Walkden, where he has spent six successful years. The reverend gentle- man has been the president of the Farnworth and District Band of Hope and Temperance Union, and also the president of the Free Church Council for the same district,a position he is just vacating. Believing that the future hops of the church depends upon the youth of the present, the Rev. R. W. Ferguson takes a great interest in the young people. Mr. Ferguson is fond of such out-door games as football, cricket and tennis, whilst now he pleads guilty to a love for what he modestly styles " the old man's game of golf."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>SUGGESTED MOTOR 'Bus SERVICE.—A meeting of the Parish Council, for 'Orford and Winwick, was held in the schoolroom. Mr. P. Longton presided. and there were also present, Messrs. Wallace, Hambleton, Alker, Shaw, and Dell), and Mr. C. Gerosa, clerk. The Clerk was instructed to write the Town Clerk of Warrington as to the possibility of arranging a motor 'bus service to or through Winwick. At present there Was no service of any kind, and it would be of great advantage to the people of Winwick and district, if a regular service could be arranged. It was also decided to write to Mr. W. Forshaw, the member of the County Council for the Winwick division, with reference to the condition of the roads, roadsides and footpaths. Councillor Hambleton brought forward the question of fire precautions in the parish. He said he understood that for a certain sum the Warrington Corporation would attend any fire which might occur. This matter was disomsed at some length, but no decision was arrived at.</p>
        <p>- FIRE PRECAUTIONS. SUGGESTED ARRANGEMENT WITH WARRINGTON OR WILDERSPOOL. Mr. McDermid, chairman of the Fire Appliances Com- mittee, reported that Superintendent Turner, of the Warrington Fire Brigade, had been invited to inspect and advise concerning the existing equipment and staff, and that the Clerk had been instructed to obtain the terms and cost of direct communication and service from the Wilderspool and Warrington brigade. Mr. McDermid stated that he had seen Superintendent Turner, who received him very favourably, and seemed prepared to do his best to co-operate with them in any- thing that was required. He said he had been with the Stockton Heath brigade on several occasions, and.spoke highly of them. Superintendent Turner had since Inspected the equipment, and bad offered several suggestions, one of which was that the hose should be soaked in warm water. That had been done, and the hose was found to he in a fair condition. The superin- tendent said he would be willingto meet the committee at any convenient time. Mr. Bottomley suggested that in the meantime the Clerk should to instructed to obtain the charges of the Warrington Corporation and Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Co., for the services of their fire brigades. Mr. McDermid said that since they last met the Corporation had practically settled the question. Latchford had asked terms from Warrington. and had been placed on a basis in proportion to rateable value. In the case of Stockton Heath, the sum required on that basis—one penny in the £ on the assessment— would be a very large one. Mr. Whitley asked if terms were arranged witb the Wilderspool Brigade and a messige was sent at night- time, would there be anyone in the office to receive it? Mr. McDermid: Oh yes. Mr. Whitley: At all hours of the night? Mr. McDermid: There is always someone on duty. Mr. Clarke said that although he did not speak with authority, he could give the opinion that if a connec- tion could be made with the telephone, so as to. fret bell ringing at the lodge, the attention Of the watch- man could be secured. He was not always in the lodge, as he had to go on his rounds, but if the bell rang he would probably hear it, wherever he was. Mr. McDermid said there was such an arrangement at the Warrington fire station and it acted well. Mr. C. W. Hallows: I don't see why we should go to Warrington when we can get a brigade nearer borne. Mr. Bottomley : It is not a question of where to go to, but of getting prices from both places and then accepting the most favourable terms. Mr. Clarke said the Wilderspool Brigade did not require payment for their services. Mr. Bottomley remarked that it would be advisable to obtain an official communication tn that effect, and it could be considered with the other letters. Mr. J. Hallows: I don't see why we want to have the Corporation if we can gat the Wilderspool brigade. Mr. Westbrook : There is no objection to setting terms. It has been represented that an engine is not necessary in Stockton Heath, and that Superintendent Turner at the fire in Grappenhall-road said : ".What do you want an engine for? You have got quite sufficient pressure on the mains without pumping." Is that a fact? The Chairmen (Mr. G. Pilling) : It would be very much better with an engine. Mr. Westbrook: The Superintendent said not. A Member: Then why do they bring it? Mr. Bottomley observed that Superintendent Turner could be questioned on these matters when he met the committee. Mr. MoDermid : We have greater pressure on the high pressure mains in Stockton Heath than they have in Warrington. Mr. Westbrook: Greater than could be got by an engine. Mr. McDermid: Yes. It was decided to write to the Warrington Corpora- tion and Messrs. Greenall Whitley and Co. to ascertain their terms.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL. The Parish Council afterwards held its quarterly meeting, under the presidency of Mr. J. Whitley. A CONVENIENCE TO THE SICK POOR. A letter was read from Mr. H. Lawrence, clerk to the Parish Council of Walton Inferior, asking the Council to join that body in a representation to the Guardians with a view to arranging for a room to be hired in Stockton Heath for the attendance of Dr. Bowers on Union medical cases in both townships. They con- sidered it a great hardship for the sick poor to beve to travel to such a great distance before they could pro- cure medical attention or medicine. Mr. J. Hallows said the proposed arrangement would be a blessing and comfort to many sick people. It was decided to join with the Walton Inferior Council in bringing the question to the notice of the Board of Guardians. THE ASSESSMENT OF THIt DISTRICT. Mr. Bottomley asked the Clerk how many assess- ments there were in that district? The Clerk (Mr. E. Bradshaw) : Between 1,100 and 1,200. Mr. Bottomley : Have you any compounding of property ? The Clerk: Yes, we have; anything under ,£B. Mr. Bottomlev : There are no large works here? The Clerk : No. The forge is the largest works. He added that between 700 and 800 demand notes were issued.</p>
        <p>THE HOARDINGS QUESTION. Mr. McDermid asked whether the committee bad any control over the erection of hoardings, or could any other authority exercise it? He noticed a new hoarding had been erected in West-avenue. The Chairman said his attention had been (Hawn to the unsightliness of the boirding. Mr. Clarke: The owners of the property don't know anything about it. Mr. McDermid : Have we no control under the Advertisement Regulation Act? Mr. Westbrook said the question had been considered before. It was for the County Council to enforce the regulations of the Act. Mr. McDermid : Have we no power undef our by- laws. Mr. J. Hallows: We have power to prevent fresh ones being erected, tut we have no control over the old ones. The Chairman : We could report the matter to the County Council or the District Council and they would deal with it. The hoarding has been erected on District Council property.</p>
        <p>Principles of Cookery (Advanced).—Distinction Bessie Speakman. First class: Mary V. Brady, Lillie Crawshaw, Alice Foden, Annie Hayes, Annie Hitchmough, Rose Lawton, Elsie Leigh, Ada Phillips, Annie Taylor, Florence Toft, Fanny Wilkinson. Second class: Lily Ashurst, Lucy Ashurst, Annie H. Bell, Amy Bleasdale, Teresa Crannigan, Alice Fairhurst, Eva Hank inson, Elizabeth Holmes, May Jefferson, Gertrude Jones, Doris Kinsey, Margaret Reynolds, Florence Roberts, Nellie Simister, Dora Smallwood, Gladys Starkie, Lily Sudlow, Annie Webb, Ethel Whittaker, Alice Wood, Edith Yates. Pass : Clara Bowden, Nellie Gamble, Florence Jackson, Edith Mitchell, Ellen , Menks, Gladys Pomfret, Margaret Tabernacle, Ada I Wallington, Mary 'J. Weston. Principles of Cookery (Elementary),—First class: Elizabeth Brooks, Clara Brown, Amy Cragg, Amy Holt, May Holt. 'Second class: Ellen Bond, Annie Hardy, Nellie McCormack, Gladys Podmore, Annie Roberts. Practical Cookery (Advanced).—Distinction : Fanny Wilkinson. First class: Teresa Crannigan, Lillie Crawshaw, Alice Fairhurst, Alice Foden, Ada Phillips Nellie Simister, Dora Smallwood, Bessie Speakman, Annie Taylor. Annie Webb, Practical Cookery (Elementary).—First class : Ellen Bond, Clara Brown, Nellie McCormack. Second class: Elizabeth Brooks, Amy Cragg, Annie Hardy, Amy Holt, May Holt, Gladys Podmore, Annie Roberts. • Dressmaking (Advanced). First class : Edith Blomeley, Olive M. DAle, Florence V. Toft. Pass: Annie Bail. Ethel Whitby.</p>
        <p>Thompson, Percival E. Titley, Arthur Topping, George H. Wood. Second class: John A. Brown, John Brown, Albert H. Catterall, 'Harold Clayton, Dorothy Cotterill, Sydney Egerton, Ada Ellison, Percy R. Fair, John Haighton, Albert Hill, Albert H. Mather, Arthur H. Norse, Gladys R. Peters, Irene R. Stewart, Doris Webster, David S. Wood: PBS 3 : Reginald Byrarn, Harry Caldwell, Alfred Ellsmore, Miriam Goldstone, Doris Hill, Stanley Hill, Thomas A. Kendrick, John Newell John Stuart Henry J. Welch. _ , _</p>
