        <head>CAUSE AND EFFECT.</head>
        <p>CAUSE AND EFFECT.</p>
        <p>•tbout People speak glibly of cause and effect, °i'tbeir stopping to think that their own health, age„' c'l very existence, depends upon that great Pri"; co' Why do so many folks suffer from all kinds °dists plaints? Why this constant necessity of bug ',,-tbec the body, steadying the nerves, and a host adventitious aids to health in daily use ? because the majority of folk do not study t131,:5, (1° cause and effect as applied to themselves. not, and will not, study Nature, but prefer to 11,9"-bear the tales invented by quacks. Once and for all', od° in mind that Nature is the true mistress. Ver f': the are the genuine ones—all others are spurious ore matter of foodstuffs, an ounce of Nature is Wort" .kiag than a ton of artifice. Take the most etri the example of the efficacy of Nature's pure f°°d tsiu° little Greek Currant. It is only small, but 013,01 d an enormous wealth of powerful nutriment. YTI,ØS not do better than eat plenty of Currants, at .".010`, of the year, because the fruit is in every caY 'eh-A bet° te health. The Currant contains grape-sugar, of all foods. A liberal diet of Curranta—ira;ror. cooked—is always the cause of good health aLlu.</p>
        <p>VINES IN AUSTRALIA. the Australia offers splendid opportunities ferf tbe practical vine grower. In South Australia one o.`e of most successful viticulturists is Mr. E. Maztir-vi'ere Auldana. His returns during the recent vintage of simply remarkable, and provided further evidesce id the wisdom of his methods. An acre of 40101 a trellised Doradillas scaled 10 tons 7 cwt.tr.lle single vine produced 1371 b. of beautiful fruit, the seven-acre plot of two and three-year-old vines n't 50 same variety—three-and-a-half acres of each—oenbors tons ti cwt. 2 qr., and one of the younger vines 421 b. of excellent •berries.</p>
        <p>CEMENTING THE ENTENTE. sdai M. Poincare, French President on Wedije na visited various French institutions' in London' wiss6 subsequently drove to the Guildhall, where be entertained to luncheon by the Lord Mayor. 11°,51-tbat mg to the toast of his health, If. Poincare , to-day the friendship between England and rr°,l3lo became, if possible, strengthened and re-affirnied• co-operation between the two Governments 00 q the tions affecting international politics tended t° two eoill maintenance of peace, and established between tb, countries a fraternal confidence and a con3o3°ll the In the evening the President gave a banquet at French Embassy in honour of the King. The r°0101" were magnificently decorated, and the table aPP 0 ments were specially brought for the occaBio° ft° Paris.</p>
