      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>LAST STRAW. tio'inrirehaloner Dowdall's amusing quota- orn a letter he once received from an icr_ate Liverpudlian voter recalled an 1713-el'ience of my own while on a Yorkshire aneeklY many years ago. Occasionally co„angrY subscriber would appear with a hole' of the current number in his hand, and th,ff, Protest against something therein nadannoyed him. He would tell how and CO taken the paper from the beginning, acu, Conclude by declaring that he would editor have another. Then would the officor,_, with mournful countenance, call the of the Y, and solemnly say in the hearing shat,e too self-important "Put up the %y,Le,re,• we're ruined. This gentleman us - I'l never spend another penny with i(4 we're done for." Then would the hisignant subscriber either laugh outright, OF tiro(' humour restored by the absurdity Ethe 4e situation, or quietly fold the offensive aw:„'' and, like the Arabs, silently steal N'ERAN N, TO. colt'w and again I have taken occasion to feet 111 end this admirable and assuredly per- enc international language, and for the wiin°llragement of young Warringtonians wo:,,lllaY be hesitating as to whether it is itia"'". while to acquire it as an auxiliary, I ay Y Mention an incident which came my 1d Saturday. ni_n Saturday. Travelling from Wake- found in my compartment a young EngLeihman who did not know a word of poiTtish, but who, trusting to the five- ali;,-"‘ed green star in his coat, had ventured withe from Rotterdam to spend a holiday latter a, correspondent in Lancashire. The thnad previously communicated with ofe officials of the Hull group, and one th these had seen the visitor from tlooe boat to the train, with instruc- tiie to change at Normanton, and hi`re for further guidance simply show I:icket to the railway people. When he triai uted at his destination I was still in the Nvn, and had the pleasure of witnessing the tharin welcome he met with from members of eocal group. From the conversation I ar,ered that his name is Pienar, that he has 14,4aaY friend in a certain Fraulino Berta de caving, that his neighbours marvelled at his er,age in thus coming here knowing no teish at all, and that he must forthwith ',g,raPh his safe arrival to his anxious Ivicli"uer• All eager to greet the stranger, ac 0, like the little English company, was eslaintel only with his own tongue and wpemath, they were a lively party, and it %as Pleasant to see how thoroughly at home the visitor seemed at once. "The silent t;hgtish," indeed ! With the spread of Da-veranto the reproach of last week's be-ragraPh under that heading will certainly 14e.„inoved from us. "-SSON IN MANNERS. 0:1nd how very polite our Hollander was. being introduced to the ladies, he No,'„nved his hat and remained uncovered w!e he chatted with them. In•contrast bilm the good manners habitually displayed most Continentals whom I have ar°.untered, I put those of a magistrate "ng in one of the Police Courts which I onsed to attend. He once had before him a °r wretch charged with being drunk and a-deanable, and, not content with inflicting :equate punishment, he added with nitous impertinence, "It must have cost al a lot of money to paint your nose that " i°ll,r." "Alas," sighed the weary prisoner, " nas." Then, suddenly drawing himself ii,with an unexpected access of dignity, he .f,'Ailvered this well-deserved home-thrust: kiln(' if your parents had spent on your ncation anything like as much as I have );flasted on thus disfiguring my countenance, Vtl would have been taught manners and uld have known better than make such a trlark to one in my position." Poor fellow! d the better of the exchange, though. THE MAKE. 411 an East Lancashire town there d,„ a sort of ragged school with a tfigin and fife band. It has long been !'137 Practice to take the latter out ~ ion'„etk, "on Saturdays during the summer teilt,i‘,lls. The superintendent takes care to Vet for the purpose some Lancashire or C)trkshire town where there happens to be attiestival or demonstration calculated to Iva!aet multitudes of visitors. Recently I Iv;° surprised to find that the youngsters ke-:0 going as far as Wakefield. I li 'w they would play and make the iiae,vitable collection at stations where they L0`:,,,t0 change ,• still, I could not understand in view of the travelling expenses, &amp;c., au enterprise could be made to yield a fio;rins. I was destined, however, soon to le: out how it is done. Into my compart- fire-" there entered a solitary juvenile with a joe-',.and immediately upon resumption of the io,-;11eY he began to perform upon this 1)r-4111/lent, and treated us to so creditable a Da,Zramme that an enthusiastic fellow- ell'enger passed the hat round for him and At P.sr,esent gave willingly. That was not all. fiod!'ne next stopping place, the smart lad, to 111,g that none of the others were due left for an adjoining carriage, rein to a fresh audience, he might repeat tulr, experiment with reasonable hope of like b s'eess. No doubt his fellow-members of the aft were, for the most part, busily engaged them the same fashion, having distributed 'ph atselves judiciously throughout the train. tl to to, then, was the explanation. They were, A pput it, working their passage. 1-.4A-0E IN "THE SUN." knonow well, and have for many year, b red s, n those articles in the American " Sun o . to 4 • .1),. in characteristic phraseology by Lite ,dhesterton, and mentioned in the rei 4.1.P.17 Gleanings" a week ago. I have „ablve in New York who regularly sends its i'c/Pies of that amazing publication, with the ielnendous scare-heads. In a corner of ' ey r2nt page the bold assertion strikes thF, ine„ If You see it in the Sun,' it's so. it ha- 8,(1111e of our home papers adopted this, Nvith„°ecome a little stale; but it originated eaine tue New York " Sun," and when first I der os s it I enjoyed it immensely.</p>
        <p>BRITISH LADS FOR AUSTRALIA. ,ttuur.-,,4 kw° years ago the Government of Victoria, tia -4,a, entered into an arrangement with the West iztdrs Guardians to take selected parties of a dozen 44re Per month from that congested district of Britain's to,ePolis. The experiment turned cut a great to tje", and larger parties'of lads were taken until up Viet present about 500 West Ham lads have gone to roorrlrla to take up farm work. The farmers were in Itthao Batified with the boys, although, obviously, see - Rreat majority of cases, none of them had ever werea farm until they arrived in Victoria. The lads loette delighted with their new surroundings and their 108. rto Prospects. They usually started at from and 1,55. per week in wages with their board andu:"Aging, and as they became more experienced 'eloh, their pay rapidly increased to £1 a week.</p>
