      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>"I REMEMBER</head>
        <p>"I REMEMBER</p>
        <p>Election Excitement in the Long Ago.</p>
        <p>SOME QUAINT STORIES.</p>
        <p>A lady who wishes her c, ntribution to remain anonymous sends us the following : Nomination day is not the lively and exciting time it used to be over 60 years ago. I remember' the hustings, for it was put up in the garden at the end of the Bridewell, nearest to the Savings Bank. There was a garden also at the other end in front of the offices. I remember it was there I first saw tulips growing. The head officials of the town lived round about there. I remember two maiden ladies, the Misses Noble, living in the big house .standing behind what we called the back of the Bridewell. I think it is a lodging-house now. At the time crinolines were worn ; the Misses Noble did not wear them, and being tall and slim, to us they looked rather remarkable, but they were very nice ladies. I have often seen men sitting in the stocks at the front of the Bridewell. The first election I remember was between Allcard and Greenall (1847). The party colours were pink and white for Allcard (Liberal), and red and blue for Greenall (Tory). We children would go skipping and singing about with our bits of party-coloured ribbons pinned on us. The songs we sang were not very good as poetry or sentiment, but they suited us immensely. Here is a sample :</p>
        <p>Pink and white is my delight, And no one else can bang it, BefoLe we'll hare Old Greenall in, We'll cheer Old Harry, dash, dung it! The opposite party sang : Red and blue will pull us through, And no one else can bang it, Before we'll have Old Allcard in We'll cheer Old Harry, dash, clang it ! Another sample : Alleard and Greenall were eating two pits, Allcard gave Greecall two bonny black eyes. Alicard said to Greenall, "Do you want any more ? " Oh no ! lir. Allcard, my eyes are too sore. Of course, the opposite party sang with the names reversed. Another election I remember, some good few years after, between Peter Rylands and Gilbert Greenall. Liberals and Tories had their colours put out on props through the windows. At night it was said Greenall had got in. The Liberals quietly drew their props in, but next morning it was said there had been a mistake in the counting and By lands had got in, so the Liberals quickly put their flags out again and made a brave show. I remember at the election of Allcard and Greenall I liked the colours pink and white the best, and I recollect how I gave a girl a ball of my mother's worsted for a bit of pink and white colour. I don't know whether it was conscience or the birch rod that made me remember it so well. Most houses in those days had a corner for the salt-box, bellows, and birch rod. My mother didn't believe in sparing the rod and spoiling the child. She believed in spoiling the rod instead; consequently we often had to have a new one. I remember an incident in connection with -which if I did not get the rod I deserved it. At our house there always seemed to be a baby, and as I was the eldest, I had the burden of them. There was one thing I liked in connection with them and that was going for their soothing syrup to a Mr. Bullocks, druggist, whose shop was by Turner's, locksmith, in Buttermarket-street. Later on he went across the way, and then came into Sankey- street. I thought this soothing syrup was so nice, and I used to taste it. One day I tasted too much, and then I got frightened. It was raining and I went to a spout that was dripping, and let some drops of water into the bottle. A Gruesome Story.</p>
        <p>The old lady from whom I used to fetch the birch rods, kept a shop in Buttermarket- street. The shop is still there by Gandy's, clogger. She was a nice old lady, and she also sold besoms and pan mugs, and she made barm. Most people then made their own bread and would take it to the public bake- houses, situated in different parts of the town. There was one on Town Hill, or Pig Hill. The initials were pricked out on the bread with a fork. Ours were W. W. The mention of bellows reminds me of another incident about which I have heard my mother speak. There was a young man who worked at Cockhedge Factory who was in the habit of going to the Old Coffee House on Saturday night with other young men. One night, after closing time, he hid himself on the back premises, for some unlawful pur- pose, and an elderly woman, who helped at the house, went out to see if all was right, as was her custom. She was found strangled the next morning. A silver snuff box, belong- ing to the old lady, was found in the reservoir at Cockhedge. The murder was traced to this young man. The shock affected his poor mother's brain, and for days she would sit doing nothing but blow the bellows. I believe the young man was gibbeted and his body was taken to the old Workhouse in Church-street. His skin was tanned and made into purses. I heard of one man who had one of these purses and' he was haunted by a big black dog. He was glad to bury the purse to get rid of the dog. Some years ago I asked an old lady, who was likely to know, if it was correct, and she said it was, as she knew the man personally. Thinking about Knutsford-road, reminds me of an incident. It was told to me by an old gentleman, about a market gardener who owned some property in Knutsford-road. His speciality was growing onions. He was called Onion John. He was returning home one night, very late, from some market, dressed in his ordinary working clothes. He had a big sum of money in his pocket. At a very lonely part of the way, he saw two men making for the hen-roost. Unfortu- nately, they saw him, and came for him, and asked him what he was after. He said, "I am for owt I con geet, lads, like yo." They gave him a kick and sent him off. He was glad to escape so easily. Not Many Amusements. I remember, over 50 years ago, we had not very many amusements, but what few we had we thoroughly enjoyed. There was the Scotland-road Theatre and then we used to have penny concerts at the Old Music Hall. I remember a Mr. Warburton and his sister, Miss Warburton, often sang, and very nicely, too. Mr. Warburton would sing "Old simon the Cellarer," which was a favourite with us. The humorous element was supplied by a Mr. Clayton, and I remember the chorus of one of the songs, which the audience always joined in, keeping time with their feet :</p>
        <p>Over the hills and far away, I've got a tidy job to-day; Little work and lots of play, O'er the bills and far away. Rector Quekett's trips were always a gre&amp; (Continued in next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 28, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>WHERE THE KING WILL Si</head>
        <p>WHERE THE KING WILL Si</p>
        <p>HOW A-.':,GREAT FAMILY WAS FOUNDED.</p>
        <p>HALL</p>
        <p>UTH</p>
        <p>The Stanleys easily hold pre-eminence among the old and famous families of England. Descended from one of the fierce marauders who followed William the Conqueror, they pushed their way to the front rank of English nobility, and have held ever since a place—sometimes a very large place—in the story of England. The first known ancestor was Adam of Audleigh, who took his name from Audith- legh in Normandy. This Adam did bravely. at the battle of Hastings, and as a reward for his services in helping to defeat the Saxons, received some large estates when England was divided up among William's hungry followers. Adam of Audleigh had two sons who accompanied him, and these in course had sons—grandsons of the warrior of Hastings —who were shrewd enough to marry into Saxon families whose lands had not been confiscated. Thus early did lands and estates begin to multiply in the hands of the family. The name Stanley comes from Stoneleigh or Stanleigh, a small place in Staffordshire, which means in Anglo-Saxon a stony field. One of the grandsons of Adam de Audleigh married a daughter of Thomas of Stanleigh, and, in honour of his wife, took her maiden name. Thus came the name around which so much lustre was destined to gather as the centuries rolled. SucCessful marriage added to the already large estates of the Stanleys. By one came the estate at Storeton, and by another, that of Hooton. The famous family of Lathom and Knowsley, destined to overshow in splendour the parent stock, sprang from a young brother of the William Stanley who married the heiress of -Hooton. This John Stanley was in the service of Richard 11., but deserted him when his fortunes failed. Isle of Man Given Away. As a reward for the transfer of his services, the new King gave him the Isle of Man, where he and his successors reigned as little monarchs for centuries. To facilitate his communication with the island, he received permission to build a castle at Liverpool, and in the tower, thus built, several generations of the family lived. A further successful alliance of Sir John Stanley in the fifteenth century brought the estate oc. Sir Thomas Lathom,lord of Lathom. There came with this alliance the crest of the Eagle and Child, adopted in the lady's honour, , and still promin3nt as part of the Stanley , emblem.</p>
        <p>it on the head of Henry and proclaimed him king.</p>
        <p>Thomas Stanley was ever the faithful friend of the party of good luck, but never did such reward come for so trifling service or to a man who deserved it less. Lands and estates in the North of England were showeKed upon him, all in token of the bravery shown in crushing a defeated man. Shrewd judgment and good fortune had never yet deserted the Stanley&amp; After Bosworth,</p>
        <p>There is only space to mention the Earls who succeeded the hero of Bosworth, though many long chapters could be written about them.</p>
        <p>Thomas, the second Earl, succeeded to the title in 1504, and handed it to his son William in 1521.</p>
        <p>The fourth Earl, Henry, flourished in the spacious days of Elizabeth, and was com- missioned by her to raise forces in Lancashire to resist the threatened Spanish Invasion. He died in 1593, and his body lies in Ormskirk Church. The fifth Earl was Ferdinando, a poet and author, who died, it is supposed, by poison in 1594. After him came William, a romantic wanderer over the face of the earth. The eighth Earl lived in the days of the Civil War, fought hard for the King,and was beheaded at Bolton in 1651. Charles, his son, succeeded him, and lived through the quieter days of the Restoration, he being "restored to blood "—to quote the wording of the Bill—by an Act of Parliament passed in 1665. His son William, who succeeded him, died without children, and the title passed to a son of Earl Charles. Trained under the Prince of Orange, he saw much fighting in Flanders. Again, the earldom shifted its course, this time falling to Sir Edward Stanley, of Bickerstaffe. _ _</p>
        <p>It was the grandson of Edward, who became 12th Earl, and who, as a lover of the turf, founded the famous " Derby " race in 1780. The 14th Earl became Prime Minister in 1852, and his son succeeded him as an able statesman in the House of Lords. The 16th Earl was also a statesman, and became Governor-General of Canada. In 1908, the present Earl, Edward George Villiers, succeeded to the title. Mentioned in this hasty fashion, the Stanley pedigree suggests only a procession of names, but if told by a literary craftsman, their story would be little less than fascin- ating.</p>
        <p>Three generations later the Earlship of Derby was conferred upon Thomas Stanley, who, after waiting long enough to see who would prove winner at Bosworth Field, joined in the struggle while Richard was making his last stand and helped to crush him. Picking up Richard's battered circle of gold, he placed</p>
        <p>Historic Knowsley and the Romantic Story of the Lucky Stanleys.</p>
        <p>middle of Knowsley Park. This event has some interest for Warrington, and those who like to trace resemblances may find one here. Whilst on his way to be the guest of the Stanley's, Henry Vll.,with his Queen,opened anew bridge over the Mersey at Warrington, and then travelled by road to Knowsley. The Hall was at this time a hunting seat, the home of the Stanleys being at Lathom, Eut it underwent some alterations in prepara- tion for the visit, and still contains apart- ments that are known as " King Henry's Chambers." Many Changes. - - - -</p>
        <p>From that —day until the present the house has undergone many changes. Most of its lordly occupants have left their mark upon it, and not all of them have built wisely. Yet the old place has many visible reminders of notable days of the past when the Stanleys helped to shape the destinies of England. The noble mansion stands in a fine park seven miles from Liverpool, and two from Prescot, surrounded by a landscape that pre- sents every variety of beauty and interest. Our illustration shows the south side of the Hall. High aloft carved in stone is the crest of the " Eagle and Child," and beneath it cut in stone is the inscription</p>
        <p>James,Earl of Derby. Lord of Man and tholeles, grandson of James, Earl of Detby, and Charlotte, whose Husband, James, was beheaded at Bolton ay. Oct. !MOUE for strenuously adhering to Charles tle Second, who refused a Bill passed unanimously by both Houses of Parliament for restoring to the Family the Estate lost by his loyalty to him MDCXXXII. A Dream of Luxury.</p>
        <p>Numerous treasure's adorn the walls and chambers. The library in particular is a dream of luxury. It was once the privilege of the writer to spend the hours of a June afternoon in exploration and admiration (chiefly the latter) of the Knowsley library. Pleasure gives place to awe in the presence of those ancient and gorgeous tomes, the collected treasures of the heads of this famous house. It is said that the gorgeous library was the favourite Knowsley resort of the late King Edward. We do not wonder. Luxurious seats, adapted to every degree of weariness or seriousness, allure the guest ; electric lights, hidden above the bookshelves, shine upwards upon the ceiling, and suffuse the chamber with mild and shadowless light. A true bookworm settled here would regard the mention of meal times as an annoy- ance and impertinence.</p>
        <p>Yet even the library has its grim reminder. In the centre stands the chair used by the 7th Earl of Stanley, the last upon which he sat in the "Man and Scythe" Inn at Bolton before he stepped upon the scaffold to feel the headsman's axe.</p>
        <p>No, life has not always been smiles and roses even for the lucky Stanleys. . - _</p>
        <p>A. M.</p>
