      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>KNOWSLEY HALL.</head>
        <p>KNOWSLEY HALL.</p>
        <p>And now a word about Knowsley. Though very old as a shooting lodge, it has not always been the seat of the family. Only, indeed, since the Civil War has it been the recognised home. It had, however, the honour of sheltering Royalty in days when it was only regarded as a shooting lodge in the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>LITERARY GLEANINGS.</head>
        <p>LITERARY GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>BEFORE CONSCRIPTION.</p>
        <p>"It is commonly repeated that the voluntary principle has had a full trial. If by that we mean that all alternatives to com- pulsion have been exhausted, it simply is not true. The Territorials have never ben paid for the time they give to their training, nor even indemnified for the sacrifices that they make. And until at least this much has been done, we cannot pretend that the alter- natives to compulsion have been exhausted. Everything seems to point to the fact that we could get all the men we could possibly need by the simple process of paying for them."—Norman Angell, in the "Pall Mall Magazine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>NEW USE FOR BATTLESHIPS.</head>
        <p>NEW USE FOR BATTLESHIPS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Goodwin, of Arkansas, has introduced a Bill of a novel kind into the American Parliament. It stipulates that every United States warship sailing for foreign ports should be loaded with samples of American wares—automobiles from Detroit, men's clothing from New York, canned beef from Chicago, and so forth.</p>
        <p>It is- Proposed that the Secretary for the Navy should co-operate with the Secretary for Commerce to allot space on battleships to manufacturers, merchants, exporters, or trade bodies, who shall be permitted to install and maintain the exhibits. "I am opposed to the senseless multiplication of battleships," said Mr. Goodwin, "yet I see no reason why those we have built should not be used in advancing American trade before they rot and become junk. From the latest available reports, our navy, it appears, spends much time cruising to foreign ports, yet the trade reports show how comparatively unknown are American goods in the ports frequented by American warships."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>LIBERALISM AND TORYISM.</head>
        <p>LIBERALISM AND TORYISM.</p>
        <p>IS THE STATE MADE FOR THE MAN OR THE MAN FOR THE STATE?</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Lyon Blease has written a very able "History of English Liberalism," published by Messrs. Fisher Unwin at 10s. 6d. net. Perhaps our readers will enjoy reading his definitions of Liberalism and Toryism. They are definitions which the staunchest Tory could hardly quarrel with. " By Liberalism," he says," 1 mean not a</p>
        <p>policy,but a habit of mind. It is the disposition of the man who looks upon each of his fellows as of equal worth with himself. He does not assume that all men and women are of equal capacity, or equally entitled to offices and privileges. But he is always inclined to leave and to give them equal opportunity with himself for self-expression and for self- improvement. He assumes as the basis of his activity that he has no right to interfere with any other person's attempts to employ his natural powers in what he conceives to be the best way. . . . . The Liberal habit of mind has its positive as well as its nega- tive side. Just a 3 it leads its possessor to refrain from interfering with the develop- ment of others, so it leads him to take active steps to remove the artificial barriers which impede that development.</p>
        <p>The Tory looks down from the institution to the man, the Liberal up from the man to the institution. To the Liberal, the State and all other institutions within it are things of flesh and blood, they are so many expres- sions of human society, associations of human beings for their own human purposes. To the Tory, the institution is a machine, its efficient working is everything, and it is the duty of the individual to subordinate him- self to that object whether his own interest is served by it or not. The Liberal says, 'The State is made for man, and not man for the State.' The Tory reverses the dogma, and even when he pursues the good of individuals, he pursues it rather in order to make them better soldiers or workers, that is to say, better servants of the State, than to make them better in them- selves. Democratic government to the Liberal is an essential condition to the free gr owth of the individual soul. To the Tory, if he believes in it at all, it is a piece of efficient political machinery. What use can the State make of this man?' asks the Tory. 'What use can this man make of himself ? ' asks the Liberal. The Tory theory is expressed in terms of duties, the Liberal in terms of rights. The disposing mind is at the back of the one, the encouraging mind at the back of the other. The Tory finds the good of the individual in the strength of the State. The Liberal finds the strength of the State in the good of the individual."</p>
        <p>DON'T LOOK. OLD—Keep the ravages of time at bay by darkening the grey streaks with LOCKYER'S SULPHUR HAIR RE STOREft,which deepens to a naturalcolourin afew days.Lockyer's maintai— n.,Thearance.Large bots everywhere. rost tree 1 lonclon,S.l3</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <p>event then, and a bugler would come round very early in the morning to wake us up (those who wanted waking). We would go either to Rhyl, Conway, or Bangor. I remem- ber Mackie's lectures, at the Old Music Hall, to working people. We always commenced our proceedings by singing a hymn to the tune of " Old Lang Syne," and finished with the "Old Hundredth." One of the titles of his lectures was "Devils and Dawdles." I think these lectures must have had a good influence on the people. They were full of good, sound, practical common sense, and some of the lessons I remember to this day. Water for Wine.</p>
        <p>I have just been reading the memorie in I to-day's "Examiner," and the mention of I the Tower reminds me of when they were taking it down. The Rector (Mr. Quekett) was conducting 'a .- party of us round the Church, and I remember him pointing out the lower part of the tower and the foundation, and it was built of raddle and daub. He said it was a wonder some one hadn't been killed. He also showed us the underground passage to Friars' Green from the Church. The mention of Church-street Fair remind me of an old gentleman I knew, who had, in his younger days, been a butler in a gentleman's family. He had a very small stall just by the old Workhouse and sold toffee and wine. The wine, I think, was coloured water sweetened, and we could get a fairly large piece of toffee (which was called Swaggering Dick), and a small glass of wine for a halfpenny. I have watched him making this Swaggering Dick at his home. It was made of sugar and treacle, and' when it was poured, before it set, I have seen him get a part of it and put it on a nail in his kitchen and pull it with both hands, and as he pulled it, it would go lighter in colour, then he would twist it round the other part. He was a gentleman in the true sense, always so kindly and courteous. At the latter part of his life he used to go to help an old gentleman, who kept a public-house in Buttermarket-street, to brew his own beer. He also looked after his boat for him, which he kept on the island. Most publicans brewed their own beer in those days, and he was a nice old gentleman that kept the public-house. I have heard that when the men went in for beer and bread and cheese he always supplied them with a glass of home-made pickles for them to help themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>iCHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</head>
        <p>iCHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>STORIES OF HENRY WARD BEECHER.</p>
        <p>A hundred years ago last Tuesday Henry Ward Beecher, one of the greatest preachers who ever lived, was born in America. Henry was as interesting in his boyhood as he was when a man, and with the editor's permis- sion I am taking a little extra space this week to tell you some good stories about him. He was always courageous and boisterous, and when at school he gained a reputation for wit and mischief. The stories lam copy- ing here are taken from his biography and they all belong to his school and college days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>LESSON IN GRAMMAR.</head>
        <p>A LESSON IN GRAMMAR.</p>
        <p>On one occasion, when preparing for a public examination and it was essential that all the scholars should be perfect, the teacher took Henry aside into a private apartment to put into him those definitions and dis- tinctions on which the honour of the clasg depended. "Now, Henry, `a ' is the indefinite article, you see, and must be used only with a singular noun. You can say 'a man,' • but you can't say 'a men 'can you ?" "Yes, I can say 'amen,' too." was the ready rejoinder. "Father says it always at the end of his prayers." "Come, Henry, now, don't be joking! Now decline 'he.' Nominative, he ; possessive, his ; objective, him. You see ' his ' is possessive. Now you can Isay his book,' but you can't say 'him book.' "Yes, Ido say 'hymn-book,'" said the impracticable scholar, with a</p>
        <p>quizzical twinkle. Each one of these sallies made his young teacher laugh, which was the victory he wanted. "But now, Henry, seriously, just attend to the active and passive voice. Now, 'I strike' is active, you see, because if you strike you do some- thing. But 'I am struck,' is passive, because if you are struck you don't do anything, do you ?" "Yes, I do—l strike back again." This is how he himself tells a tale of his youth :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>"FOLLOW MY LEADER."</head>
        <p>"FOLLOW MY LEADER."</p>
        <p>One sport engaged our youthful leisure. It was called," Follow my leader." It was considered as a testimony to ones courage when by acclamation he was elected to leadership. The game was simple ; but the results, always amusing, were sometimes somewhat too stimulating for pleasure. The leader started upon a run with a long trail of boys in a line behind him, whom he endeavoured to throw off by doing things which they were not strong enough or skil- ful or daring enough to imitate. If twenty boys started, half would drop away after a sharp run for mere want of breath; another section could be thrown off by some feat that terrified them. We recall, he writes, " one memorable chase. Called to the head of the column, I plunged down Margaret's-lane up Prince-street, and back up toward Copp's Hill, reducing my followers by sheer exhaus- tion by one-half. A brick house was going up ; into it I dashed ,ran up the ladder,walked along the floor-joists, and let myself down by a rope attached to a guy on the front. Only six or seven could follow. A large mortar- bed lay near by. I dashed into that, wading through the slush. Five came out on the other side with me. Tough five. They followed me into a shop right back into the adjacent parlour,lout at a side door, through some of the last got the yard-stick well laid on by the indignant shopkeeper, and the last one out came dripping from a pail of water which a woman flung after the nasty varmints,' as she styled us. Many other feats did we, but in vain. The five would stick. I remembered that a large part of Copp's Hill had been dug down, leaving a precipitous face—well, say 50ft. high to the eyes,but, if measured, perhaps 20ft. Ascend- ing the hill, I drew near the verge little hesitant to venture the plunge. But to confess that I dare not do ' anything ' would be disgraceful, and so, with but a moment's pause, I jumped for a little crumbling foothold half-way down, and off from that, as soon as on it, to the bottom, which I reached in a heap, with dirt and stones and two boys following after! Not stopping to rub my shins, rejoicing that only two were left, and desperate, I took my way to the near wharf, where Billy Gray's ships used to be, climbed the side, ran along the deck, up the bowsprit, far out, and then with a spring, off into the deep water. Down, down, down we went, and seemed likely to go on for ever. At . length the descent stopped, an we came again to the surface. Oh joy ! to see the two boys standing on the bowsprit. They did not dare! That day's work established my reputation. We know how Alexander felt. CcTsar and Napoleon can tell us nothing new about the glories of victory."_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>BEECHER AND THE TUTOR.</head>
        <p>BEECHER AND THE TUTOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Beecher, on one occasion, was informed that the head tutor of the class was about to make him a grave exhortatory visit. The tutor was almost seven feet high and as solemn as an Alpine forest. But Mr. Beecher knew that, like most solemn Yankees, he was at heart a deplorable wag, with depths of unrenewed chuckle hid away in the depths of his heart. When apprised of his approach, he suddenly, whisked away into his closet the chairs of his room, leaving only a low one which had been sawed off at the second joint, so that it stood about a foot from the floor. Then he crawled through a hole in his study table, which he had made after a peculiar plan of his own, and, seated meekly among his books, awaited the visit. A grave rap is heard. "Come in." Far up in the air the solemn, dark face appears. Beecher rose ingeniously and offered to come out. "No, never mind," said the visitor ; " I just came to have a little conversation with you ; don't move." "Oh !" said Beecher innocently; "pray sit down, sir," indicating the only chair. The tutor looked apprehensively, but began the process of sitting down. He went down, down, down, but still no solid ground being gained, straightened himself up and looked uneasy. "I don't know, but that chair is too low for you; do let me get you another," said Beecher meekly. "Oh, no, my young friend, don't rise, don't trouble yourself; it is perfectly agreeable to me, in fact, I like a low seat." And with these words the tall man doubled up like a jack- knife and was seen sitting with his grave face between his.knees like a grasshopper drawn up for a spring. He heaved a deep sigh, and his eyes met the eyes of Mr. Beecher; the hidden spark of native depravity within him was exploded by one glance at those merry eyes, and he burst into a loud roar of merriment which the two continued for some time, greatly to the amusement of the boys who were watching to hear how Beecher would come out with his lecture. The chair was known thereafter as the "Tutor's Delight."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>MORE LAND FOR SETTLERS 1N NEW ZEALAND.</head>
        <p>MORE LAND FOR SETTLERS 1N NEW ZEALAND.</p>
        <p>Two North Island Estates.—With a view to their sub-division for closer settlement, the Government has purchased two more estates in the North Island. An area of 5,000 acres, known as the Otauiti Block, at Tauranga, has been acquired, and an area of 9,000 acres near Masterton has also been bought. The land comprised is suitable for dairying and general farming. It will be cut up into areas of from 100 acres to 350 acres.</p>
      </div>
