        <p>THE SPIDER AS BUILDER.</p>
        <p>1.1111...111</p>
        <p>:RANDOM READING&amp;</p>
        <p>There is no more industrious or ingeniottii builder in the world than the spider, whose Web furnishes an example of the highest skill, from art engineering point of view, of the same typo of engineering that is displayed in skyscrapers and bridges. To appreciate what this common object, the spider web, means to its builder, one must take the builder's point of view. Mr. J. E. Murphy, writing in Popular Mechanics, takes as an illustration the webs of the orb weaver, the common variety of spider of the temperate zone of North America. The orb weavers, he says, observed in gather- ing the data averaged slightly less than three- quarters of an inch from front to back claws, and had therefore about 1-112th the reach of the aver- age man. While there are minor differences in the construction of webs, duo to local conditions or the preferences of the individual builder, the general form is the same in all. At the centre of the snare is a space filled with material that to us is a fluffy mass of fine lines, but to the builder a network of large rope, with an outside diameter of about 10ft. Outside of this is a com- paratively open space in which only the radial lines of the snare are found. Beyond the open space is A SPIRAL STRUCITRE</p>
        <p>which serves as a scaffolding for the true snare. To the builder the spiral structure has a dia- meter of about 140 ft. to 150 ft. In building the web, the outer or foundation lines are first erected. Usually a number of guy lines are re- quired for holding the foundation lines to the right position. Sometimes theese guy lines are incorporated into the snare as radial lines, but more often they are cut away at a later stage of construction. .</p>
        <p>With the foundation line; all in place, the centre of the snare, from which lines radiate in all directions, is first established. A vertical line is dropped from the top of the structure to the centre, and continued downward to a connection with the lower foundation line. Lines are then thrown out from the centre to the right and left alternately. Care is observed to keep the structure well balanced during construction. There is no uniform angular spacing between tho radial lines, nor is any exact order observed in placing these lines. But at every stage of the process a balance in stresses is maintained, and, in contrast with the situation met with in the erection of some steel structures, there is never a time when there is</p>
        <p>5„,„1„..,</p>
        <p>, . • -• . . • •, . • .N • • • SPECIALLY CHEAP FARES. COMFORTABLE STEAMERS. - - - 'Li a street London, S.W. •:,•• • • • • • ••••=4:: • • • • •::... -•• • • •</p>
        <p>it's Mason's</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>HAIR DESTROYER</head>
        <p>HAIR DESTROYER</p>
        <p>[James' Depilatory</p>
        <p>Instantly removes Superfluous Hairs from the face, Neck, or Arms, without injury to the skin. Of most chemists; r, free from observation, post free on receipt of Postal eider for is. 3d., 2s. 9d., or 51.—Mrs. N. JAMES, 268, Caledonian Road, London. N.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>DANGER OF COLLAPSE</head>
        <p>DANGER OF COLLAPSE</p>
        <p>from eccentric loading or the overloading of weak member.</p>
        <p>With the completion of the radial lines the structure is ready for the spiral scaffolding. In placing this, a line is started on the vertical extending upward from the centre and is carried around repeatedly, spreading outward in spiral form to the outer limit of the snare, this lino being permanently attached to each radial lino as it is crossed.</p>
        <p>• This completes the real structure of the snare. The spaces between the spiral lines are after- wards filled with the fine, beaded, viscid lines that serve to entangle and hold the hapless prey. The spider is able to build this complex struc- ture single-handed and without the aid of mechanical accessories because of 'just two natu- ral faculties. One is his ability to drop vertically with a line as he spins it, and the other is his skill in climbing in any direeticn,</p>
        <p>SPINNING A LINE AS HE GOES and holding it out with one claw so that it will not foul with the lino he is climbing on. The fresh-spun line is very elastic. It is often stretched to three or four times its natural length and then snapped into place after the builder has climbed around 602/le corner in his structure. In this process he shows himself to be an excellent judge of distance, knowing just when to quit spinning and to begin stretching the line.</p>
        <p>An elaborate network of steel cables, 336 ft. in height, erected in place by one man without the assistance of hoisting engine, block and tackle, or other mechanical accessories, would be a mar- vel in the construction field, and would be writ- ten up in the greatest detail in the technical papers. Yet the equivalent of'. this process is going on unnoticed in the ankdes of our back porches and in the trees and shrubbery, every day during the autumn.</p>
      </div>
