        <p>SIR W. LEVER'S ART PURCHASES.</p>
        <p>It is stated (says the "Morning Post ") that Sir W. H. Lever has acquired through Messrs. Gooden and Fox a number of notable pictures which were in the McCulloch collection sold at Christie's last month. The most important is Millais's "Sir Isumbras at the Ford." It was shown in the Academy of 1857. The picture was severely criticised by Ruskin, and the artist commented: "He did not call it a failure, but a fiasco." Millais confessed that in a passion, " I kicked it over, and the hole is still there." At the Gresham sale in 1887 the picture passed to Mr. Benson for 1,300 guineas, and was afterwards pur- chased by Mr. McCulloch for £lO,OOO. At the McCulloch sale the bidding began at 1,000 guineas, and ultimately Messrs. Gooden and Fox obtained the picture for 7,800 guineas. Through the same firm Sir W. H. Lever has secured Lord Leighton's " Daphnephoria, " which at the McCulloch sale realised 2,500 guineas, and. the "Garden of the Hesperides," which made at the same sale 2,500 guineas, also Sir Luke Fildes's "An Alfresco Toilet," which fetched 1,500 guineas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>HARD LINES.</head>
        <p>HARD LINES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Lose after Exciting Finish.</p>
        <p>J. BRADBURN'S HALF-CENTURY.</p>
        <p>The large muster of spectators at the match between Warrington and Cheetham on Saturday saw one of those rare sporting finishes which delight the heart of the true enthusiast. All were delighted with the plucky fight of the home eleven, and fully agreed that they experienced hard lines in not just pulling off the victory. The visitors, thanks to several outstanding semis, put together the formidabte total of 149, and their innings was so prolonged that the home eleven were confronted with a tough proposition if they intended even to save the match. The Warringtonians were twenty-one in arrears when Hannam their eighth man came to the wicket. Bowling is the department in which the home professional excells, but on Saturday he showed himself quite capable of rising to the occasion with the bat, and compiled runs so rapidly that his side were hopeful that he would pull the game out of the fire. lb did all the scoring, his first partner, A. Duckworth, being b)wled for a blank, while W. Bennett, who took his place, did not get a chance of making a single hit. At eighteen, Blight disturbed Hannam's middle stump and Warringtun had lost by three runs.</p>
        <p>Cheetham made the most of their opportunities in the early stages and the acme mountal in alarming fashio:,. F. J. Smethurst gave an exhilarativg dis- plpy- and had put together 58 when Bradburn caught him. Milsom had a long stay at the wicket, defying changes of bowling and carrying out his bat with 31 to his credit. As to the bowling, S. L. Partridge came cut with the highest capture cf wickets, takirg four for 37 runs. Hannam secured three for 46 runs.</p>
        <p>The prospects for Warringtm were not at all encouraging until the partnership of W. Richardson and J. Bradburn. Both players hit out merrily and the spirits of tbe Warrington supporters rose accord- ingly. When he had knocked up 39, however, the captain made a tremendous stroke, but was neatly caught in the courtry by Tyler. J. Bradburn continued his vigorous di-play and his half century was hoisted amid cheers. Singularly enough, this was to be his limit, for a few moments' later he was beautifully caught by Bailey. S. L. Partridge showed a return of the brilliant.form of a week or two ago and made 21 by clever cricket.</p>
        <p>To-day Hannan), the Warrington professional, takes a well-deserved benefit St Helens Recs. are the visitors, and there should be a large crowd to show their appreciation of his consistently good bowling.</p>
        <p>FIRST TEANI AVERAGES.</p>
        <p>BATTING.</p>
        <p>Not Aver- Inns. Out. Out. Runs. age. S. L. Partridge 5 4 1 128 32'0 J. Bradburn W. Richardson T. Wright, J. Callon. 0, Woodcock Hannifin Rev. H. Gray 3 3 0 8 2 6 J. W. Pearson A. Duckworth W. Bennett S. &amp;otter</p>
        <p>it WITH THE WOODS.</p>
        <p>Cheshire Beat Lancashire in Inter- County Championship.</p>
        <p>LOCAL COMPETITIONS.</p>
        <p>Final for the "Peter Peacock" Challenge Cup To-Day. v--- on There was another capital day for bowling Saturday, and local trundlers turned out in full fore; The vat ious greens in the town were exceedinglY We 6 patronised, and headway was made in the compe in Warrington. tition</p>
        <p>34 Cheshire were triumphant over Lancashire by at points on the aggregate totals, and in the gaerad Lostock three of the four Warrington bowlers plar ° for the County won their games.</p>
        <p>-- The first games in the semi-final of the Consolation Competition promoted by the Warrington and District As 3( ciation were played, Wbitecross beating Crrjg, field's No. 1, and Penketh disro.-,ing of the Rylau club by 90 points.</p>
        <p>v ter The final games in the third round of the A° Peacock " Challenge Cup Competition, promoted 11 the Liberal Association, were played on the green a` Bank Quay, and proved very exciting.</p>
        <p>--- The opening games in the third round of the gr°r.k.,:, Competition were decided, and some narrow wl"" were recorded.</p>
        <p>--- The c mcluding games in the second round of throe "Harold Smith Challenge Cup Competition played, and proved bigh'y interesting.</p>
        <p>The following is the record of the day's play :.--</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>BOWLING ANALYSES.</head>
        <p>BOWLING ANALYSES.</p>
        <p>0. M. R. W. Kge. Rev. 11, Gray Hanuam B2 24 208 22 9.15 S. L. Partridge _ _ _</p>
        <p>B. Si!cock - lO 0 42 3 140 W. Bennett</p>
        <p>Warrington 2nd put up a good show against the Oheetham second string, but had to be content with a draw. The home tActal compiled 177 for the loss of eight wickets, A. Evans heading the score sheet with 39. The Warringtonians responded with 160, with five wickets to fall, the best innings being that of J. S. Broome, who rattled up 47. J. W. Pearson was also in good form for his 31.</p>
        <p>Rylands are making a bold bid for the champion- ship of the Warrington and District League, and it will require a great improvement of form on the part of their rivals to dislodge them from the top position. On Saturday, they met the Conservatives and, although the game was keen, the wiremen triumphed by seven runs, McGowan and Waywell were, as usual, the destructive agents, but there were no notable batting performances on either side.</p>
        <p>The " Divex " eleven again collapsed in surprisingly easy fashion against Sankey, their corn- biced efforts producing the miserable total of eight. Sankey amassed 49, the principal contributor being Brindle with 15.</p>
        <p>Cairo-street Unitarians inflicted defeat on Orford by the comfortable margin of 22. S. Hayes and R.Craddock batted %%ell for their respective sides, each placing 13 to his credit. Plinston and Fletcher b)wled effectively for the victors.</p>
        <p>The following is the League table : P. W. L. D. Pts. Rylands 6 ... 6 ... 0 ... 0 ... 12 Conservatives . 5 ... 3 ... 1 ... 1 ... 7 Cairo-street Unitarians 4 ... 2 ... 1 ... 1 ... 5 Sankey Orford " Divex" 4 ... 0 ... 4 ... 0 ... 0</p>
        <p>Workingmen's Mission are giving a good account of themselves in the Warrington Junior League. On Saturday they met Bold-street and registered a success by 37 runs to 14. S. Lloyd captured six wickets for six runs and J McGarr three for eight runs. E. Spruce batted well for 20,</p>
        <p>Latchford were easily triumphant over Walton Juniors in a match which was chiefly notable for small scores. W. Wilkinson, who knocked up 17 for the winners, was the only player to reach double figures. Egerton and Moores shared the bowling for Latchford, and Bar low did most of the wicket-taking for Walton.</p>
        <p>There was quite an interesting batch of friendly encounters. Cairo-street Unitarians 2nd and Latchford were so easily matched and, appal ently, such poor batsmen, that they tied with 12 each.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Wesleyan had to admit defeat at the hands of Fairfield, who piled on 47 against their 29. F. Day, who made 18 for the winners, was the most successful batsman. C. Swinton (14) did good service for the Wesleyans.</p>
        <p>The most complete debilcle of the day was that in which Walton Hall eleven figured as victims. Moore and,Daresbury were the conquerors, and, chiefly to the deadly bowling of de Prez, they got 811 the Walton men out for 13. Then they proceeded to make light of 'he Walton bowlers' teasels, and ran up the nice little total of 107. H. Bailin (30) and Wilkinson (20) wore top scorers.</p>
        <p>Holy Trinity found Rylands' 2nd too bard a nut to crack and were beaten by 7 runs. 0- Williams and T. Williams shone in the bowling department, taking five wickets for eight runs, and four wickets for nine runs respectively.</p>
        <p>Lymm visited Broadheath and found superior foe- men in the Linotype eleven, who secured a margin of seven runs. The batting on both sides was poor, the only players to reach double figures being T. R. Drake (12 not out for the winners) and S. Hinde (11 for Lymm).</p>
        <p>Time spoiled what promised to be an interesting finish to the match at Win wick between the County Asylum and Leigh Wesley Guild. The home team bcored 124, and the visitors were 101 for eight. One of their men was seized with illness when he bad scored 16, and had to retire, so that it left only one wicket to fall, and 18 runs required. The visiting captain showed a sporting spirit and wanted to play to a finish, but the home men had to go on duty, so the match stood as a draw. The chief scorers for Winwick were : Dawson (35), Dr. Barnes (2 and 3 wickets for 14 runs), Cunningham (21) and Wood (16). For tbe visitors the best efforts were those of T. Leather (36 not out), H. Ward (21) and J. W. Ward (16 retired ill). E. Scott took five wickets for 31 inns, and J Hurst four for 32 runs.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES. Warrington v. St. Helens Recs. (Hannam's Bsnefl ). St. Helens Recs. 2n4 v. Warrington 2nd.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Conservatives v." Divex. " Sankey v. Orford. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE. Bold-street v. Padgate Wesleyans. Workingmen's Mission v. Latchford Baptists.</p>
      </div>
