      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COUNTY ASSOCIA-</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COUNTY ASSOCIA-</p>
        <p>TION DRAW.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Lancashire County Bowling Association, held at the Griffin Hotel, Lower Brough- ton, the draw for to-day's play in connection with the Manchester section of the Individual Merit Champion- ship was made. The following are the games in which Warrington players will be engaged : A Siddall, Whitecrossv_W Mayoh, _ Man and Scythe. _ _ _</p>
        <p>J Slavin, WLitecross v H C COx, 'Preswich J NV Jolley, Bull's Head, Warrington • R Berry, Red L P. E D Hillary, Chapel Honse v W Jones, Whitecross. A Weave, Whitecross v F Riley, Withins. J Grundy, Leigh Railway v A Thornley, Whitecross. A Hovell, Height v T Bolton, Warrington Cemetery.</p>
        <p>Friendly Matches</p>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH CLUB v. C.E.M. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Tbis match was played at the Rectory, and resulted in a narrow win for the home team by six points. Scores:— Parish Church. R W Antwis (capt.) ... 75 J George ... ... 5 G. Tilling ... 15 T J Hopkins ... 3 Carman... ... ... 15 P C Price ... ... 14 A Yates ... ... 15 3 Arnell ... 9 T Watson ... ... 73 R Squire (capt) 15 E Baston 6 Wm. Dennett ... 15 A Gresty... 11 R G Brandreth ... 15 H James ... ... 5 W J Hopkins... ... 15 F Issitt ... 15 S Brirnelow 5 T Wilkinson ... ... 15 3 Gresty 8 3 Burrows ... 10 3 Hill ...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, June 28, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 28, 1913.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. R. A. NAYLOR'S BOWLING</p>
        <p>PICNIC.</p>
        <p>The annual bowling picnic from R. A. Naylor Ltd. was held on Saturday at Stamford Arms Hotel, Bolling- ton. After a pleasant drive through Statham and Lymm, Bollington was reached about 2.95 p.m., and bowling was at once commenced. At the close of the third round a knife and fork tea was partaken of, for which the hostess Mrs. Goodier deserved great praise. After tea the remaining rounds were played, T. Carter and W. Lewis running into the final. Carter just got home at 11-0. On Wednesday Mr. Shakesbaft presented the prizes in the works dining-room, the principal winners being : 1, T. Carter ; 2, NV. Lewis ; 3, C. Phillips ; 4, M. Owen. Other prize-winners were Wicks,Lloyd, Cawley, Lloyd, Can-ley, R. P. Williams, Stout, Howard, Lunt, Gibbs, Davenport, S. Williams, Howarth, and Smart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>Successful " Strike " of Bowlers.</head>
        <p>Successful " Strike " of Bowlers.</p>
        <p>Sibce the beginning of the bowling season the Village green at Flimby, near Alaryport, has not been used by any member of the village club as a protest against the raising of the subscription from ss. to is The committee have now reduced the subscription to the old figure cn 'condition that the members share the responsibility of the debt of 3:130 on the club ; and the "strike " is now ended.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>NOTES AND COMMENTS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND COMMENTS.</p>
        <p>Cheshire had the best of matters in the inter-county championship match v, ith Lancashire on Saturday, but it was on the match at Losteck that Lancashire had hardly a ghost of a chance. It speaks well for Warrington when one can say that three of the four individual games won for Lancashire were by local players. A. Gleave (Whitecross) had very little to do -in beating T. W. Cowley (Winnington) by eight points, but T. Humphreys (Rylands) and J. Weave had not such an easy time of it, for they only won over W. Harrison (Verdin P.), and N. Grpham (Wharton) IFF 21-19 and 21-20 respectively. G. Ball, jun., the only Warrington player to lose had only a score of 10 against T. B. Burgess, who played an exceedingly good game.</p>
        <p>The finalists in the Warrington and District Association consolation competition will be White- cross or Crosfields versus Penketh, Rylands having given the villagers the game which should have been played to-day on Gorsey-lane Green.</p>
        <p>At Penketh on Saturday Rylands had only a poor team, most nf their regular players being engaged in the Association's annual handicap competition. They came back to Warrington with a 90 point defeat to sleep on, only ore of their players having recorded a a win.</p>
        <p>This was J. Wood, who cnly defeated J. Higham by the odd chalk in 31 ends. The game was a most exciting one, and although Wood got away first and led 9-3 at the 9th end Higham sent some beauties down and peeled with 12 at the 19th end. He had now most of the running, and at the 27th end led 20-16 Wood got the little 'un and scored a double on the next end, which be followed up by three singles.</p>
        <p>F. Miller (Rylands) met J. Foden and the game proved to be one of the best in the match. It was a very keen battle up to the 25th end when Miller was 15-16 in arrears. After Foden got a ,single on the next end Miller tan his woods well and led 20-17 at the 29th end. Fode,n cane in now with two singles and a double to win by one.</p>
        <p>J. Layfield was a gol match for Joe Bowyer (Penketh), for up to the 15th end he had decidedly the best of the situation, having IPd 7-3, 10-4, and 14-9. Bowyer peeled with 14 at the 20th end and after the 25th length he led 18-16. Laytield scored a beautiful double, but after that Bowyer walked home with a double and a single.</p>
        <p>Another good game was. between J. Gleave and Jeff Bowyer, the latter winning by four chalks in 28 ends. After Gleave had led 15-9 at tli 18th end Bowyer played excellent bowls and peeled with 19 at the 24th length, to win 21-17.</p>
        <p>It would be folly to try and prophecy whether Whitecross or Crosfield's will meet Penketh in the final, but we cannot see Crosfield's losing again to-day, and if they do not win on the aggregate, it is a dead cert, that they will greatly reduce the Majority of their opponents.</p>
        <p>Only a matter of 31 points devide the teams, and if all the Crosfield's men follow the example of J. Hill last Saturday, when he beat Barlow 21-8, they will gain the congratulations of the whole of the soap workers—who doubt it.</p>
        <p>Jackson and Hill led off well for the soapery men, the former beating T. Ellison 21-18, and the latter gaining the glorious victory mentioned above. Ellison played hard and kept close to his opponent until the finish. •</p>
        <p>C. Sutcliffe (Crosfield's) bucked up wonderfully against J. Horton, for after being 8-16 in arrears at the Mb end, he kept tile jack and ran to 14-16, but at the 22nd end Horton recorded a double. However, Sutcliffe went away again to lead 19-18 at the 26th end. It was now touch and go and great excitement pre- vailed when the players peeled with 20. Horton got a good single in to win.</p>
        <p>The other games were fairly interesting, one of the best in the last four being between A. Webb and T. Sutcliffe. They peeled at 5, 10, 14, 16, 17, and at the 27th end with 10. This proved to be Sutcliffe's final score, for Webb w ent to game with a couple of singles.</p>
        <p>We can only say to-day " Play up Crnsfield's, and don't be downhearted." The game will undoubtedly be one of the best to be witnessed.</p>
        <p>Forty-one games in the first round of the handicap promoted by the Warringtbn, and District Association were played, and 43 in the second round.</p>
        <p>Joe Gleave (Junior Conservatives) had to concede three points to T. C. Whittaker (King and Queen). but all the same he had little difficulty in winning 15-8 in 11 ends, Whittaker only having two singles on his card.</p>
        <p>P. Lawless (4) (Bull's Head) ought to have won against J. Donoghue (4) (fly-lands), for he led 12-6 at the 10th end. Four doubles were then recorded in favour of the wireworker, but at the 10th end Lawless peeled with 14, only to fail on the next end.</p>
        <p>H. Langley (2), Rylands, had hard times against R. Tomlinson (6) (King and Queen). Langley soon got his score to 11-6, Tomlinson not landir o• one on the; first six ends. However, he had a look in now and peeled with 12 after the 12th length. Langley then got two singles, but Tomlinson went to game with a single and a double on the 15th and 16th ends.</p>
        <p>Other interesting games in the first round were between A. Jolley, 3 (King and Queen) and W. Andrews, 2 (Bull's Head) ; C. Smith, 2 (Cemetery) and 11. Ratcliffe, 3 (Fearnhead) ; J. Melbourne, 3 (Cemetery) and J. W. Monks, 3 (Junior Conservatives) ; J. Finch, 4 (renketh) and J. Isherwood 2, (Bull's Head).</p>
        <p>In the second round some very good aames were witnessed. J. Hankey, 5 (Rvlands) and W. Pritchard, 4 (Crosfield's) leading off with a 15-14 game. Pritchard had matters all his own way at the beginning, but Hankey touched him up from the 10th to the 12th end, when he landed three successive doubles to lead 13-12. He, however, only recorded another chalk to lose by the odd one.</p>
        <p>j. Houghton (2), Whitccross, was too good of a sticker for J. Barker (4), Ryland. Despite the fact that Barker led 10-2 at the sth end, Houghton pegged away and at the 12th end led 12-11. having landed four doubles and two singles. Another double and a single saw_him the victor by four chalks.</p>
        <p>S. Massey (2), Pylands, and Alf Gleave (2), Liberal Club, were a well-matched couple, and their game took 21 ends to complete. They Teeled with Sat the 10th end. Weave then ran to 13, but Massey. pulled up again and the scores were °pee more ( qual at 14. Massey was fortunate in securing the lucky one.</p>
        <p>After some great surprises, R. Tilling (2), Rylands, met S. Oddy (3), Itylands, the latter failing to record a single chalk in nine ends.</p>
        <p>A. Jolley (3), King and Queen, had bard lines in losing to J. W. Monks (3), Junior Conservatives, by the odd chalk, for ite had kept up to his man all the way, and at the 17 end had peeled with 14. Monks got home on the next end with a beautiful single.</p>
        <p>J. Ashton (2), Cemetery, played a capital game</p>
        <p>against A. Houghton (5), Cemetery, and despite the disadvantage in handicap, was ucc e'ss ful in winning by one point after IS exciting ends.</p>
        <p>Some good games should be witnessed to-day, and a good attendance at Gorsey-lane is expected.</p>
        <p>To-day is final. day, in connection with the "Peter Peacock " Challenge Cup Competitifn, and after four games have been played in the fourth round, the semi-final And final will be played. Mr. A. H. Crosfivid will present the cup aLd watches. The handicap will commence on Saturday, July 10th, and the draw will be published ,in the "Examiner " on July 12th.</p>
        <p>The games last Saturday were of a very interesting character, and excitement ran high.</p>
        <p>St. Austin's G (17) are going strong, and on Satur- day they won a magnificent victory over 13ewsey A (25) by .56 points. Not one of the Bewseyites recorded a double figtife score, and the St. Austin' o men won on every game.</p>
        <p>Some good games were witnessed when Orford A (25) beat Town Hall A (23) by nine points. J. Wood gained the be“ victory of the match, for he defeated J. H. Archer, Orford, 13-1 the latter Dever having a look in.</p>
        <p>St. Austin's I) (P) just managed to scrape in the f,)urth round by two paints against Whitecross G (S). The WhitelcroF.s contingent brought up their score at the finish and they had hard lines not to win,</p>
        <p>Fairfield G (33) played well to beat Howley E (30) by 20 points and no one can dispute the fact that they fully deserved their victory.</p>
        <p>St. Austin's ."F' (14) were by far the better team against (irford 1) (31), for when one considers the great difference in tile handicaps, the win of 11 points by St. Austin's men reflects great credit on each individual member of the team.</p>
        <p>Whitecross F (19), had to play hard to triumph over Fairfield H (`2,5), and they were only successful by one point. Fairfield missed it on their fourth, fifth, sixth and eighth men, but in the other four games they won by fair majorities.</p>
        <p>St: John's B (28) won by four points against 'White- cross E (31), winning half of the games played. The scores were very equal all along, but after the sixih man had played Whitecross were leading I.y four points. Theyaost eight chalks on the last two men and St. John's were victorious.</p>
        <p>The general feeling is that the following teams will enter the semi-final to-day: St. Austin's G. White- cross F, St. Austin's F, and Fairfield G. However, the games will b, exceptionally keen ones and the teanis who win will well deserve their victories.</p>
        <p>In, the " Finlayson " cup competition St. Helens Cable No. 1 won a narrow victory over Fletcher's Moulders No. 2 by two points. P. and K. Wheel Shop had all their work cut out to beat the men from Thewlis Griffith and Edelstens by 16 points.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's No. 1 defeated the Warrington Printers by 3 points, after a very e±-citing match. Some good games were won by bath sides, but such a result can hardly be anticipated.</p>
        <p>The County Asylum are doing big things this year, and on Saturday they beat Crosfield's Shipping by 14 points, winning seven of-the games played. They will no doubt be somewhere in the prize list at the finish.</p>
        <p>The games on the Brooklands Hotel Green in con- nection with the " Harold Smith" Cup competition were witnessed by a number ( f spectators. The quality of the bowling was much above the ordinary, and the competition is proving a successful one. .</p>
        <p>The following are the Warrington Winners in the first round cf the Lancashire Merit Competition, played on Saturday at the Griffin Hotel, Lower Brotighton :—J. Allison (Warrington Cemetery) and H. Eyes (Warrington Bull's Head). " THUMB BIAS."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>ANGLING NOTES. , r Warrington Workingmen's Angling Association (N.A. Centre).</head>
        <p>ANGLING NOTES. , r Warrington Workingmen's Angling Association (N.A. Centre).</p>
        <p>I have again to report a week-end that was not all that could be desired by the angler who can only forsake work for one day, and that day being Sunday the train service is not quite as good as that placed at the disposal of the more fortunate individual who can enjoy the sport on Saturday. If my friend" Au Old Journalist" could only be with us some week-end at the Gowy when William is grazing and the anglers attempt to cross his lordship's meadows, he would no doubt he in a position to relate funnier stories than the crab story. Last week-end was almost a blank for the set board, Mr. T. Dixon coming to the rescue with a roach of 7oz. 6drms. HARTFORD.</p>
        <p>The Weaver last week-end did not provide the matdh angler with much practice for the coming season. We heard of a match taking place among about forty mem- bers and only three, however, took fish. The rivers up to the present seem to be far behind canals for coming into form for coarse fish. CHESTER.</p>
        <p>The Dee had a fair number of anglers last week, several staying for the three days, but the results were very poor; out of thirteen anglers who stayed on Satur- day and Sunday only four had scale fish. The finest match tackle was tried for roach, but results were nil. One,or two walked over to the canal, to meet with no better</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>NORTON.</head>
        <p>NORTON.</p>
        <p>This water seemed to come aud go last week, as the following dilry shows : Monday : Off colour ; many baits tried, results very _p___</p>
        <p>&amp;or. - Tuesday : Very good. Could not mind two rods ; fish on teed in all parts of length,three members taking over two hundred roach.</p>
        <p>_ \Ned:lead:ly : A failing off, but one or two fair catches.</p>
        <p>Thursday : Fair, one member takirg thirty roach from noon.</p>
        <p>Friday : Off colour. Could not get them going. Saturday: Very good. Anglers said it was murder, taking them as fast as they laid in. Sunday : Off colour, fish not on feed, best roach taken 7oz. Gdrms., with a great crowd of members present, to try for £l special.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>GO WY.</head>
        <p>THE GO WY.</p>
        <p>The Gowy had a fair amount of locals, but "nothing doing" was the general experience. One or two took up to a dozen eels, but general fishing was quite flat. There are rumours of the natives using nets in this stream. If so, it is the duty of all anglers to find out who they are and report to the secretary at headquarters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>LECTURE ON FRESHWATER FISH.</head>
        <p>LECTURE ON FRESHWATER FISH.</p>
        <p>As announced in these notes, Mr. G. Hamblett gave a very lucid and inteiesting lectur3 on " The easiest way to distinguish the different species of freshwater fish," at headquarters (Gladstone Arms) last week, before a good number of Northern Anglers and others interested in freshwater fish. The General Secretary (Mr. A. Stout) occupied the chair. Mr. Hamblett said there appeared to be a large amount of difference amongst Warrington anglers as to how to distinguish one species itrom another. He had come prepared to show the meeting how simple it was to do so, with a little teaching and study on their part. Previous to giving the distinguish- ing points, Mr. Hamblett gave interesting information with regard to a fish's senses, feeling,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
