        <p>bearing, smell, also on the questions of digestion breeding, and how to distinguish the different sexes and ages by their build and scales, &amp;c. One interesting part was as to how the eels breed. This has puzzled scientists for hundreds of years, and has only within the last few years been found out, thanks to the German scientist, J. Schmidt, who traced them from the rivers to mid-Atlantic, and by a continuous netting found that eels, when in condition, go from the rivers into the ocean, there deposit their eggs, and are no more seen or heard of. When the fish hatch out, they make their way back to the rivers. Then the lecturer went on to classify the different species. He intimated that if any angler caught any species of freshwater fish, of which be had any doubt, he would be at his service at any time, and it would give him pleasure to classify or advise in connection with fresh- water fish. After the lecture a large number of ques- tions were asked, which the lecturer answered.</p>
        <p>PREFERENCE FOR ONTARIO.</p>
        <p>That the majority of British people who go to Canada prefer to start in Ontario, is shown by the information given to the House of Commons by the Minister of the Interior. During the fiscal year in 1912 there arrived in Canada 354,237 people, and over 100,000 of these settled in Ontario. The preponder- ance of British people in the province has had the result of making conditions very much like those of the old country, as one writer remarks :—" ltural Ontario follows the general pattern of rural England. Campbell's farm of about a hundred acres adjoins Walker's farm of similar extent, and then comes Graham's, Smith's and Wicksteed's- ; and across the road is Cartwright's farm and old Bob Purdy's, with other farms to iight and left of diem, stretching half a mile in one direction to a village, and two miles in the other to a town. And behind all these farms are still more farms, bordered by another road, and that road leads also to a cluster of churches and chapels and pretty homes, with streets of shops and a town hall, a post office, a fire station and a market square." The similarity in conditions, and the presence of so many people from their own country. makes life very comfortable for the new-corner in Ontario.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>NOTICE.</head>
        <p>NOTICE.</p>
        <p>Next week will be published a full list of specials or this season, along with dates of matches. A.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>CYCLIST'S NOTE BOOK.</head>
        <p>THE CYCLIST'S NOTE BOOK.</p>
        <p>Tours in Yorkshire That Provide</p>
        <p>Rich scenery.</p>
        <p>CRAG AND TORRENT.</p>
        <p>[BY "SILVER-STREET."]</p>
        <p>If " J. W. S." will get sheets No. 5,6, and 9, of I Bat tholonaew's half-incn maps he and his friends will I find there the whole of the route they want, and it includes the very finest districts in Yorkshire. The nearest way from Warrington to Harrogate is by North-Eastern train from Newton-le-Willows, but the best way is somewhat round about. • All direct ways, via Glo,isop, Oldham, Ecchdale,Burnley, are anathema. Miles of setts, and tram-lines, and the ugliness of cotton mills, bleach works, and woollen factories darken the cyclist's path into the fair county of York, and if they have a week for a cycling holiday I should recommend the following : Through Wigan and limber Bridge to Walton, near Preston. No need to avoid Preston, altogether, for it is a fine town, with two splendid parks, and the Harris Institute contains the finest collection of butterflies and moths I have ever seen outside London. From Walton the route is altogether the cyclists' ideal. Through the Ribble Valley to Whalley (abbey), Clitheroe (castle), Sawley (abbey remains), Gisburn, Long Preston to Settle. Tne long pull of Buckhaw Brow crosses a spur of the Pennines and lands you at the toot of Ingleborough. Ingleton and its waterfalls and glens should on no account be hurried through. The six miles down to the Lune Valley are full of memorials of Charlotte Bronti3, and although Kirkby Lonsdale is a mile out of our way, yet the Devil's Bridge should be crossed, and the view from the churchyard enjoyed. The valley of the Lune is left at Sedbergh, where the next climb begins. It threads the glorious valley of Garsdale, and finishes at the Moorcock Inn, 1,027 feet, where the inquest was held on the victims of the Hawes Junction railway disaster. The worst of Wensleydale is that there is a road down each side of the valley, and when you get to the fork four miles on, jump off the machine, and toss up which side of the river to take. If heads turns up, take the left-hand, for you ought not to miss Hardraw Foss, and at Askrigg the Whitfield Foss and Mill Gill FOES. At Curperby take the hyroact to the other side of the valley, for it leads you rust the magnificent Aysgarth Falls, probably the finest flow of water in all England, not excepting even the Lake District. West Witton Wensley and Leyburn are all pretty, and from Leyburn Shawl can be seen a stretch of 40 miles of country down to York and Melton. From Leyburn the river is crossed again to Middleham. Here is the castle, and plenty ot it left, too, of Warwick. the King maker, which ought to fill almost a morning's programme. Diet finishes the pick of Wensleydale scenery. To reach Wharfedale a long, rough ride or walk up Coverdale (the abbey at Cover- ham is now farm buildings mostly) is endured, and at the top, under the wing of Great Whernside, the \Vest Riding is entered again and a fall of 900 feet in two miles lauds us, either quick or dead, at Kettlewell. Two roads, equally good in surface, again distract us, and after tossing up once name, the river is crossed and the right bank chosen ; right under the mass of K Linsey Crag to Thresiafleld, and, of course, Grassiogton, and Linton, crossing the valley twice, and Burnsall. No river valley in England can show fairer scenes than the next eleven miles to Ilkley. It includes Barden l'ower, the feudal home of the Cliffords, the wonderful river race of the Strid, Bolton Park, where visitors are allowed by the Duke of Devonshire to gather wild flowers (contrast this with regulations at Eaton Hall, Cheshire), and Bolton Abbey, one of the finest of our ruined churches. At Bolton Bridge the other side of the river should be chosen, and a crossing again effected at Ilkley. Another bridge (suspension) at Ben Rhydding gives access again to the north side of the valley through Denton Park, Askwith and Weston, to Otley. From Otley to Harrogate is an easy though plain ride of 11 miles through spacer houses. The easiest way tack is via Bradford, HalifaN, Hebden Bridge, Todmorden. Rochdale, Middleton, Manchester and Eccles, and I hope you like the prcspect. I will give a better, though longer route next. week, and I tiust it won't be too late for your holiday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>CHESHIRE REST HOUSES.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE REST HOUSES.</p>
        <p>Plough Inn, Wbitegate. This delightful little public- house, with only a six days' licence, lies snugly on a by-road just off the main highway from Weaverham to Over. It is the next by-lane to the left after Foxwhist Green, with its splendid grove of beeches. Mrs. Williamson is an ideal ea terer,and as many as twenty can be accommodated, if notice be given. In the garden two apple trees are decorated with splendid bunches of growing mistletoe. The choice walks are through Pettypool and Vale Royal Parks, and if a camera be taken, some difficulty will be found in choosing subjects. This district has been visited more than once by the Warrington Photo- graphic Society.</p>
        <p>UNION OF LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE INSTITUTES.</p>
        <p>The results of the examinations conducted by the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes have just been announced. Including grouped courses, 123,328 papers were requisitioned, compared with 125,203 in 1912, while a total of 103,827 (1912, 107,547) exercises were worked. The percentage of exercises which satisfied the examiners (ordinary examinations) was 79 67 (1912, 8261), the details being as follow : Passed with distinction 6.72 (1912, 4.3), first-.lass 28.93 (1912, 3514), second-class 2667 (1912, 2445), vatisfied the examiners 20-35 1912, 1872), failed 2033 (1912, 17.39). In the grouped course examinations the percentage of candidates who iatisfied the requirements of the examinations was as follows: Technical course, first year, 4542 (1912, 5733); technical course, second year, 44.24 (1912, 48'S) ; commercial course, first year, 66.48 (1912, 651) ; commercial course, second year. 5149 (1912, 614); rural course, first year, 62.89 (1912, 5585); rural course, second year, 7188 (1912, 7602); art course, first year, 45.65 (1912, 4545); art course, second year, 4815 (1912, 4895); domestic course, 89 65 (1912, 67.7),</p>
