      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-06-28_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>GIRDLES AND BELTS.</head>
        <p>GIRDLES AND BELTS.</p>
        <p>Every day some novelty appears in the shape of a new girdle or belt, and an immense amount of energy must be expended by designers in this direction. A girdle that could be easily made at home consists of two bands of narrow heavy corded ribbon arranged to go twice round the waist, with a narrow space between. The two ends reach the knees in front where they are knotted and finished with silk tassels. In the bright futurist ribbon such a belt gives a vivid touch of colour to a sombre dress. Frequently these startlingly bright, boldly patterned ribbons are merely draped round the waist, with one long end gathered to a point and weighted with a tassel hanging in front, the other behind, or they may fall at the sides. Oa many evening bodices the belt is the most striking feature, and slim figures may swathe themselves in velvet, finished in front with a large plaque of gold and jewels.</p>
