        <p>COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRIN GTON.— The HEALTH COMMITTEE INVITE APPLI- CATIONS for the position of SCHOOL NURSE, to act under the supervision of the Medical Officer of Health in the work of Medical Inspection of Children attending the Elementary Schools, and in the perform- ance of such duties as may be allotted. Applicants should be trained nurses, and preference will be given to those who have had special experience in the diseases of children and also in infectious diseases. Salary £BO per year, with uniform. Applications, with copies of three recent testimonials, to be sent in to the undersigned by Thurwdav,loth July. Health Department, G. W. N. JOSEPH, 78, Sankey-street, Medical Officer of Health. Warrington.</p>
        <p>FOCAL BANKS.—WALKING DAY, 4Th t JULY. The BANKS IN WARRINGTON, EARLES- TOWN, and STOCKTON HEATH will be CLOSED as usual on Walking Day, Friday, 4th July, 1913, at 11 o'clock. NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS. The Bank will he open from 10 to 11 o'clock. LYMM, HEATLEY, and PENKETEL The Banks will be open from 9,30 to 1045. KING'S VISIT, 7TH July. The Banks in WARRINGTON and STOCKTON HEATH will be closed on MONDAY, 7th JULY, 1913, at 11 o'clock. LYMNT, HEATLEY, and PENKETH. The Banks will be open from 9.30 to 10.45. COUNTY BOROUGH OF WARRINGTON.— PROCESSION OF SUNDAY SCHOLARS. JULY 4TH, 1913. VISIT OF HIS MAJESTY KING GEORGE V., JULY 7TH, 1913. By virtue of the powers given in that helwilf by the TONVOS Police Clauses Act, 1817 and by Order of the Town Council, I DO HEREBY ORDER AND DIRECII that in order to prevent Accidents on the above occasions the following regulations be adopted, viz. :—On the occasion of the Public Procession of Sunday Scholars on Friday, the 4th July, 1913, no Cart, Carriage, Tram- car Motor Car, Lurry, or other vehicle (excepting vehicles taking part in the procession, and mail carts), and no horse or other animal will be allowed to remain in or travel along or over any of the following stree between the hours of 9 a.m. and 12.30 p.m., viz : Arpley-street, Kendrick-street, Barbauld-street, Legh-street, Bath-street, Museum-street, Bewsey-street, Palmyra-square, Bold-street, Queen-street, Bridge-street, Rolleston-street, Buttermarket-street, Rylands-street, Cairo-street, Sankey-street, Church-street, Scotland-road, Ed geworth-street, Springfield-street, Friars'-gate, Suez-street, Froghall-lane. Tanners-lane, Golborne-street, Winmarleigh-street, Hanover-street, Winwick-street. Horsemarket-street, The above Order shall cease to apply to any of the above-named streets as soon as the procession in its proper order has passed along any such street. On the occasion of the Visit of His Majesty KING GEORGE V., on Monday, the 7th July, 1913, no Cart, Carriage, Tramcar, Motor Car, Lurry, or other Vehicle, and no horse or other animal will be allowed to enter, remain in, or travel along or over any of the following streets between the hours of One p.m. and 4.15 p.m., viz. :—Arpley-road, Bridge-street, Sankey-street, Liverpool- road. All streets leading into the above thoroughfares WILL BE CLOSED. BICYCLES and TRICYCLES are included in the term " vehicle " and must not be RIDDEN through any of the above-named streets during the hours specified. Any person wilfully acting in contravention of the above Order will be liable to a Penalty not exceeding Forty Shillings. Dated this 11th day of June, 1913. GEORGE WILLIAM JOSEPH, Mayor.</p>
